00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.300
Before diving into all of that,
I'd like to highlight a new,

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:10.140
what I think is a very useful
and zero cost resource.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:13.620
This resource is what's
called Non-Sleep Deep Rest

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.750
or NSDR protocol.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.000
I've talked many times before
on the Huberman Lab Podcast

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.334
about Non-Sleep Deep Rest, AKA, NSDR.

00:07:21.334 --> 00:07:23.710
NSDR is sort of an umbrella term

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:26.700
for a variety of different practices,

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.200
so these are behavioral practices

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.540
that allow you to direct
your brain and body

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:34.410
into a particular state,
and most typically,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.210
that state is one of deep relaxation,

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:40.080
but also one in which you
can access your bodily

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.900
and brain systems for teaching yourself

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:46.539
how to relax in real
time and how to recover

00:07:46.539 --> 00:07:49.650
some of the sleep that you
may not be getting at night,

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.230
and to restore levels of dopamine

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:54.210
in particular areas of the
brain that are involved

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.000
in motivation and cognitive
and motor control.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.360
Basically, NSDR is a
wonderfully restorative tool.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.670
This has been demonstrated
many times over now

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.290
through quality peer-reviewed science,

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.000
and there are neurochemical benefits,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.080
and there are physical benefits,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.600
and there are certainly
psychological benefits.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.096
I highly recommend NSDR to anybody

00:08:12.096 --> 00:08:14.670
that has trouble falling asleep

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.080
or that wakes up in
the middle of the night

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.060
and needs to get better
at falling back asleep,

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.280
as well as to anyone out
there that has issues

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.770
with anxiety or
self-regulation of any kind.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:24.630
And if you don't have any of those issues,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.150
it's also immensely beneficial
just as a restorative

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.340
for leaning back into
focused work of any kind.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.690
Now, a number of people have reached out

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.910
about finding NSDR protocols,
and there are a number

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:36.030
of different good ones
out there floating around.

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.712
I've decided to put a
zero-cost NSDR script

00:08:38.712 --> 00:08:41.070
out there on the internet
for people to access,

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.780
so you can find it by going to YouTube

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:46.500
and simply put my last
name, 'Huberman' and 'NSDR'

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.120
into the search function.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:50.850
This is a YouTube channel
that's hosted by Virtusan,

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:52.827
which has a terrific
app that includes NSDR

00:08:52.827 --> 00:08:55.740
and a number of other health
and wellness protocols,

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:56.820
but they've been quite generous

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:00.000
in hosting a 10 minute NSDR, read by me.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.980
This NSDR is distinct from,

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.320
although I should say,
similar to yoga nidra,

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.420
which some of you are familiar with.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:09.150
This NSDR is different than yoga nidra

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.190
in the sense that it
doesn't have intentions.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:12.600
There's no mystical component,

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.790
and I describe a little bit of the science

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:17.460
and why specific components
of the NSDR are included,

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.050
things like long exhale breathing,

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:20.610
I talk about perceptual shifts

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:22.680
and how to move from thinking and planning

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:23.790
to pure sensation.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:25.290
If none of that makes sense right now,

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:28.380
it'll make total sense after
listening to the NSDR script.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.673
Again, it's a 10 minute
NSDR script, read by me.

00:09:31.673 --> 00:09:34.350
You can do this first thing in
the morning when you wake up,

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.330
especially if you didn't
get enough sleep that night,

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.220
and you're feeling a little bit fatigued,

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.260
and you need to lean into
the day with full vigor.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.120
You could do it at any
point during the day

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.470
or if you wake up in
the middle of the night.

00:09:43.470 --> 00:09:46.020
Again, this is a zero-cost
resource for you.

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:47.340
You can find it by going to YouTube,

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.710
put my last name in and NSDR.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:52.500
I encourage you to try
it, and if you like it

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:54.990
or frankly, if you don't,
you can just put that

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:56.910
in the comment section there on YouTube,

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.610
and I've put it there as
a free resource to you,

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:02.808
so that you can benefit
from the research-backed,

00:10:02.808 --> 00:10:05.220
peer-reviewed studies that point to NSDR

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.500
as a very useful practice.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.960
Before we begin, I'd like to
emphasize that this podcast

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.720
is separate from my teaching
and research roles at Stanford.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:14.880
It is, however, part
of my desire and effort

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.800
to bring zero-cost to consumer information

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.810
about science and science-related tools

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.220
to the general public.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:21.270
In keeping with that theme,

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:23.970
I'd like to thank the
sponsors of today's podcast.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.560
Our first sponsor is Levels.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.570
Levels is a program that lets you see

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.010
how different foods affect your health

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.170
by giving you real-time
feedback on your diet

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:33.660
using a continuous glucose monitor.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:34.980
One of the most important factors

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:36.503
in your immediate and long-term health

00:10:36.503 --> 00:10:39.060
is how well you're
managing your blood glucose

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:40.080
or blood sugar.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.910
I started using Levels about a year ago

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.440
as a way to see how different foods

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.420
and different lifestyle factors

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.790
were impacting my blood glucose levels.

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.070
So for instance, it allowed
me to see how different foods

