00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:57.330
Okay, so somewhat surprisingly,

00:31:57.330 --> 00:31:58.710
we're going to set aside cannabis.

00:31:58.710 --> 00:32:01.260
We're going to take what we
know about sativa, indica,

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:02.947
type one, type two, type
three, CBD, et cetera,

00:32:02.947 --> 00:32:05.700
and we're just going to set
that aside for a moment.

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:06.533
Why would we do that?

00:32:06.533 --> 00:32:08.640
Well, we have to ask ourselves,

00:32:08.640 --> 00:32:10.530
why would any of these plants,

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:13.830
why would any of these
compounds, THC, CBD,

00:32:13.830 --> 00:32:15.660
sativa, indica, et cetera,

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:19.332
why would any of that have
any effect on us at all?

00:32:19.332 --> 00:32:21.750
And this discussion
that we're about to have

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:23.820
very much resembles the discussion

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:26.550
that we had on a previous
episode about nicotine,

00:32:26.550 --> 00:32:28.050
because as many of you know,

00:32:28.050 --> 00:32:30.480
nicotine is a commonly used substance.

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:31.440
In fact, if we were to look

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:34.110
at the three most commonly used drugs,

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:35.640
alcohol will be at the top of the list.

00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:38.550
Many billions of people
regularly use alcohol

00:32:38.550 --> 00:32:40.770
or occasionally use alcohol.

00:32:40.770 --> 00:32:44.250
Many billions of people also use nicotine.

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:48.540
It's the second most consumed
drug, so more than 1 billion

00:32:48.540 --> 00:32:51.120
and probably closer to 2
billion people consume nicotine,

00:32:51.120 --> 00:32:53.528
and then the third most consumed drug

00:32:53.528 --> 00:32:56.580
is cannabis in one form or another.

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:58.267
And many of you are probably shouting,

00:32:58.267 --> 00:32:59.850
"What about caffeine?
What about caffeine?"

00:32:59.850 --> 00:33:02.220
Well, in the context of drugs,

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:03.600
and in particular, addictive drugs,

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:06.180
caffeine doesn't quite rise to the list,

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:09.180
but if we were to look at caffeine

00:33:09.180 --> 00:33:10.830
and include it in that list,

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:12.630
caffeine would be above all of those.

00:33:12.630 --> 00:33:14.430
But the most commonly
used drugs are alcohol,

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:19.430
second after that is
nicotine and then cannabis.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:21.900
Nicotine, as some of you may know,

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:23.370
if you listen to the episode on nicotine,

00:33:23.370 --> 00:33:25.320
but even if you didn't,

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:27.720
nicotine comes from the tobacco plant,

00:33:27.720 --> 00:33:29.640
and there are a few other
plants that include nicotine,

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:32.148
and typically it's brought
into the brain and body

00:33:32.148 --> 00:33:34.290
by smoking tobacco, dipping tobacco,

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:37.443
snuffing tobacco or vaping nicotine.

00:33:39.390 --> 00:33:41.010
Nicotine exists in the outside world

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:42.840
in these plants, the tobacco plants,

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:44.670
but the reason it has
an effect on the body

00:33:44.670 --> 00:33:47.700
is that there are so-called
nicotinic receptors in the body.

00:33:47.700 --> 00:33:50.370
Now, those nicotinic receptors
were named after nicotine,

00:33:50.370 --> 00:33:52.934
the compound, but they
existed in the brain and body,

00:33:52.934 --> 00:33:56.010
not because of the existence
of a tobacco plant,

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:59.130
but because there are
other chemicals in the body

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:02.010
that naturally occur, namely acetylcholine

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:04.113
that bind the nicotine receptor.

00:34:05.070 --> 00:34:07.350
Those chemicals, such as acetylcholine,

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:09.748
that bind the nicotinic
receptor in your brain and body

00:34:09.748 --> 00:34:12.883
create an enhanced sense of
focus, et cetera, et cetera.

00:34:12.883 --> 00:34:17.883
But, nicotine from tobacco
binds that same receptor,

00:34:18.631 --> 00:34:20.700
but with much greater affinity,

00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:23.610
and therefore also
creates a state of focus,

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:26.844
but a much greater one than we
can achieve without nicotine.

00:34:26.844 --> 00:34:28.530
So you can see the nicotine episode

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:30.280
if you want to learn more about that.

00:34:31.160 --> 00:34:35.580
In a very similar way, all
of our brains and bodies,

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:38.820
from the time that we are
conceived, believe it or not,

00:34:38.820 --> 00:34:41.417
very shortly after conception,
if we want to be accurate,

00:34:41.417 --> 00:34:44.746
very early conception,
when you were in the womb,

00:34:44.746 --> 00:34:47.070
and still now, if you're
listening to this,

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:51.840
you have what are called
cannabinoid receptors,

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:55.593
because you also have
endogenous cannabinoids.

00:34:55.593 --> 00:34:56.670
What do we mean by that?

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:59.490
You have receptors, which
are like little parking spots

00:34:59.490 --> 00:35:03.360
that are present on cells
in your brain and body,

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:05.730
and what we call a ligand,

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:07.620
which is basically just a
chemical that's released,

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:09.750
parks in that receptor

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:12.753
and causes a number of
different biological effects.

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:17.370
Cannabis contains compounds

00:35:17.370 --> 00:35:19.530
that also bind to those receptors,

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:21.450
but here I want to make a
really clear distinction.

00:35:21.450 --> 00:35:23.700
We have what are called
endogenous ligands,

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:26.280
those just mean chemicals from within us

00:35:26.280 --> 00:35:27.420
that we make naturally,

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:29.400
even if we never go
near the cannabis plant

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:31.170
or any other source of cannabis,

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:34.230
we have chemicals that are created in us

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:38.340
that park in those receptors
and cause biological effects

00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:40.350
on mood, on perception,
on the immune system,

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:41.670
on hunger, et cetera,

00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:44.430
again, without ever going
anywhere near cannabis,

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:47.070
we have these endogenous cannabinoids.

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:49.710
So endogenous cannabinoids
are floating around in us,

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.270
or I should say, they are released in us

00:35:51.270 --> 00:35:53.100
in particular ways,
bind to these receptors

00:35:53.100 --> 00:35:55.800
and cause changes in
mood, appetite, et cetera.

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:57.127
They have many different
effects on the brain and body,

00:35:57.127 --> 00:35:58.803
and we'll talk about those.

00:35:59.700 --> 00:36:01.260
But just like with nicotine,

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:03.330
there are substances in the outside world,

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:06.840
in this case, cannabis
contains these substances,

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:11.840
so things like THC and like
CBD, that when ingested

00:36:12.660 --> 00:36:16.226
by smoking or vaping
or by ingesting edibles

00:36:16.226 --> 00:36:20.190
also will park in those same receptors,

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:24.390
the cannabinoid receptors and
lead to biological effects.

00:36:24.390 --> 00:36:25.620
Now, it's a little bit misleading,

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:27.510
because we call them cannabinoid receptors

00:36:27.510 --> 00:36:31.400
as if they were there to bind cannabis,

00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:33.570
or just like we call
the nicotine receptors,

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:36.930
nicotinic receptors, it makes
it seem as if they were there

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:40.350
in order to bind nicotine from tobacco,

00:36:40.350 --> 00:36:42.600
but that's not the way our
brains and bodies evolved.

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:45.630
Our brains and bodies
evolved for these receptors

00:36:45.630 --> 00:36:48.600
to make use of chemicals
that exist within us,

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:51.510
called again, endogenous chemicals,

00:36:51.510 --> 00:36:54.240
and those endogenous chemicals
lead to certain effects,

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:55.470
as I mentioned before.

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:56.640
The key thing here,

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:58.741
if you haven't understood
anything I've set up until now,

00:36:58.741 --> 00:37:02.370
please understand this, the key thing is

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:06.780
that THC and CBD and the
other components of cannabis

00:37:06.780 --> 00:37:08.460
bind to those receptors,

00:37:08.460 --> 00:37:10.710
those endogenous cannabinoid receptors,

00:37:10.710 --> 00:37:14.970
the ones that we naturally
make, with much greater affinity

00:37:14.970 --> 00:37:18.630
and exert a vastly
greater potency and effect

00:37:18.630 --> 00:37:20.850
on mood and perception, et cetera,

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:23.973
than do our endogenous cannabinoids.

00:37:25.470 --> 00:37:26.790
Another analogy that one could take

00:37:26.790 --> 00:37:28.320
in order to understand this

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:31.830
would be hormones, like
testosterone and estrogen.

00:37:31.830 --> 00:37:35.070
Many people, I would say all people,

00:37:35.070 --> 00:37:37.620
make testosterone and
estrogen to varying degrees.

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:39.330
It's going to depend on whether
or not you're male, female,

00:37:39.330 --> 00:37:40.860
your age, whether or not
you've gone through puberty,

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:42.270
et cetera, et cetera,

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:45.390
but let's just take
testosterone for example.

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:48.990
There's testosterone
circulating in your body.

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:50.820
That's true if you're male or female,

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:53.100
and there are receptors
called androgen receptors,

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:56.100
we could even call them
testosterone receptors.

00:37:56.100 --> 00:37:58.230
And the testosterone
binds to those receptors

00:37:58.230 --> 00:37:59.370
and has effects on cells.

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:01.710
It causes hair growth, changes the voice.

00:38:01.710 --> 00:38:02.970
It can affect libido.

00:38:02.970 --> 00:38:04.680
It affects all sorts of
things in the brain and body,

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:07.770
depending on which organ and
tissue you're talking about.

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:08.880
But of course,

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.100
there are people that take
synthetic testosterone

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:14.040
or derivatives of testosterone,

00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:15.180
and some of those derivatives,

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:16.740
for instance, in the
body building community

00:38:16.740 --> 00:38:17.640
and the sports community,

00:38:17.640 --> 00:38:19.320
they will take things like Dianabol.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:23.190
These are modified
versions of testosterone

00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:26.160
that can bind to the testosterone receptor

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:28.140
with much greater affinity,

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:29.850
or I should say the androgen receptor

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:31.080
with much greater affinity,

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:34.110
and have supra physiological effects,

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:36.930
effects that would
essentially never be seen

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:41.483
from testosterone that was endogenously,

00:38:41.483 --> 00:38:45.660
excuse me, endogenously
released within the body.

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:47.310
We could say the same thing for estrogen.

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:51.003
There are estrogen receptors,
they bind estrogen,

00:38:51.870 --> 00:38:54.300
but if someone were to
take synthetic estrogen

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:55.890
or to ingest a plant compound

00:38:55.890 --> 00:38:57.867
that contains various
estrogenic compounds,

00:38:57.867 --> 00:39:00.720
and those plants
certainly exist out there,

00:39:00.720 --> 00:39:03.270
they can have supra physiological effects

00:39:03.270 --> 00:39:04.830
on those receptors.

00:39:04.830 --> 00:39:06.120
Why am I telling you this?

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:10.080
Well, many people believe
that because cannabis,

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:14.130
marijuana is a plant, and
plants grow out of the ground,

00:39:14.130 --> 00:39:16.140
and they're naturally occurring,

00:39:16.140 --> 00:39:19.380
and because we have receptors in our body

00:39:19.380 --> 00:39:23.070
that are there without
the need to engineer them

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:26.070
from some external source,
they're in our genome.

00:39:26.070 --> 00:39:27.720
They're program for it, and
we're born with these things,

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:30.120
and we keep these our entire life.

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:34.560
Many people mistakenly think,
ah, these plant compounds

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:36.600
are safer for us or better for us,

00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:39.528
or are somehow appropriate
for us to ingest,

00:39:39.528 --> 00:39:42.600
but that's simply not true,
and here, I'm not saying

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:44.760
that cannabis is always
a bad idea for people.

00:39:44.760 --> 00:39:46.500
There are certain populations
and certain people

00:39:46.500 --> 00:39:49.530
for which it can be relatively
safe recreationally,

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:51.510
that's the truth, and
there are other populations

00:39:51.510 --> 00:39:53.490
for which it can be downright dangerous,

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:55.920
recreationally or medicinally.

00:39:55.920 --> 00:39:57.420
And of course, there
are medicinal purposes

00:39:57.420 --> 00:39:59.640
that are being explored, and
we'll talk more about that.

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:01.170
But this is vital to understand,

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:02.527
because I think that when we hear,

00:40:02.527 --> 00:40:04.710
"Oh, it's from a plant, it's natural,"

00:40:04.710 --> 00:40:06.240
and then you also have
a receptor for these,

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:08.070
endogenous cannabinoid receptors,

00:40:08.070 --> 00:40:11.520
and therefore, the marriage of those two,

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:15.390
the coming together of the
chemical THC or CBD or both

00:40:15.390 --> 00:40:18.300
with these receptors is
somehow supposed to happen

00:40:18.300 --> 00:40:21.480
as if this was a purpose
of having these receptors,

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:22.610
but it's simply not the case.

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:24.840
In the same way that
the nicotinic receptors

00:40:24.840 --> 00:40:27.390
are not there because
nicotine is good for us,

00:40:27.390 --> 00:40:28.680
they're there because there are compounds

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:30.960
that exist within us that are good

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:34.200
to bind to those receptors
from time to time.

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:36.270
Now, here's the key thing about,

00:40:36.270 --> 00:40:37.800
I guess today, I'm saying
there are a lot of key things,

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:41.070
but here's another key thing
about understanding cannabis

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:45.780
and the way that it works,
which is that THC and CBD,

00:40:45.780 --> 00:40:47.640
when they're brought
into the brain and body

00:40:47.640 --> 00:40:50.493
by smoking or edible, et cetera,

00:40:52.170 --> 00:40:53.970
they bind to those receptors,

00:40:53.970 --> 00:40:56.370
those endogenous cannabinoid receptors,

00:40:56.370 --> 00:40:58.500
and they tap into the same systems

00:40:58.500 --> 00:41:01.860
that your endogenous
cannabinoids would tap into,

00:41:01.860 --> 00:41:03.420
the ones that affect mood and energy

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:05.910
and creativity and relaxation, et cetera,

00:41:05.910 --> 00:41:10.910
but they do so with thousand
fold greater potency.

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:13.290
And as a consequence of that,

00:41:13.290 --> 00:41:16.860
your endogenous cannabinoids
are outcompeted.

00:41:16.860 --> 00:41:18.630
They really get no opportunity

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:20.760
to interact with those receptors.

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:22.710
And understanding that,

00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:25.620
can lead to a very clear
understanding of why, for instance,

00:41:25.620 --> 00:41:28.800
when people use cannabis
to relieve anxiety,

00:41:28.800 --> 00:41:31.170
or they use cannabis to
enter a certain brain state

00:41:31.170 --> 00:41:33.660
for creativity or to enter sleep,

00:41:33.660 --> 00:41:37.110
why a dependence on
cannabis starts to emerge.

00:41:37.110 --> 00:41:39.570
Because if they don't ingest cannabis,

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:42.770
and again, ingest could
mean smoke to bring THC in,

00:41:42.770 --> 00:41:46.200
or CBD in, or ingest
orally or even transdermal

00:41:46.200 --> 00:41:48.103
or tincture or one of the other varieties,

00:41:48.103 --> 00:41:51.708
if they don't do that,
then what happens is

00:41:51.708 --> 00:41:55.530
not only are the receptors not
stimulated to the same degree

00:41:55.530 --> 00:41:59.100
or with the same potency
that they normally are,

00:41:59.100 --> 00:42:01.200
but the endogenous cannabinoids

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:02.670
can no longer have their effect,

00:42:02.670 --> 00:42:05.190
so people experience
heightened levels of anxiety,

00:42:05.190 --> 00:42:09.900
disrupted mood, disrupted
brain state, and so on.

00:42:09.900 --> 00:42:11.940
Now, again, I want to be very clear

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:14.010
that I'm not trying to
paint a picture of cannabis

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:16.170
as all bad or even partially bad.

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:17.490
What I want to do today is give you

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:21.480
as much information I can
as to how cannabis works,

00:42:21.480 --> 00:42:23.070
how its different component parts work,

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:24.720
how the different types of cannabis work,

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:28.201
and point to some of
the valid medicinal uses

00:42:28.201 --> 00:42:30.240
and some of the recreational uses,

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:32.960
and then lay out the landscape for you

00:42:32.960 --> 00:42:34.761
as to who is really most at risk

00:42:34.761 --> 00:42:38.430
in terms of psychoactive components,

00:42:38.430 --> 00:42:40.680
immune components and so on and so forth,

00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:42.570
so that you can make the
most informed choice for you.

00:42:42.570 --> 00:42:45.607
I am not here to tell you
what to do or what not to do.

00:42:45.607 --> 00:42:48.829
As I like to say, do as you wish,

00:42:48.829 --> 00:42:51.270
I mean, don't do as you wish

00:42:51.270 --> 00:42:52.500
if it harms other people or yourself,

00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:55.050
but do as you wish, but
know what you're doing,

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:56.550
so that's really my goal here.

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:58.620
So as we begin to dive
further into the biology,

00:42:58.620 --> 00:42:59.890
I think you'll start to
get a clearer picture

00:42:59.890 --> 00:43:03.780
of why cannabis is so
effective in some context,

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:07.770
but also why it can create
such massive suffering

00:43:07.770 --> 00:43:10.290
in other contexts because of the way

00:43:10.290 --> 00:43:12.270
that it out-competes your own natural

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:14.066
endogenous cannabinoid systems.

00:43:14.066 --> 00:43:16.800
So let's talk about those
endogenous cannabinoid systems,

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:18.690
what they are and how they work,

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:20.430
because that will give us a lens

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:24.690
into what the higher
potency or maximum impact

00:43:24.690 --> 00:43:26.567
of the various cannabis
plant varieties and strains

00:43:26.567 --> 00:43:31.020
in THC and CBD and so forth,
how and why those work.

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:33.030
So what are the endogenous cannabinoids,

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:36.870
these chemical substances
that everybody makes?

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:38.430
You make them, I make them.

00:43:38.430 --> 00:43:40.050
You've been making them basically

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:41.220
from the time that you were conceived,

00:43:41.220 --> 00:43:43.920
and you are going to make them
until the time that you die.

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:45.540
Whether or not they have the impact

00:43:45.540 --> 00:43:47.670
and the biological functions
that I'm about to describe

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:49.590
will depend a lot on whether or not

00:43:49.590 --> 00:43:52.642
you are using your own
endogenous cannabinoids

00:43:52.642 --> 00:43:55.860
to park in those receptors
that you also have

00:43:55.860 --> 00:43:57.648
from birth until death,

00:43:57.648 --> 00:44:01.800
or whether or not you are
tickling those receptors

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:04.410
or strongly activating those receptors

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:09.410
using some external source like
cannabis and THC, et cetera.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:14.070
The two main endogenous cannabinoids

00:44:14.070 --> 00:44:16.710
that we want to consider are anandamide,

00:44:16.710 --> 00:44:20.370
which we will refer to
as EAE, so anandamide,

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:24.720
and another one, which
is arachidonoyl-glycerol,

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:28.170
arachidonoyl-glycerol, which
we will abbreviate 2-AG.

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:32.760
So let's just take EAE and
2-AG, lump them together

00:44:32.760 --> 00:44:34.800
and talk about the
endogenous cannabinoids,

00:44:34.800 --> 00:44:35.910
just to make it simple.

00:44:35.910 --> 00:44:38.280
But if you want to do the
deep dive on anadamide

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:41.643
versus 2-AG, please be my guest.

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:46.020
The endogenous cannabinoids
are released from neurons.

00:44:46.020 --> 00:44:48.630
What are neurons? Neurons are nerve cells.

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:51.960
And nerve cells should be
conceptualized like this.

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:54.990
You have presynaptic neurons
and postsynaptic neurons.

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:57.780
Presynaptic neurons basically
contain little vesicles,

00:44:57.780 --> 00:45:00.840
little bubbles full of neurotransmitter,

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:02.070
which are chemicals,

00:45:02.070 --> 00:45:05.160
and when neurons are
stimulated electrically,

00:45:05.160 --> 00:45:06.600
and that could be from a thought,

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:08.880
it could be from the desire to move,

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:10.740
it could be because of a drug,

00:45:10.740 --> 00:45:13.044
it could be because you're hungry,

00:45:13.044 --> 00:45:18.044
the relevant neurons will vomit
out or will fuse, as we say,

00:45:18.300 --> 00:45:20.640
those little packets, those little bubbles

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:22.770
of neurotransmitter into the gap

00:45:22.770 --> 00:45:23.667
between the pre and postsynaptic neuron,

00:45:23.667 --> 00:45:26.580
and we call that a synaptic
cleft or the synapse.

00:45:26.580 --> 00:45:27.930
It's a little gap, a little space,

00:45:27.930 --> 00:45:30.810
and the neurotransmitter
flows across that synapse,

00:45:30.810 --> 00:45:33.510
and some of it will park
in the little parking spots

00:45:33.510 --> 00:45:36.510
that we call receptors on
the postsynaptic neuron side.

00:45:36.510 --> 00:45:38.430
Depending on which neurotransmitter it is,

00:45:38.430 --> 00:45:41.380
and a bunch of other things
not worth going into right now,

00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:43.440
the parking of that chemical

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:44.850
and those neurotransmitter receptors

00:45:44.850 --> 00:45:48.930
will either cause that neuron
on the postsynaptic side

00:45:48.930 --> 00:45:52.390
to itself, release
neurotransmitter elsewhere,

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:54.690
or it will quiet it down,

00:45:54.690 --> 00:45:56.430
so-called excitation and inhibition.

00:45:56.430 --> 00:45:58.590
That's kind of neuro
transmission in a nutshell.

00:45:58.590 --> 00:46:00.240
If you don't understand it, no big deal.

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:01.170
It's not going to prevent you

00:46:01.170 --> 00:46:02.430
from understanding today's discussion.

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:04.410
If you understand even a small fraction

00:46:04.410 --> 00:46:05.790
of what I've just said,

00:46:05.790 --> 00:46:08.700
then it's going to allow you to understand

00:46:08.700 --> 00:46:10.508
not just today's discussion,
but a lot of neuroscience

00:46:10.508 --> 00:46:13.593
with a lot more nuance and
depth of understanding.

00:46:15.210 --> 00:46:18.960
The key thing to know about
the endogenous cannabinoids

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:22.560
is that unlike most neurotransmitters,

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:25.740
they are released from
the postsynaptic side.

00:46:25.740 --> 00:46:28.230
So what happens is neurotransmitter goes

00:46:28.230 --> 00:46:31.170
from presynaptic neuron
to postsynaptic neuron,

00:46:31.170 --> 00:46:33.030
but under certain conditions,

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:36.510
the postsynaptic neuron,
itself, releases a chemical,

00:46:36.510 --> 00:46:39.037
and that chemical goes backward,
what we call retrogradely,

00:46:39.037 --> 00:46:43.020
to the presynaptic neuron,
binds to receptors there

00:46:43.020 --> 00:46:46.182
and changes the probability
that the presynaptic neuron

00:46:46.182 --> 00:46:48.123
will release neurotransmitter.

00:46:49.341 --> 00:46:52.500
Put simply, endogenous cannabinoids

00:46:52.500 --> 00:46:55.200
tend to decrease the probability

00:46:55.200 --> 00:46:59.040
that a neuron will
release neurotransmitter.

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:00.810
They're a break on the system.

00:47:00.810 --> 00:47:04.050
They're a way of shutting
down the communication

00:47:04.050 --> 00:47:06.150
between neurons, regulating it,

00:47:06.150 --> 00:47:07.530
not to make it completely quiet,

00:47:07.530 --> 00:47:10.710
but to adjust the levels
with a lot of nuance.

00:47:10.710 --> 00:47:13.620
Now, the other thing that the
endogenous cannabinoids do

00:47:13.620 --> 00:47:17.340
is a mind bender, because
we're talking about cannabis,

00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:19.650
and a commonly known feature

00:47:19.650 --> 00:47:22.020
of cannabis and marijuana consumption

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:24.060
is disruptions in short-term memory,

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:26.250
and there is essentially zero debate

00:47:26.250 --> 00:47:27.570
as to whether or not that occurs,

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:29.820
and we'll talk about the
mechanisms a little bit later.

00:47:29.820 --> 00:47:33.359
And yet, endogenous cannabinoids,

00:47:33.359 --> 00:47:35.340
the chemicals that you naturally release

00:47:35.340 --> 00:47:36.750
from these postsynaptic neurons

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:39.759
that travel retrogradely back
to the presynaptic neuron,

00:47:39.759 --> 00:47:43.860
actually can lead to
strengthening of connections

00:47:43.860 --> 00:47:47.310
between the presynaptic neuron
and the postsynaptic neuron

00:47:47.310 --> 00:47:51.630
through a process called
long-term potentiation, or LTP.

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:52.920
They can also cause

00:47:52.920 --> 00:47:56.250
what's called depression of communication

00:47:56.250 --> 00:47:59.000
between a presynaptic neuron
and a postsynaptic neuron.

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:03.630
Long-term depression has nothing to do

00:48:03.630 --> 00:48:08.431
with depression as a psychological
state or as a illness.

00:48:08.431 --> 00:48:11.100
Long-term potentiation
and long-term depression

00:48:11.100 --> 00:48:12.960
simply refer to the probability

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:14.640
that one neuron will be able

00:48:14.640 --> 00:48:17.280
to stimulate and activate another neuron.

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:19.237
And, as I just told you,

00:48:19.237 --> 00:48:23.250
the endogenous cannabinoids
can either turn up the dial

00:48:23.250 --> 00:48:24.450
or turn down the dial.

00:48:24.450 --> 00:48:26.370
They can either increase the probability

00:48:26.370 --> 00:48:28.110
or decrease the probability

00:48:28.110 --> 00:48:30.600
that a given connection between neurons

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:33.360
will function more or will function less.

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:35.100
So if you think about the
communication between neurons

00:48:35.100 --> 00:48:37.890
as crosstalk, as a conversation,

00:48:37.890 --> 00:48:42.000
well, the endogenous cannabinoids
can dictate whether or not

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:44.700
that conversation is likely
to occur or not to occur.

00:48:44.700 --> 00:48:47.400
Think of them as either putting someone

00:48:47.400 --> 00:48:49.979
at the top of your text
chain in your phone,

00:48:49.979 --> 00:48:52.500
which would be long-term potentiation,

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:55.140
or essentially blocking their number,

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:57.723
which would essentially
be long-term depression.

00:48:58.740 --> 00:49:00.900
So, if you're getting the impression

00:49:00.900 --> 00:49:02.280
that the endogenous cannabinoids

00:49:02.280 --> 00:49:04.200
are working in a number of different ways,

00:49:04.200 --> 00:49:06.270
and it's not very
straightforward, you're right.

00:49:06.270 --> 00:49:08.490
In fact, that's the message
that I'd like you to take away.

00:49:08.490 --> 00:49:10.290
The endogenous cannabinoids

00:49:10.290 --> 00:49:12.450
are sometimes increasing
neuronal communication.

00:49:12.450 --> 00:49:14.460
This can lead to increases in mood

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:16.380
or increases in the likelihood

00:49:16.380 --> 00:49:18.420
that someone will talk a certain way

00:49:18.420 --> 00:49:20.523
or behave a certain way
or feel a certain way.

00:49:20.523 --> 00:49:24.240
That can also lead to decreases
in synaptic transmission,

00:49:24.240 --> 00:49:25.800
that is communication between neurons,

00:49:25.800 --> 00:49:29.190
in ways that will make
somebody's mood lower,

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:32.400
or will make them less
hungry or more hungry.

00:49:32.400 --> 00:49:35.010
And, here's the really key thing,

00:49:35.010 --> 00:49:38.280
there are two kinds of
endogenous cannabinoid receptors,

00:49:38.280 --> 00:49:40.980
referred to as CB1 and CB2,

00:49:40.980 --> 00:49:44.670
and we can say with confidence that CB1

00:49:44.670 --> 00:49:46.680
is highly enriched in the nervous system,

00:49:46.680 --> 00:49:48.180
and especially within the brain.

00:49:48.180 --> 00:49:49.530
It's found not everywhere,

00:49:49.530 --> 00:49:51.120
but almost everywhere in the brain

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:52.200
and elsewhere in the nervous system,

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:55.980
so spinal cord and other
aspects of the nervous system.

00:49:55.980 --> 00:50:00.270
CB2, the cannabinoid receptor, CB2

00:50:00.270 --> 00:50:02.550
is largely located in
the tissues of the body,

00:50:02.550 --> 00:50:04.165
including the immune system, the liver,

00:50:04.165 --> 00:50:07.080
even the genitals, et cetera.

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:09.413
So what this means is that
the endogenous cannabinoids

00:50:09.413 --> 00:50:11.400
are having these sorts of effects

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:12.660
on neurons that I talked about,

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:15.690
but they are also having
effects on immune cells,

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:17.640
on reproductive organs, on liver,

00:50:17.640 --> 00:50:19.710
on digestion, on hunger, et cetera,

00:50:19.710 --> 00:50:22.050
through mechanisms that are divorced

00:50:22.050 --> 00:50:24.930
from the function of the
nervous system of neurons.

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:26.910
Now, in reality, no system
of the brain and body

00:50:26.910 --> 00:50:28.110
is divorced from the nervous system,

00:50:28.110 --> 00:50:30.360
because the nervous system
is controlling everything.

00:50:30.360 --> 00:50:31.680
It is really the master controller,

00:50:31.680 --> 00:50:33.480
and everything's feeding
back to the nervous system,

00:50:33.480 --> 00:50:34.920
so it's a two way street.

00:50:34.920 --> 00:50:37.819
But the simple way to think
about it is CB1 receptors

00:50:37.819 --> 00:50:39.927
are mainly in the nervous system,

00:50:39.927 --> 00:50:42.360
and CB2 receptors are mainly in the body

00:50:42.360 --> 00:50:47.360
and endogenous cannabinoids,
again, EAE, anandamide,

00:50:47.378 --> 00:50:50.580
and 2-AG, arachidonoyl-glycerol,

00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:53.490
are impacting CB1 and CB2 receptors.

00:50:53.490 --> 00:50:55.307
Today we're mainly going to
talk about CB1 receptors,

00:50:55.307 --> 00:50:57.270
'cause they are the ones
that are responsible

00:50:57.270 --> 00:50:59.820
for most of the familiar
effects of cannabis,

00:50:59.820 --> 00:51:04.290
but the key takeaway at this
point is to really understand

00:51:04.290 --> 00:51:07.740
that the major effects of
your endogenous cannabinoids

00:51:07.740 --> 00:51:12.240
on these receptors in this
particular CB1 are very nuanced.

00:51:12.240 --> 00:51:14.280
It depends on context,
depends on which neurons.

00:51:14.280 --> 00:51:17.040
It's sometimes increasing
communication between neurons,

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:21.270
sometimes decreasing it, and
then along comes cannabis,

00:51:21.270 --> 00:51:23.070
and that cannabis, again,

00:51:23.070 --> 00:51:26.376
can arrive by smoking,
by vaping, by edible.

00:51:26.376 --> 00:51:29.758
And cannabis contains THC and CBD

00:51:29.758 --> 00:51:34.740
that potently bind the CB1 receptor,

00:51:34.740 --> 00:51:37.620
and now the effects of the CB1 receptor

00:51:37.620 --> 00:51:42.620
being occupied by THC or
being occupied by CBD,

00:51:43.199 --> 00:51:44.880
are not very nuanced.

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:47.550
In fact, they are very predictable

00:51:47.550 --> 00:51:50.160
and especially important is to understand

00:51:50.160 --> 00:51:53.280
that they are so strong, and
they park in that receptor

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:56.580
with such affinity, with
such force and precision

00:51:56.580 --> 00:51:59.140
and stubbornness and refusal
to leave that receptor

00:52:00.150 --> 00:52:02.040
that they completely outcompete

00:52:02.040 --> 00:52:04.140
the endogenous cannabinoid system.

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:06.480
In fact, they leave the
endogenous cannabinoid system

00:52:06.480 --> 00:52:10.050
essentially dysfunctional,
which, in some cases,

00:52:10.050 --> 00:52:12.600
may be a good thing, but in most cases,

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:15.120
is going to lead to
problems of various kinds,

00:52:15.120 --> 00:52:16.560
and we'll talk about
what sorts of problems.

00:52:16.560 --> 00:52:18.511
And again, I feel obligated to say,

00:52:18.511 --> 00:52:23.250
this is not me saying don't
ingest cannabis or THC or CBD.

00:52:23.250 --> 00:52:24.420
That's not what I'm saying.

00:52:24.420 --> 00:52:27.528
What I'm saying is if
you evaluate the potency

00:52:27.528 --> 00:52:29.490
that is in technical terms,

00:52:29.490 --> 00:52:32.580
it would be the affinity
with which these compounds,

00:52:32.580 --> 00:52:37.260
THC and CBD and CBN, bind to
these endogenous receptors,

00:52:37.260 --> 00:52:40.740
that would be like a howitzer
gun, like a cannon going off,

00:52:40.740 --> 00:52:44.040
as compared to endogenous cannabinoid,

00:52:44.040 --> 00:52:46.620
whether or not it's EAE or 2-AG,

00:52:46.620 --> 00:52:49.740
which is more like a cap
gun level of activation,

00:52:49.740 --> 00:52:51.000
at least in this analogy.

00:52:51.000 --> 00:52:53.096
So now you have what at
least I would like to think

00:52:53.096 --> 00:52:55.680
is a fairly complete understanding

00:52:55.680 --> 00:52:57.810
of the different varieties of cannabis,

00:52:57.810 --> 00:52:59.962
at least at a broad sweep,

00:52:59.962 --> 00:53:03.120
and the different biological
effects that they can have,

00:53:03.120 --> 00:53:04.920
at least in terms of the major receptors

00:53:04.920 --> 00:53:07.980
and in retrograde signaling,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:53:07.980 --> 00:53:10.530
Now, let's take a step
back into the real world

00:53:10.530 --> 00:53:12.540
and evaluate or think about

00:53:12.540 --> 00:53:15.889
what happens when somebody smokes cannabis

00:53:15.889 --> 00:53:18.720
or ingests cannabis by way of edible

00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:20.620
or tincture or something of that sort.

00:53:22.290 --> 00:53:25.443
Cannabis is very fast to
enter the bloodstream.

00:53:26.472 --> 00:53:30.090
In fact, within 30 seconds,
it's going to enter the brain

00:53:30.090 --> 00:53:32.370
and permeate throughout
the brain and body.

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:33.750
That's very, very fast.

00:53:33.750 --> 00:53:34.800
I mean, when you contrast that

00:53:34.800 --> 00:53:37.320
with something like
alcohol or even nicotine,

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:39.180
depending on how the
nicotine is delivered,

00:53:39.180 --> 00:53:42.600
that is a very fast
delivery of the psychoactive

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:44.400
and biologically active compound,

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:46.347
which in this case is THC and CBD

00:53:46.347 --> 00:53:48.810
and probably some other things as well.

00:53:48.810 --> 00:53:49.950
So within 30 seconds,

00:53:49.950 --> 00:53:52.950
it reaches the brain and bodily tissues,

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:56.190
and within 30 to 60 minutes,

00:53:56.190 --> 00:53:58.342
it's going to reach
its peak concentrations

00:53:58.342 --> 00:54:00.960
and have its peak biological effects.

00:54:00.960 --> 00:54:01.980
Those aren't always the same thing,

00:54:01.980 --> 00:54:03.420
but in the case of cannabis,

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:06.259
again here I'm using
cannabis as an umbrella term

00:54:06.259 --> 00:54:08.850
for THC and CBD, the
effects are going to peak

00:54:08.850 --> 00:54:12.600
at about 30 to 60 minutes
after bringing those compounds

00:54:12.600 --> 00:54:15.060
into the body in some way or another.

00:54:15.060 --> 00:54:16.410
And the effects tend to last

00:54:16.410 --> 00:54:18.030
anywhere from three to four hours,

00:54:18.030 --> 00:54:19.770
although there's some variation on that,

00:54:19.770 --> 00:54:21.900
depending on individual metabolism,

00:54:21.900 --> 00:54:26.400
whether or not somebody is
familiar with the compound,

00:54:26.400 --> 00:54:27.840
believe it or not,
psychologically familiar,

00:54:27.840 --> 00:54:29.520
but also biologically familiar,

00:54:29.520 --> 00:54:30.840
or whether or not it's a first time use

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:32.240
or occasional use and so on.

00:54:33.746 --> 00:54:37.710
THC and CBD and other
components of cannabis

00:54:37.710 --> 00:54:39.600
are highly what we call lipophilic.

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:42.390
That is, they have an affinity toward,

00:54:42.390 --> 00:54:44.203
and they can actually pass
through fatty tissues.

00:54:44.203 --> 00:54:47.100
Now, every cell in your
body, but especially neurons,

00:54:47.100 --> 00:54:49.917
have a double layer of
fat on their outside.

00:54:49.917 --> 00:54:51.840
And of course, when people hear "fat,"

00:54:51.840 --> 00:54:54.150
they always think, ooh, fat's bad.

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:57.060
Most of the world seems to
want to lose fat or bodily fat.

00:54:57.060 --> 00:54:59.850
Here we're talking about
the fatty membrane,

00:54:59.850 --> 00:55:01.650
the barrier around each tissue,

00:55:01.650 --> 00:55:04.410
in this case we're talking
particularly about neurons.

00:55:04.410 --> 00:55:08.790
And THC and CBD and the
other components of cannabis

00:55:08.790 --> 00:55:11.040
are highly lipophilic,
so they can get into

00:55:11.040 --> 00:55:13.740
essentially all cells just
simply by flowing into them.

00:55:13.740 --> 00:55:17.040
They will also remain in
those cells for a long time.

00:55:17.040 --> 00:55:19.350
So, I know that a number of people,

00:55:19.350 --> 00:55:21.420
depending on whether
or not they get tested

00:55:21.420 --> 00:55:23.520
for work or for sport or otherwise

00:55:23.520 --> 00:55:26.343
for cannabis or CBD and THC,

00:55:27.240 --> 00:55:29.010
don't take this as a strict number,

00:55:29.010 --> 00:55:34.010
but typically, if one ingests CBD or THC,

00:55:34.020 --> 00:55:36.960
smokes cannabis, ingests
by orally, et cetera,

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:39.600
doesn't matter, it's going
to stay in that fatty tissue

00:55:39.600 --> 00:55:42.990
and can be detected for at
least as long as 80 days

00:55:42.990 --> 00:55:44.850
after ingestion, and
there's a whole industry

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:47.910
as to how to accelerate the clearance,

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:51.300
and I should just tell you
that just losing bodily fat

00:55:51.300 --> 00:55:54.008
isn't going to eliminate
it from your system,

00:55:54.008 --> 00:55:56.460
maybe partially in those fat cells,

00:55:56.460 --> 00:56:00.450
but certainly intravisceral
fat and other fatty tissue

00:56:00.450 --> 00:56:02.400
that's in around the brain and body

00:56:02.400 --> 00:56:06.720
is going to harbor that THC
molecule and the CBD molecule

00:56:06.720 --> 00:56:09.300
for quite a long while, at least 80 days.

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:13.500
So, if someone smokes cannabis
or they ingest cannabis,

00:56:13.500 --> 00:56:16.440
it very rapidly gets into the bloodstream,

00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:18.210
and the components that are psychoactive

00:56:18.210 --> 00:56:19.260
get into the bloodstream

00:56:19.260 --> 00:56:23.610
and are immediately able to
access neurons and other cells

00:56:23.610 --> 00:56:24.630
and start having these effects

00:56:24.630 --> 00:56:27.240
of parking at those endogenous
cannabinoid receptors

00:56:27.240 --> 00:56:29.520
and impacting the
signaling between neurons,

00:56:29.520 --> 00:56:34.020
which leads to the subjective
effects of cannabis,

00:56:34.020 --> 00:56:35.970
including THC and CBDs,
so let's talk about

00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:38.550
what those different
subjective effects are.

00:56:38.550 --> 00:56:40.530
Again, this is going to vary
depending on whether or not

00:56:40.530 --> 00:56:44.307
people are ingesting sativa
varieties of cannabis,

00:56:44.307 --> 00:56:47.550
just to remind you, those
tend to be elevated mood,

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:49.020
alertness, talkativeness.

00:56:49.020 --> 00:56:51.750
People who take sativa varieties
tend to talk a lot more

00:56:51.750 --> 00:56:53.430
than they would otherwise.

00:56:53.430 --> 00:56:54.840
Again, there are exceptions to this,

00:56:54.840 --> 00:56:56.070
of course there are exceptions.

00:56:56.070 --> 00:56:57.420
I'm sure there are people
out there shouting,

00:56:57.420 --> 00:56:58.650
although I guess if
you're the quiet people

00:56:58.650 --> 00:57:00.320
who don't talk too much,
you're probably not shouting,

00:57:00.320 --> 00:57:02.940
or if you're not, you're
not doing on sativa,

00:57:02.940 --> 00:57:05.970
Joe contended, but in any
event, there are exceptions,

00:57:05.970 --> 00:57:07.770
but there are also general rules.

00:57:07.770 --> 00:57:10.110
And the sativas tend to
make people's mood elevated,

00:57:10.110 --> 00:57:13.080
energetic, again, this sort of head high,

00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:16.530
and indica varieties
tend to do the opposite.

00:57:16.530 --> 00:57:19.920
more of a sedative, relaxant, et cetera.

00:57:19.920 --> 00:57:21.930
Why and how would they do that?

00:57:21.930 --> 00:57:24.330
Well, without going into
an extensive deep dive

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:25.700
into the different
neurotransmitter systems

00:57:25.700 --> 00:57:28.650
of the brain and body,
what we know for sure

00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:31.290
is that CB1 receptors are present

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:33.090
on an enormous number of different neurons

00:57:33.090 --> 00:57:35.370
in brain structures in neural circuits,

00:57:35.370 --> 00:57:39.780
so that the sativa varieties
that act as a stimulant,

00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:42.870
making people feel happy,

00:57:42.870 --> 00:57:45.450
'cause in general, they
do tend to elevate mood,

00:57:45.450 --> 00:57:49.140
at least at certain dosages, talkative,

00:57:49.140 --> 00:57:51.960
tend to make people feel
like they have ideas

00:57:51.960 --> 00:57:54.540
that are interesting, that
they might want to share,

00:57:54.540 --> 00:57:57.270
tend to narrow their context,
so we tend to increase focus.

00:57:57.270 --> 00:57:59.460
This is something that's not
often discussed about cannabis,

00:57:59.460 --> 00:58:01.320
but especially the sativa varieties

00:58:01.320 --> 00:58:04.410
can increase people's level
of focus to particular things,

00:58:04.410 --> 00:58:06.300
something they're watching
or something they're doing

00:58:06.300 --> 00:58:09.100
or music, it allows them to
narrow their sense of focus.

00:58:10.260 --> 00:58:13.440
That's going to occur by
activation of CB1 receptors

00:58:13.440 --> 00:58:14.940
in the so-called prefrontal cortex,

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:16.590
which is just behind the forehead,

00:58:16.590 --> 00:58:19.890
and the prefrontal cortex
acts as a strong modulator

00:58:19.890 --> 00:58:22.620
of so-called limbic
circuitry and other circuitry

00:58:22.620 --> 00:58:24.600
that is more stress oriented.

00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:27.990
The way to think about the
stress and limbic circuitry,

00:58:27.990 --> 00:58:29.963
such as the amygdala, which
many people have heard about,

00:58:29.963 --> 00:58:32.728
is that they aren't really
circuits for fear and stress.

00:58:32.728 --> 00:58:35.400
They are circuits that
are constantly evaluating

00:58:35.400 --> 00:58:38.280
one's own internal state,
heart rate, et cetera,

00:58:38.280 --> 00:58:41.790
and what's happening
externally, and, sorry to say,

00:58:41.790 --> 00:58:44.850
but the default of those
systems is to detect danger,

00:58:44.850 --> 00:58:46.380
so threat detection systems.

00:58:46.380 --> 00:58:48.540
And then the prefrontal
cortex largely acts

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:50.340
as a brake on those systems,

00:58:50.340 --> 00:58:53.190
so like the reins pulling
back on a steed of horses

00:58:53.190 --> 00:58:55.500
that would otherwise just take off.

00:58:55.500 --> 00:58:59.310
And so, the sativa varieties
tend to increase CB1 activation

00:58:59.310 --> 00:59:02.130
in the prefrontal cortex
and in other circuitry

00:59:02.130 --> 00:59:05.250
that then leads to an
overall reduction in stress,

00:59:05.250 --> 00:59:06.720
because of the way that
prefrontal circuitry

00:59:06.720 --> 00:59:09.270
can reduce activation or the amygdala.

00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:11.580
Now that, of course, does not explain

00:59:11.580 --> 00:59:14.550
why some people become very
stressed and very paranoid

00:59:14.550 --> 00:59:16.200
when they smoke sativa varieties

00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:17.970
or other varieties of cannabis

00:59:17.970 --> 00:59:19.650
or ingest other varieties of cannabis.

00:59:19.650 --> 00:59:22.560
We'll talk about the paranoid
effect and why that occurs

00:59:22.560 --> 00:59:25.230
and who might predict
that would occur to them

00:59:25.230 --> 00:59:26.760
in a little bit.

00:59:26.760 --> 00:59:28.950
But I just want to give you a
sense of how this is working,

00:59:28.950 --> 00:59:30.513
because as I mentioned before,

00:59:31.350 --> 00:59:34.920
THC and/or CBD are going
to bind that CB1 receptor,

00:59:34.920 --> 00:59:35.847
let's say in prefrontal cortex,

00:59:35.847 --> 00:59:38.400
the neurons of the prefrontal
cortex, it's going to bind there,

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:40.350
and then they'll be a retrograde signaling

00:59:40.350 --> 00:59:41.337
back to the presynaptic neuron,

00:59:41.337 --> 00:59:43.470
and in the case of prefrontal cortex,

00:59:43.470 --> 00:59:46.470
what's happening is it's
increasing transmission,

00:59:46.470 --> 00:59:48.150
increasing the release of neurotransmitter

00:59:48.150 --> 00:59:49.740
in the prefrontal cortex.

00:59:49.740 --> 00:59:51.483
However, at the same time,

00:59:53.070 --> 00:59:56.520
the very same THC and CBD that
was brought into the system

00:59:56.520 --> 01:00:00.120
is binding the very same type
of receptors, CB1 receptors,

01:00:00.120 --> 01:00:03.300
in other brain structures,
such as the amygdala

01:00:03.300 --> 01:00:05.820
and causing retrograde signaling

01:00:05.820 --> 01:00:09.180
back to the presynaptic
neurons in the amygdala,

01:00:09.180 --> 01:00:12.886
but it's quieting the
activation of those neurons,

01:00:12.886 --> 01:00:14.490
so this is interesting.

01:00:14.490 --> 01:00:18.240
We have the same
compounds, THC and/or CBD,

01:00:18.240 --> 01:00:20.910
brought into the body and brain,

01:00:20.910 --> 01:00:25.230
binding the same receptors, in
this case the CB1 receptors,

01:00:25.230 --> 01:00:28.470
but depending on where
those receptors are located

01:00:28.470 --> 01:00:31.110
and which brain areas we're referring to,

01:00:31.110 --> 01:00:36.110
they are either causing
heightened levels of alertness

01:00:36.240 --> 01:00:38.580
and activation of
systems that are designed

01:00:38.580 --> 01:00:41.070
to make you talkative and
alertness and mood, et cetera,

01:00:41.070 --> 01:00:45.720
focus, or they're causing
suppression of those circuitries.

01:00:45.720 --> 01:00:47.730
So we have kind of a seesaw effect here

01:00:47.730 --> 01:00:49.920
where the same compound is
increasing mood and alertness

01:00:49.920 --> 01:00:51.270
and focus in the prefrontal cortex

01:00:51.270 --> 01:00:54.540
and is decreasing stress
and threat detection

01:00:54.540 --> 01:00:57.420
in the amygdala, and that's
one of the reasons why,

01:00:57.420 --> 01:01:00.360
especially the sativa
varieties of cannabis,

01:01:00.360 --> 01:01:02.820
allow people to enter
these states of focus,

01:01:02.820 --> 01:01:05.610
some might even say flow,
although I don't want to go

01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:07.380
into what flow states really are.

01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:09.210
That's for a different discussion,

01:01:09.210 --> 01:01:12.630
and it's very poorly defined as it is.

01:01:12.630 --> 01:01:14.400
And I certainly don't want
to give people the impression

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:16.080
that cannabis increases flow states,

01:01:16.080 --> 01:01:18.660
because that's not always the case,

01:01:18.660 --> 01:01:21.060
and certainly most often is
not going to be the case.

01:01:21.060 --> 01:01:24.540
But the idea here is that this
molecule comes into our brain

01:01:24.540 --> 01:01:27.720
and is shifting everything
towards a state of focus,

01:01:27.720 --> 01:01:30.960
elevated mood of heightened
sense of importance

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:32.820
about whatever it is that
we happen to be doing.

01:01:32.820 --> 01:01:35.430
And now of course, whatever
we could happen to be doing

01:01:35.430 --> 01:01:39.240
could be writing a song, writing poetry,

01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:40.560
communicating with somebody,

01:01:40.560 --> 01:01:42.300
but it could also be something as trivial

01:01:42.300 --> 01:01:45.870
as watching cartoons or watching a movie,

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:48.120
which is not trivial in its own right,

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:50.910
but in terms of thinking
about the creative aspects

01:01:50.910 --> 01:01:54.240
or the creativity stimulating
aspects of cannabis,

01:01:54.240 --> 01:01:57.030
not productivity oriented.

01:01:57.030 --> 01:01:59.070
So narrowed focus, elevated mood,

01:01:59.070 --> 01:02:01.230
more relaxed and yet energetic,

01:02:01.230 --> 01:02:04.170
that's the major effects
of the sativa varieties,

01:02:04.170 --> 01:02:07.050
except, and this is a really big boldface,

01:02:07.050 --> 01:02:08.910
triple underlined 'except,'

01:02:08.910 --> 01:02:12.360
except in some individuals,
depending on dosage,

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:16.410
but also depending on
preexisting neural circuitry

01:02:16.410 --> 01:02:18.270
and propensity for anxiety,

01:02:18.270 --> 01:02:22.170
some people ingest or
smoke sativa varieties,

01:02:22.170 --> 01:02:23.940
and regardless of whether
or not it's a type one,

01:02:23.940 --> 01:02:25.980
type two or type three variety,

01:02:25.980 --> 01:02:29.160
regardless of the ratio
between THC and CBD,

01:02:29.160 --> 01:02:33.930
people will experience
intense anxiety and paranoia.

01:02:33.930 --> 01:02:35.640
Now, how do you predict

01:02:35.640 --> 01:02:38.220
who will experience intense
anxiety and paranoia

01:02:38.220 --> 01:02:41.700
and who will experience
intense relaxation, focus

01:02:41.700 --> 01:02:44.730
and sense of creativity from ingesting

01:02:44.730 --> 01:02:47.940
or smoking a type one, type
two or type three sativa?

01:02:47.940 --> 01:02:50.550
Well, there is no way to predict that,

01:02:50.550 --> 01:02:53.010
and there's a lot of what
I would call street lore

01:02:53.010 --> 01:02:57.900
or dorm room lore or not peer reviewed,

01:02:57.900 --> 01:03:02.130
but peer discussed
among friends and people

01:03:02.130 --> 01:03:05.850
and acquaintances lore out
there that what one needs to do

01:03:05.850 --> 01:03:08.640
is simply smoke more, or just ingest more.

01:03:08.640 --> 01:03:10.987
You hear that, "Oh, well listen,
if it makes you paranoid,

01:03:10.987 --> 01:03:12.360
"you simply need to use more,"

01:03:12.360 --> 01:03:14.850
that is absolutely categorically false.

01:03:14.850 --> 01:03:18.210
Everything we know about the
way that THC and CBD work

01:03:18.210 --> 01:03:20.070
is that they tend to potentiate,

01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:25.070
that is increase the effects
of these different systems

01:03:25.530 --> 01:03:27.300
at given synapses and in different areas

01:03:27.300 --> 01:03:28.133
of the brain and body.

01:03:28.133 --> 01:03:32.910
That is, if someone
experiences paranoia or anxiety

01:03:32.910 --> 01:03:37.140
from a given strain of the marijuana plant

01:03:37.140 --> 01:03:39.033
or from ingesting an
edible in a particular way

01:03:39.033 --> 01:03:40.773
or a particular kind of edible,

01:03:41.700 --> 01:03:44.850
that person is very likely
to experience the same effect

01:03:44.850 --> 01:03:47.433
every time they ingest
that strain or variety.

01:03:48.630 --> 01:03:52.080
This is part of what's led
to this enormous industry.

01:03:52.080 --> 01:03:53.100
I mean, there are a number
of different reasons,

01:03:53.100 --> 01:03:55.260
but this is part of what's
led to this enormous industry

01:03:55.260 --> 01:03:58.560
of highly customized cannabis

01:03:58.560 --> 01:04:00.270
where people will spend some time

01:04:00.270 --> 01:04:02.730
really seeking out the
different strains of cannabis

01:04:02.730 --> 01:04:06.329
and hybrids of cannabis
that work best for them

01:04:06.329 --> 01:04:09.690
and work best for them
in particular context.

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:12.840
I wish I could tell you
that if you are a person

01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:16.320
who is between 5' 7" and 6' tall,

01:04:16.320 --> 01:04:19.440
and you have blue eyes or brown eyes,

01:04:19.440 --> 01:04:21.810
that the sativa varieties are
going to be right for you,

01:04:21.810 --> 01:04:24.450
or that sativa varieties are
going to give you panic attacks,

01:04:24.450 --> 01:04:25.470
I can't do that.

01:04:25.470 --> 01:04:27.180
The only way to determine it would be

01:04:27.180 --> 01:04:30.180
to actually experience ingesting
those or smoking those,

01:04:30.180 --> 01:04:32.460
which is certainly also
not what I'm suggesting.

01:04:32.460 --> 01:04:33.293
That's up to you.

01:04:33.293 --> 01:04:35.220
I'm not telling you what
to do or what not to do,

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:36.990
but there are no good predictors.

01:04:36.990 --> 01:04:39.960
In fact, if you look in the literature,

01:04:39.960 --> 01:04:41.550
it is not at all clear that people

01:04:41.550 --> 01:04:44.151
who have a heightened level of anxiety

01:04:44.151 --> 01:04:47.040
when they do not smoke cannabis

01:04:47.040 --> 01:04:49.890
will experience cannabis

01:04:49.890 --> 01:04:53.250
as less paranoia inducing
or more relaxing.

01:04:53.250 --> 01:04:55.230
That's simply not the case.

01:04:55.230 --> 01:04:57.930
Now, what we can say for sure

01:04:57.930 --> 01:05:00.810
is that general categories of effects,

01:05:00.810 --> 01:05:03.630
such as increased focus
and reduced anxiety

01:05:03.630 --> 01:05:05.460
are largely due to activation of areas

01:05:05.460 --> 01:05:06.990
like the prefrontal cortex.

01:05:06.990 --> 01:05:10.200
Now, unlike other compounds
like nicotine or alcohol

01:05:10.200 --> 01:05:12.180
or neurotransmitter systems like dopamine,

01:05:12.180 --> 01:05:14.520
when we talk about the cannabinoid system,

01:05:14.520 --> 01:05:18.990
and I say effects, biological
effects, psychoactive effects,

01:05:18.990 --> 01:05:20.910
I want you to keep in mind always,

01:05:20.910 --> 01:05:22.890
please, please, please keep in mind

01:05:22.890 --> 01:05:25.530
that those effects can be varied

01:05:25.530 --> 01:05:28.710
and often opposite in direction,

01:05:28.710 --> 01:05:30.900
so let's just give an example of that.

01:05:30.900 --> 01:05:34.800
I just mentioned that when
people smoke or or eat sativa,

01:05:34.800 --> 01:05:37.145
that it tends to lead to one specific set,

01:05:37.145 --> 01:05:40.590
or generally leads to one
specific set of effects,

01:05:40.590 --> 01:05:41.970
heightened focus, mood, et cetera.

01:05:41.970 --> 01:05:46.970
Whereas when they ingest or
smoke indica and its components,

01:05:47.340 --> 01:05:49.890
again, we're still
talking about THC and CBD

01:05:49.890 --> 01:05:54.457
in varying ratios, but now
indica cannabis, and you say,

01:05:54.457 --> 01:05:58.200
"Well, why would it improve
the transition time to sleep?"

01:05:58.200 --> 01:05:59.520
Or at least give people the impression

01:05:59.520 --> 01:06:01.050
that it improves the
transition time to sleep.

01:06:01.050 --> 01:06:02.610
We'll talk about what indica actually does

01:06:02.610 --> 01:06:04.470
for sleep in a little bit,

01:06:04.470 --> 01:06:07.220
but indica also tends
to suppress activation

01:06:07.220 --> 01:06:09.630
of the amygdala and threat
detection centers in the brain,

01:06:09.630 --> 01:06:11.610
again, binding the same CB1 receptors

01:06:11.610 --> 01:06:13.719
in those retrograde signaling mechanisms

01:06:13.719 --> 01:06:15.150
that I talked about before,

01:06:15.150 --> 01:06:18.360
but it also tends to shut
down the hippocampus,

01:06:18.360 --> 01:06:21.750
an area of the brain
associated with memory,

01:06:21.750 --> 01:06:26.100
which is why indica
varieties lead to pronounced,

01:06:26.100 --> 01:06:29.430
or I should say profound
defects in short-term memory

01:06:29.430 --> 01:06:31.050
and sometimes in long-term memory as well,

01:06:31.050 --> 01:06:33.930
if it's consumed over
long periods of time.

01:06:33.930 --> 01:06:36.300
We'll talk about short, medium
and long-term consumption,

01:06:36.300 --> 01:06:38.040
occasional consumption going forward.

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:39.090
So what I'd like you to take away

01:06:39.090 --> 01:06:41.793
from this component of the
discussion is first of all,

01:06:42.690 --> 01:06:43.950
the mechanism of action

01:06:43.950 --> 01:06:47.896
by which cannabis impacts
the brain and body,

01:06:47.896 --> 01:06:49.320
but in particular the brain,

01:06:49.320 --> 01:06:50.607
is going to be through CB1 receptors,

01:06:50.607 --> 01:06:54.240
and those CB1 receptors can
lead to either an acceleration

01:06:54.240 --> 01:06:56.730
or a brake on particular
biological mechanisms.

01:06:56.730 --> 01:06:59.640
And there are going to be a constellation

01:06:59.640 --> 01:07:02.010
of different accelerations and braking

01:07:02.010 --> 01:07:05.190
of different neural systems
in the brain and body,

01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:07.950
depending on whether or
not people ingest sativa

01:07:07.950 --> 01:07:10.084
or indica or some hybrid strain.

01:07:10.084 --> 01:07:11.910
And perhaps most importantly,

01:07:11.910 --> 01:07:12.743
even if you didn't understand anything

01:07:12.743 --> 01:07:14.490
that I've said about the biology

01:07:14.490 --> 01:07:16.440
of these different
strains in the receptors,

01:07:16.440 --> 01:07:20.400
please do understand that
there is no way to predict

01:07:20.400 --> 01:07:24.390
what the effect of a given
strain will be on an individual.

01:07:24.390 --> 01:07:26.760
There has been extensive exploration

01:07:26.760 --> 01:07:29.370
as to whether or not people
who are so called mellower

01:07:29.370 --> 01:07:32.310
or more anxious or any number

01:07:32.310 --> 01:07:34.680
of different personality dimensions

01:07:34.680 --> 01:07:36.150
will respond in one way or the other,

01:07:36.150 --> 01:07:38.610
but, in fact, there is no way to tell.

01:07:38.610 --> 01:07:43.260
Layer on top of that the
fact that dosing THC and CBD

01:07:43.260 --> 01:07:46.350
can be fairly straightforward
in the form of edibles,

01:07:46.350 --> 01:07:49.934
because there can be, at least
if it's a controlled source,

01:07:49.934 --> 01:07:53.580
a defined number of milligrams of THC,

01:07:53.580 --> 01:07:56.010
a defined number of milligrams of CBD.

01:07:56.010 --> 01:07:57.960
That's true for ingestibles.

01:07:57.960 --> 01:08:00.180
It's much harder to gauge that

01:08:00.180 --> 01:08:02.580
from the smokeable forms of cannabis,

01:08:02.580 --> 01:08:04.226
especially if those
smokeable forms of cannabis

01:08:04.226 --> 01:08:05.730
are obtained through sources

01:08:05.730 --> 01:08:08.190
where there isn't a lot
of clear information

01:08:08.190 --> 01:08:11.280
about the total amount
of THC in that product.

01:08:11.280 --> 01:08:13.320
Now, this is all changing
quite a lot nowadays

01:08:13.320 --> 01:08:17.550
because of the commercialization
of of THC and CBD products

01:08:17.550 --> 01:08:19.530
and cannabis in a number
of different areas,

01:08:19.530 --> 01:08:21.270
including in the United States.

01:08:21.270 --> 01:08:24.240
But still, many people
are ingesting cannabis,

01:08:24.240 --> 01:08:26.220
THC, CBD through sources
where they don't really know

01:08:26.220 --> 01:08:28.107
how much they're bringing
into their system.

01:08:28.107 --> 01:08:30.180
And so, whether or not someone

01:08:30.180 --> 01:08:34.080
gets incredible anxiety
relief, enhanced sense of mood

01:08:34.080 --> 01:08:37.110
and focus and wellbeing,
pain relief, et cetera,

01:08:37.110 --> 01:08:39.690
or whether or not they have
full blown panic attacks,

01:08:39.690 --> 01:08:42.960
et cetera, is very hard to predict

01:08:42.960 --> 01:08:45.240
based on dosage information alone.

01:08:45.240 --> 01:08:47.730
Now, of course, we can
create broad categories,

01:08:47.730 --> 01:08:48.930
and we are going to talk about studies

01:08:48.930 --> 01:08:51.570
that create broad categories
of low dose, moderate dose,

01:08:51.570 --> 01:08:54.960
and high dose. frequent
use and infrequent use,

01:08:54.960 --> 01:08:57.780
but unlike alcohol, unlike nicotine,

01:08:57.780 --> 01:09:02.780
we can't really point to
specificity of X amount of alcohol,

01:09:03.450 --> 01:09:05.340
grams of alcohol per week, which is safe,

01:09:05.340 --> 01:09:07.740
or X amount of alcohol, which is not safe.

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:10.260
And so I know a lot of people
out there are wondering,

01:09:10.260 --> 01:09:13.290
how often can they smoke cannabis,

01:09:13.290 --> 01:09:16.890
or how often can they eat
cannabis or THC or CBD

01:09:16.890 --> 01:09:21.240
in any number of its different
forms and products, safely?

01:09:21.240 --> 01:09:23.790
Well, we have to really
define what 'safely' means,

01:09:23.790 --> 01:09:25.260
and we have to really acknowledge

01:09:25.260 --> 01:09:29.460
that there's a pretty
loose set of controls

01:09:29.460 --> 01:09:32.550
over what one is bringing
into their brain and body

01:09:32.550 --> 01:09:35.220
as they ingest THC and CBD,

01:09:35.220 --> 01:09:38.490
but even under conditions in
which it's very controlled,

01:09:38.490 --> 01:09:41.070
it's very hard to predict
what those effects will be.

01:09:41.070 --> 01:09:43.260
So before moving into specifics

01:09:43.260 --> 01:09:45.900
of taking cannabis or not taking cannabis,

01:09:45.900 --> 01:09:46.800
who should, who shouldn't,

01:09:46.800 --> 01:09:49.317
what the medicinal purposes are

01:09:49.317 --> 01:09:52.620
and what some of the newer,
exciting data point to,

01:09:52.620 --> 01:09:53.940
I just briefly want to make a list,

01:09:53.940 --> 01:09:56.700
and I promise very briefly,
I know I'm not often concise,

01:09:56.700 --> 01:09:59.430
but I do try to be thorough for your sake.

01:09:59.430 --> 01:10:03.360
I want to make a very brief list
of the different brain areas

01:10:03.360 --> 01:10:06.600
that are impacted by THC and CBD

01:10:06.600 --> 01:10:10.203
and why THC and CBD have
the various effects they do.

01:10:12.930 --> 01:10:17.370
When somebody smokes or ingests cannabis,

01:10:17.370 --> 01:10:21.513
doesn't matter what the
THC or CBD ratio is,

01:10:22.440 --> 01:10:26.760
if they experience deficits in memory,

01:10:26.760 --> 01:10:30.540
and that's almost always
present, that's going to be

01:10:30.540 --> 01:10:32.910
because of reductions
in electrical activity

01:10:32.910 --> 01:10:35.430
within this brain region
we call the hippocampus.

01:10:35.430 --> 01:10:37.620
Hippocampus means seahorse.
It's shaped like a seahorse.

01:10:37.620 --> 01:10:40.440
Anatomists like to name things
after what things look like,

01:10:40.440 --> 01:10:43.470
but hippocampus memory, memory is reduced,

01:10:43.470 --> 01:10:45.030
in particular short-term memory.

01:10:45.030 --> 01:10:46.860
That's true regardless of whether or not

01:10:46.860 --> 01:10:51.003
one is using sativa indica or some hybrid.

01:10:52.246 --> 01:10:57.246
In general, the prefrontal
cortex is going to be activated

01:10:57.330 --> 01:11:00.090
by the sativa varieties, which
is going to increase thinking

01:11:00.090 --> 01:11:04.290
and narrowly constrain
focus to some activity,

01:11:04.290 --> 01:11:05.730
and that's more commonly associated

01:11:05.730 --> 01:11:07.080
with the sativa varieties.

01:11:07.080 --> 01:11:09.210
The indica varieties,
as I mentioned before,

01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:11.220
tend to lead to a suppression of activity

01:11:11.220 --> 01:11:13.470
in prefrontal cortex, believe it or not,

01:11:13.470 --> 01:11:15.660
and turn off thinking and planning.

01:11:15.660 --> 01:11:18.540
This is why indica
varieties are often used

01:11:18.540 --> 01:11:21.963
for relaxation and for promoting sleep.

01:11:23.610 --> 01:11:26.463
Regardless of whether or not
sativa or indica variety,

01:11:27.390 --> 01:11:30.780
and again, regardless of
the ratio of THC to CBD,

01:11:30.780 --> 01:11:33.780
there is a general
suppression of neural circuits

01:11:33.780 --> 01:11:36.000
within the so-called basal
ganglia and cerebellum.

01:11:36.000 --> 01:11:38.139
Basal ganglia and cerebellum
are areas of the brain

01:11:38.139 --> 01:11:41.190
that are involved in action
planning and withholding action,

01:11:41.190 --> 01:11:42.330
so that would be the basal ganglia,

01:11:42.330 --> 01:11:44.880
so-called go, no go circuitry,

01:11:44.880 --> 01:11:47.430
and the cerebellum, which
is involved in balance,

01:11:47.430 --> 01:11:50.073
but also motor planning
and motor sequencing.

01:11:51.240 --> 01:11:55.265
This is why people who smoke marijuana,

01:11:55.265 --> 01:11:57.090
regardless of the strain,

01:11:57.090 --> 01:12:00.060
will tend to be less physically mobile.

01:12:00.060 --> 01:12:02.550
Other common effects are
reddening of the eyes,

01:12:02.550 --> 01:12:03.480
dryness of the mouth,

01:12:03.480 --> 01:12:06.330
that's actually caused by
the same general mechanism,

01:12:06.330 --> 01:12:09.360
which is a reduction in
the secretion of saliva

01:12:09.360 --> 01:12:12.450
and of tears and lubrication of the eyes

01:12:12.450 --> 01:12:14.220
from the lacrimal glands of the eyes

01:12:14.220 --> 01:12:17.820
because of the presence of largely CB2,

01:12:17.820 --> 01:12:21.420
but also CB1 receptors in
the mouth and on the eyes.

01:12:21.420 --> 01:12:23.370
And there tends to be,

01:12:23.370 --> 01:12:26.070
especially with certain
strains of cannabis,

01:12:26.070 --> 01:12:28.860
increase in appetite, so-called munchies,

01:12:28.860 --> 01:12:33.450
and that has everything to do
with very, very high density

01:12:33.450 --> 01:12:35.640
of CB1 receptors in the hypothalamus,

01:12:35.640 --> 01:12:37.380
and in particular areas
of the hypothalamus,

01:12:37.380 --> 01:12:39.498
like the arcuate nucleus
of the hypothalamus,

01:12:39.498 --> 01:12:41.670
other areas as well, of course,

01:12:41.670 --> 01:12:46.440
that have tons of CB1
receptors, bind THC and CBD

01:12:46.440 --> 01:12:49.800
and activate the neurons that
strongly stimulate appetite

01:12:49.800 --> 01:12:53.610
through two mechanisms, one
is a cognitive mechanism

01:12:53.610 --> 01:12:55.860
of creating a preoccupation with food

01:12:55.860 --> 01:12:58.020
in anticipation of taste,

01:12:58.020 --> 01:12:59.910
as well as the experience of taste.

01:12:59.910 --> 01:13:04.017
So the narrowing of focus
to what you want to go eat.

01:13:04.017 --> 01:13:06.900
You really crave, I dunno, pizza.

01:13:06.900 --> 01:13:08.700
It seems to be high fat,
high carbohydrate foods,

01:13:08.700 --> 01:13:13.550
but really crave pizza
and narrowing of focus,

01:13:13.550 --> 01:13:15.210
so that you're not thinking
about anything else,

01:13:15.210 --> 01:13:18.557
but also signaling from
the hypothalamus to the gut

01:13:18.557 --> 01:13:21.360
to neurons within the stomach, itself,

01:13:21.360 --> 01:13:23.070
that regulate blood sugar,

01:13:23.070 --> 01:13:27.870
so there are strong effects
on blood sugar of THC and CBD

01:13:27.870 --> 01:13:30.000
that generally lead to
increases in appetite,

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:32.730
so two parallel mechanisms,
one within the brain,

01:13:32.730 --> 01:13:34.920
one within the body, increasing appetite,

01:13:34.920 --> 01:13:36.567
so there's an array of different effects.

01:13:36.567 --> 01:13:40.020
And as I mentioned before,
CB1 receptors are present

01:13:40.020 --> 01:13:42.240
all over the nervous system
in the brain, the spinal cord.

01:13:42.240 --> 01:13:44.970
In fact, the presence of CB1
receptors in the spinal cord

01:13:44.970 --> 01:13:49.110
largely explains the fact that
THC and CBD, to some extent,

01:13:49.110 --> 01:13:50.370
although it's not very well studied,

01:13:50.370 --> 01:13:52.470
can provide some pain relief.

01:13:52.470 --> 01:13:55.411
I say some, because a
lot of people perceive

01:13:55.411 --> 01:13:58.530
or believe that they
experience more pain relief

01:13:58.530 --> 01:14:00.720
from cannabis than they actually do.

01:14:00.720 --> 01:14:03.300
It actually has a lot to
do with a perceptual shift

01:14:03.300 --> 01:14:04.950
to basically focusing on other things,

01:14:04.950 --> 01:14:08.400
but there does seem to
be some antinociceptive,

01:14:08.400 --> 01:14:13.020
meaning anti pain effects of
cannabis, THC in particular,

01:14:13.020 --> 01:14:17.063
and that is exerted largely
through effects on CB1 receptors

01:14:17.063 --> 01:14:19.740
in neurons of the spinal cord.

01:14:19.740 --> 01:14:21.990
So a broad array of
effects are taking place,

01:14:21.990 --> 01:14:23.370
regardless of what strain you take

01:14:23.370 --> 01:14:25.530
and whether or not you eat the cannabis

01:14:25.530 --> 01:14:27.000
or you smoke the cannabis.

01:14:27.000 --> 01:14:30.030
And the broad array of effects
can be explained by the fact

01:14:30.030 --> 01:14:32.280
that that retrograde signaling
can lead to activation

01:14:32.280 --> 01:14:34.950
or suppression of activity
in various neurons.

01:14:34.950 --> 01:14:36.570
So now I'd like to take a step back

01:14:36.570 --> 01:14:39.090
from the biology of
cannabis and THC and CBD

01:14:39.090 --> 01:14:41.100
and all the signaling
and receptors, et cetera,

01:14:41.100 --> 01:14:44.160
and really just focus on cannabis use,

01:14:44.160 --> 01:14:45.330
and wherever possible,

01:14:45.330 --> 01:14:48.358
I will point to the specific
strains that have been studied

01:14:48.358 --> 01:14:51.240
and the ratios of THC to CBD,

01:14:51.240 --> 01:14:54.300
but I have to say that unfortunately,

01:14:54.300 --> 01:14:57.510
most studies of marijuana, of cannabis,

01:14:57.510 --> 01:14:59.850
while they have been very careful

01:14:59.850 --> 01:15:02.490
to detail the amount of THC,

01:15:02.490 --> 01:15:03.990
low dose, medium dose, or high dose,

01:15:03.990 --> 01:15:05.100
and actually getting very specific,

01:15:05.100 --> 01:15:06.390
right down to the number of milligrams

01:15:06.390 --> 01:15:08.340
or even how much
circulating THC is present

01:15:08.340 --> 01:15:13.340
after somebody smokes a
joint or ingests cannabis,

01:15:13.410 --> 01:15:15.990
most studies have not distinguished

01:15:15.990 --> 01:15:19.920
between sativa and indica strains.

01:15:19.920 --> 01:15:22.200
And that's unfortunate,
because in the real world,

01:15:22.200 --> 01:15:25.470
people are distinguishing
between sativa and indica strains

01:15:25.470 --> 01:15:27.300
in their patterns of
use and what they prefer

01:15:27.300 --> 01:15:28.133
and what they don't prefer,

01:15:28.133 --> 01:15:30.330
or even what they prefer
to smoke during the day

01:15:30.330 --> 01:15:31.680
or ingest during the day versus night.

01:15:31.680 --> 01:15:32.520
Believe it or not, there are people

01:15:32.520 --> 01:15:34.890
who are using certain
strains during the day

01:15:34.890 --> 01:15:36.240
and other strains at night,

01:15:37.320 --> 01:15:39.690
but the science is yet
to catch up to that,

01:15:39.690 --> 01:15:42.810
or I should say, more accurately,

01:15:42.810 --> 01:15:45.420
the general public and the
themes that are emerging

01:15:45.420 --> 01:15:48.000
and the practices that are
emerging around cannabis,

01:15:48.000 --> 01:15:51.270
especially in states where it's
decriminalized or legalized,

01:15:51.270 --> 01:15:52.650
are occurring at such a rapid rate

01:15:52.650 --> 01:15:53.820
that there's absolutely no way

01:15:53.820 --> 01:15:55.140
that the science could keep up.

01:15:55.140 --> 01:15:57.240
This is a naturally occurring experiment,

01:15:57.240 --> 01:15:59.130
not to say that it's natural,
like people should do it,

01:15:59.130 --> 01:16:01.200
but it's an experiment
that's happening in real time

01:16:01.200 --> 01:16:05.100
in the real world, much
faster than controlled studies

01:16:05.100 --> 01:16:06.390
within university laboratories

01:16:06.390 --> 01:16:07.980
and other laboratories can keep up.

01:16:07.980 --> 01:16:10.710
So at this point, I think it's
appropriate to ask ourselves,

01:16:10.710 --> 01:16:12.960
why do people even use cannabis?

01:16:12.960 --> 01:16:14.100
What are they trying to achieve?

01:16:14.100 --> 01:16:16.440
Is it always about not feeling pain?

01:16:16.440 --> 01:16:18.390
Is it always about reducing anxiety?

01:16:18.390 --> 01:16:19.320
Well, sometimes it is,

01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:21.540
but oftentimes people are using cannabis

01:16:21.540 --> 01:16:23.880
in order to achieve a particular state.

01:16:23.880 --> 01:16:25.897
And we could use a broad brush and say,

01:16:25.897 --> 01:16:27.960
"Well, they just like being high,"

01:16:27.960 --> 01:16:30.693
but while that may be true
in a lot of circumstances,

01:16:31.560 --> 01:16:33.330
and I have to believe it actually is true

01:16:33.330 --> 01:16:35.460
in a lot of circumstances,

01:16:35.460 --> 01:16:37.920
there are a lot of people who use cannabis

01:16:37.920 --> 01:16:41.190
in a very directed way, or
they get, quote unquote, high,

01:16:41.190 --> 01:16:43.050
in order to achieve states

01:16:43.050 --> 01:16:45.300
that to them are particularly attractive,

01:16:45.300 --> 01:16:49.047
and one such state is
a state of creativity.

01:16:49.047 --> 01:16:51.090
And this brings us to a broader theme,

01:16:51.090 --> 01:16:54.390
which is, does cannabis
increase creativity?

01:16:54.390 --> 01:16:56.760
And if so, is it the THC, the CBD,

01:16:56.760 --> 01:16:58.110
what's the appropriate ratio

01:16:58.110 --> 01:17:00.930
or the best ratio for
accessing creativity?

01:17:00.930 --> 01:17:02.880
This is an interesting and
important discussion, I believe,

01:17:02.880 --> 01:17:05.910
because creativity is one
of the more sought after

01:17:05.910 --> 01:17:08.850
and more elusive states
that humans can experience.

01:17:08.850 --> 01:17:11.220
And yet, if you look at human evolution,

01:17:11.220 --> 01:17:12.990
you look at our progression

01:17:12.990 --> 01:17:14.730
in terms of technology development

01:17:14.730 --> 01:17:18.759
and culture and music
and poetry, et cetera,

01:17:18.759 --> 01:17:21.720
we really can look to
creativity as the state

01:17:21.720 --> 01:17:24.600
that fostered so much of that evolution.

01:17:24.600 --> 01:17:26.490
So whether or not you're into technology,

01:17:26.490 --> 01:17:28.230
or you're into art or music,

01:17:28.230 --> 01:17:29.460
whether or not you're just somebody

01:17:29.460 --> 01:17:31.830
who wants to expand their understanding

01:17:31.830 --> 01:17:33.750
or their experience of life in some way,

01:17:33.750 --> 01:17:37.200
creativity is a
fundamentally important state

01:17:37.200 --> 01:17:39.480
to try and access and to
try and access regularly

01:17:39.480 --> 01:17:40.770
and to try and tap into,

01:17:40.770 --> 01:17:43.350
in order to eventually produce something,

01:17:43.350 --> 01:17:45.000
in order to create something of meaning

01:17:45.000 --> 01:17:47.070
that exists not just
in that creative state,

01:17:47.070 --> 01:17:51.000
but to yourself after that
creative state has gone away,

01:17:51.000 --> 01:17:52.920
so the painting that you
paint in the creative state

01:17:52.920 --> 01:17:54.650
hopefully is a painting
that still inspires

01:17:54.650 --> 01:17:57.968
and has impact after you
exit that creative state

01:17:57.968 --> 01:18:00.510
and that will inspire others,
and this could be true

01:18:00.510 --> 01:18:03.270
for any number of different
things, not just painting.

01:18:03.270 --> 01:18:06.120
So does cannabis increase creativity?

01:18:06.120 --> 01:18:08.460
The short answer is, it depends.

01:18:08.460 --> 01:18:10.680
First of all, we need
to define creativity.

01:18:10.680 --> 01:18:12.810
Here we are, thinking as scientists,

01:18:12.810 --> 01:18:14.610
if not already scientists,

01:18:14.610 --> 01:18:17.370
and there are basically
two modes of thinking

01:18:17.370 --> 01:18:18.750
that are associated with creativity,

01:18:18.750 --> 01:18:21.450
and they don't completely
explain creativity.

01:18:21.450 --> 01:18:22.770
But if you look in the research,

01:18:22.770 --> 01:18:25.350
the psychology research and
the neuroscience research,

01:18:25.350 --> 01:18:28.440
you'll hear about convergent thinking

01:18:28.440 --> 01:18:29.733
and divergent thinking.

01:18:30.900 --> 01:18:34.410
Convergent thinking is taking loose ideas

01:18:34.410 --> 01:18:36.720
and kind of braiding them together,

01:18:36.720 --> 01:18:39.840
finding a common thread, synthesizing,

01:18:39.840 --> 01:18:41.940
and organizing those different ideas

01:18:41.940 --> 01:18:45.240
into some common or specific framework

01:18:45.240 --> 01:18:49.170
in order to get or create
some specific outcome.

01:18:49.170 --> 01:18:53.370
So convergent thinking is
basically the person in the room

01:18:53.370 --> 01:18:56.910
who's listening to all the
ideas and taking them all in.

01:18:56.910 --> 01:19:00.953
Maybe it's a panel of, how should we

01:19:00.953 --> 01:19:03.030
get a certain product out to market?

01:19:03.030 --> 01:19:06.120
Or what are the different motifs

01:19:06.120 --> 01:19:07.860
that we should include
in a piece of music?

01:19:07.860 --> 01:19:10.260
Or what should we do

01:19:10.260 --> 01:19:14.130
in terms of rearchitecting
a given physical space?

01:19:14.130 --> 01:19:15.630
Taking in those different opinions,

01:19:15.630 --> 01:19:17.550
those different ideas
from different people,

01:19:17.550 --> 01:19:20.070
and then synthesizing them and coming up

01:19:20.070 --> 01:19:23.490
with one or a small
subset of coherent ideas

01:19:23.490 --> 01:19:26.340
that incorporate some or all
of the ones that they heard,

01:19:26.340 --> 01:19:27.390
so that's convergent thinking.

01:19:27.390 --> 01:19:30.554
It doesn't have to involve a
panel of people talking to you.

01:19:30.554 --> 01:19:32.790
I use that as an example

01:19:32.790 --> 01:19:34.530
of what goes on inside your own head

01:19:34.530 --> 01:19:36.240
when you are engaging
in convergent thinking.

01:19:36.240 --> 01:19:38.760
You're thinking, well,
so and so said this,

01:19:38.760 --> 01:19:40.710
and I think that, and
you're braiding them through

01:19:40.710 --> 01:19:43.290
and trying to get some common themes,

01:19:43.290 --> 01:19:45.990
some common vector to emerge from that.

01:19:45.990 --> 01:19:48.360
Divergent thinking, on the other hand,

01:19:48.360 --> 01:19:50.460
is best described as brainstorming.

01:19:50.460 --> 01:19:53.160
It's exploring ideas
and continuing to move

01:19:53.160 --> 01:19:56.880
into the variation and
the vastness of ideas

01:19:56.880 --> 01:19:59.040
in hopes of eventually
being able to converge

01:19:59.040 --> 01:20:01.306
on some novel idea or framework.

01:20:01.306 --> 01:20:03.120
So these are similar and related,

01:20:03.120 --> 01:20:05.010
but typically the creativity process

01:20:05.010 --> 01:20:09.000
involves first brainstorming
and divergent thinking,

01:20:09.000 --> 01:20:11.280
and then in order to arrive at something,

01:20:11.280 --> 01:20:13.950
to actually create
something, the verb 'create,'

01:20:13.950 --> 01:20:17.130
not just thinking about
what you might create,

01:20:17.130 --> 01:20:18.420
which occurs during divergent thinking,

01:20:18.420 --> 01:20:21.240
but actually creating
something, a specific painting,

01:20:21.240 --> 01:20:25.500
a specific song, a specific
body of literature,

01:20:25.500 --> 01:20:28.623
a specific scientific project
or experiment and so on,

01:20:29.760 --> 01:20:32.970
that usually involves convergent thinking.

01:20:32.970 --> 01:20:35.640
Now these can be explored
in the laboratory,

01:20:35.640 --> 01:20:37.290
and they can be explored
in the laboratories

01:20:37.290 --> 01:20:39.270
through sets of different
types of questionnaires

01:20:39.270 --> 01:20:41.220
or even tasks that you
can give human subjects,

01:20:41.220 --> 01:20:43.320
and this has been done extensively

01:20:43.320 --> 01:20:47.100
and across the entire body of data,

01:20:47.100 --> 01:20:49.770
and by that I mean,
literally hundreds of studies

01:20:49.770 --> 01:20:50.940
that have explored the relationship

01:20:50.940 --> 01:20:53.160
between particular neuro
circuits and neurochemicals,

01:20:53.160 --> 01:20:54.860
convergent and divergent thinking.

01:20:55.770 --> 01:20:57.600
We can arrive at a principle,

01:20:57.600 --> 01:21:01.170
and the principle involves a molecule

01:21:01.170 --> 01:21:03.289
that many of you have heard
about before, which is dopamine.

01:21:03.289 --> 01:21:04.530
Dopamine is a neuromodulator.

01:21:04.530 --> 01:21:06.210
It's involved in motivation,

01:21:06.210 --> 01:21:07.500
and it tends to direct our attention

01:21:07.500 --> 01:21:10.980
to things outside of us, but
it's also closely related

01:21:10.980 --> 01:21:13.380
to convergent thinking
and divergent thinking

01:21:13.380 --> 01:21:15.120
and to the creative process.

01:21:15.120 --> 01:21:17.273
And therefore, it should
come as no surprise

01:21:17.273 --> 01:21:21.060
that diseases of the
nervous system, excuse me,

01:21:21.060 --> 01:21:23.130
such as bipolar disorder,

01:21:23.130 --> 01:21:25.830
which we've done in episode
all about bipolar disorder

01:21:25.830 --> 01:21:29.760
or schizophrenia or mood disorders

01:21:29.760 --> 01:21:31.800
that impact the levels of dopamine,

01:21:31.800 --> 01:21:35.880
either make it way, way too
high or way, way too low,

01:21:35.880 --> 01:21:38.550
strongly impact whether or
not people will be creative.

01:21:38.550 --> 01:21:41.250
And I think the short takeaway
that makes the most sense

01:21:41.250 --> 01:21:42.630
in terms of framing this,

01:21:42.630 --> 01:21:45.390
and we covered this on the
episode on bipolar disorder,

01:21:45.390 --> 01:21:48.780
sometimes called bipolar
depression, is that in professions

01:21:48.780 --> 01:21:50.880
where there's a lot of creativity required

01:21:50.880 --> 01:21:53.580
in order to succeed, so
again, musicians, composers,

01:21:53.580 --> 01:21:57.930
artists, et cetera, you tend
to find more manic depression,

01:21:57.930 --> 01:21:59.490
and manic depression,

01:21:59.490 --> 01:22:02.640
at least in the manic states,
the hyperactive states,

01:22:02.640 --> 01:22:05.523
are correlated with
elevated levels of dopamine.

01:22:06.510 --> 01:22:10.315
Likewise, it has been seen over
and over throughout history

01:22:10.315 --> 01:22:13.530
that individuals that have
mild forms of schizophrenia,

01:22:13.530 --> 01:22:15.690
or even full-blown schizophrenia,

01:22:15.690 --> 01:22:18.960
many famous painters for
instance, or musicians,

01:22:18.960 --> 01:22:21.630
they are known to have
elevated levels of dopamine,

01:22:21.630 --> 01:22:22.770
and they are quite creative.

01:22:22.770 --> 01:22:24.540
Now, that doesn't mean
everybody who's creative

01:22:24.540 --> 01:22:26.310
has elevated levels of dopamine,

01:22:26.310 --> 01:22:28.530
although it's likely that
their levels of dopamine

01:22:28.530 --> 01:22:29.670
are at least not diminished,

01:22:29.670 --> 01:22:31.470
and it doesn't mean
that non-creative people

01:22:31.470 --> 01:22:32.580
have low levels of dopamine,

01:22:32.580 --> 01:22:34.830
so don't get carried away
with the interpretation here.

01:22:34.830 --> 01:22:36.494
But the point is this,

01:22:36.494 --> 01:22:41.130
dopamine levels strongly
relate to the probability

01:22:41.130 --> 01:22:44.010
that you can engage in convergent
and divergent thinking,

01:22:44.010 --> 01:22:45.870
and they do so in the following way.

01:22:45.870 --> 01:22:49.050
When dopamine levels are high,

01:22:49.050 --> 01:22:51.180
divergent thinking is more likely.

01:22:51.180 --> 01:22:53.310
That is, when people
have a lot of dopamine

01:22:53.310 --> 01:22:54.930
circulating in their system,

01:22:54.930 --> 01:22:57.510
they tend to be very
expansive with their ideas,

01:22:57.510 --> 01:22:58.710
they tend to brainstorm a lot,

01:22:58.710 --> 01:23:02.550
they tend to be comfortable
and even want to,

01:23:02.550 --> 01:23:05.060
or reflexively throw out a lot of ideas

01:23:05.060 --> 01:23:07.890
so that sometimes even
seem a little disconnected.

01:23:07.890 --> 01:23:09.180
Some people might think of this

01:23:09.180 --> 01:23:11.550
as attention deficit, but it's not.

01:23:11.550 --> 01:23:15.213
It's really the idea of
throwing out disparate ideas.

01:23:16.560 --> 01:23:18.570
You hear sometimes, you
throw things against the wall

01:23:18.570 --> 01:23:21.990
and see what sticks, that's
a obviously an analogy,

01:23:21.990 --> 01:23:25.170
but people are throwing lots
of things against the wall

01:23:25.170 --> 01:23:26.280
and seeing what stick,

01:23:26.280 --> 01:23:28.530
and then seeing how the things
that stick fit together,

01:23:28.530 --> 01:23:31.650
that's divergent thinking,
and elevations in dopamine

01:23:31.650 --> 01:23:34.080
tend to increase divergent thinking.

01:23:34.080 --> 01:23:38.700
However, they tend to do this
in an inverted U-shape way.

01:23:38.700 --> 01:23:39.900
For those of you either
watching on YouTube,

01:23:39.900 --> 01:23:42.360
I'm just drawing a hump, obviously,

01:23:42.360 --> 01:23:43.650
and for those of you listening,

01:23:43.650 --> 01:23:46.920
just imagine a U, the shape of a letter U,

01:23:46.920 --> 01:23:49.388
and then just flip it upside
down, so it looks like a bump.

01:23:49.388 --> 01:23:51.690
Turns out that when dopamine
levels are very low,

01:23:51.690 --> 01:23:53.850
there's a low probability
of divergent thinking.

01:23:53.850 --> 01:23:56.130
When dopamine levels are
high, as I mentioned before,

01:23:56.130 --> 01:23:59.190
there's a high level or
probability of divergent thinking.

01:23:59.190 --> 01:24:02.520
But when dopamine levels
go very, very high,

01:24:02.520 --> 01:24:05.520
then there's again, a reduction
in divergent thinking.

01:24:05.520 --> 01:24:07.890
In other words, there's a sweet spot

01:24:07.890 --> 01:24:10.350
of elevated dopamine
for divergent thinking.

01:24:10.350 --> 01:24:12.090
And again, divergent thinking

01:24:12.090 --> 01:24:14.550
is critical for the creativity process,

01:24:14.550 --> 01:24:16.650
because creativity by definition

01:24:16.650 --> 01:24:19.230
is taking a novel set of ideas

01:24:19.230 --> 01:24:21.240
and arranging them in a particular way,

01:24:21.240 --> 01:24:25.200
or taking existing ideas and
arranging them in a novel way

01:24:25.200 --> 01:24:26.820
that then you eventually converge

01:24:26.820 --> 01:24:29.733
on some new product, new
idea, new song, et cetera.

01:24:30.780 --> 01:24:34.572
Now, convergent thinking follows
a very different pattern.

01:24:34.572 --> 01:24:36.540
When dopamine levels are high,

01:24:36.540 --> 01:24:39.600
convergent thinking is not very likely.

01:24:39.600 --> 01:24:41.370
And when dopamine levels are low,

01:24:41.370 --> 01:24:43.110
convergent thinking is very likely.

01:24:43.110 --> 01:24:47.070
So here, using arguably
a very reductionist view,

01:24:47.070 --> 01:24:49.410
we're looking at all of this
thing we're calling creativity

01:24:49.410 --> 01:24:51.720
through a very neurosciencey
reductionist lens,

01:24:51.720 --> 01:24:52.623
we can say this.

01:24:53.550 --> 01:24:55.530
The creative process
involves going into a state

01:24:55.530 --> 01:24:58.200
where you're willing to
consider a lot of options,

01:24:58.200 --> 01:24:59.970
many of which seem distantly

01:24:59.970 --> 01:25:01.680
or not even connected to one another,

01:25:01.680 --> 01:25:05.010
and dopamine facilitates
that divergent thinking state

01:25:05.010 --> 01:25:06.480
in which you are perfectly happy

01:25:06.480 --> 01:25:11.480
and in fact, experience a
joy, or elation, a comfort

01:25:12.210 --> 01:25:15.450
and a pleasure in organizing
all these different ideas

01:25:15.450 --> 01:25:19.170
that to anyone else might
seem not that related.

01:25:19.170 --> 01:25:20.937
But when your dopamine
levels are elevated,

01:25:20.937 --> 01:25:22.257
these all seem like great ideas

01:25:22.257 --> 01:25:24.390
and that maybe there
are connections there.

01:25:24.390 --> 01:25:26.460
You're not accepting all
of them as true and valid

01:25:26.460 --> 01:25:29.670
and interesting in combining
them, but there's this idea

01:25:29.670 --> 01:25:32.700
that it's worth entertaining
the possibility,

01:25:32.700 --> 01:25:34.440
at least for moments.

01:25:34.440 --> 01:25:38.070
And then, as dopamine levels drop,

01:25:38.070 --> 01:25:39.840
there is the process
of convergent thinking,

01:25:39.840 --> 01:25:43.087
which is taking options
down off the wall saying,

01:25:43.087 --> 01:25:44.830
"No, no, that doesn't fit with that,

01:25:44.830 --> 01:25:46.027
"that doesn't fit with that,

01:25:46.027 --> 01:25:48.337
"but ah, that fits with
that, and that can work.

01:25:48.337 --> 01:25:52.260
"That feels right or sounds
right or looks right."

01:25:52.260 --> 01:25:54.027
That's the creativity process.

01:25:54.027 --> 01:25:56.880
And so, I think this is not just important

01:25:56.880 --> 01:25:58.320
for understanding cannabis,

01:25:58.320 --> 01:25:59.730
which we'll get back to in a moment,

01:25:59.730 --> 01:26:02.100
but it's important for
understanding creativity

01:26:02.100 --> 01:26:03.870
and brain states in general.

01:26:03.870 --> 01:26:05.730
Brain states are not, as we would say,

01:26:05.730 --> 01:26:06.630
a square wave function.

01:26:06.630 --> 01:26:08.700
You don't just drop into
a trench of creativity.

01:26:08.700 --> 01:26:11.392
Creativity is not an
event, it's a process.

01:26:11.392 --> 01:26:13.743
And what I'm telling you
is that it's a process

01:26:13.743 --> 01:26:15.420
that involves divergent thinking

01:26:15.420 --> 01:26:17.130
and consideration of a
lot of different ideas.

01:26:17.130 --> 01:26:20.940
That's correlated with high
but not too high dopamine.

01:26:20.940 --> 01:26:24.342
And then, one has to
transition into a state

01:26:24.342 --> 01:26:27.420
of convergent thinking,
which is really honing in

01:26:27.420 --> 01:26:29.790
on the ideas that seem to have validity

01:26:29.790 --> 01:26:31.080
or that could have validity

01:26:31.080 --> 01:26:32.400
and getting rid of everything else,

01:26:32.400 --> 01:26:34.500
and that's associated with low dopamine.

01:26:34.500 --> 01:26:37.710
It's more about logical
implementation and consideration

01:26:37.710 --> 01:26:40.920
as opposed to thinking about
and considering everything.

01:26:40.920 --> 01:26:42.390
So let's now return to the question

01:26:42.390 --> 01:26:43.890
of whether or not cannabis

01:26:43.890 --> 01:26:46.983
and its different components
increase creativity.

01:26:48.210 --> 01:26:50.250
And when you look at
the literature on this,

01:26:50.250 --> 01:26:53.880
you find studies that very
clearly point to a yes,

01:26:53.880 --> 01:26:56.760
it increases creativity,
and it's not surprising,

01:26:56.760 --> 01:27:00.990
therefore, that cannabis can
increase dopamine transmission,

01:27:00.990 --> 01:27:03.390
that is dopamine levels,
in certain brain areas,

01:27:03.390 --> 01:27:07.050
in particular brain areas
involved in thinking and planning.

01:27:07.050 --> 01:27:09.450
So cannabis increases
dopamine in these areas.

01:27:09.450 --> 01:27:12.000
Elevated dopamine increases
divergent thinking,

01:27:12.000 --> 01:27:14.340
and divergent thinking is
associated with creativity,

01:27:14.340 --> 01:27:16.380
and there are studies
that support the idea

01:27:16.380 --> 01:27:20.280
that cannabis can increase creativity.

01:27:20.280 --> 01:27:23.730
However, there are at
least as many studies

01:27:23.730 --> 01:27:26.760
that say that cannabis does
not increase creativity,

01:27:26.760 --> 01:27:30.120
that cannabis increases
consideration of multiple ideas,

01:27:30.120 --> 01:27:33.870
perhaps through elevation of
dopamine and related systems,

01:27:33.870 --> 01:27:36.900
but that ultimately the
ideas that converge from that

01:27:36.900 --> 01:27:38.370
are not truly creative ideas,

01:27:38.370 --> 01:27:39.720
at least they don't meet the criteria

01:27:39.720 --> 01:27:41.340
for creative brainstorming

01:27:41.340 --> 01:27:44.130
and extraction of ideas
that are truly novel,

01:27:44.130 --> 01:27:48.060
so it doesn't increase
creativity, so which one is it?

01:27:48.060 --> 01:27:48.893
Well, fortunately,

01:27:48.893 --> 01:27:51.510
there's an entirely
distinct set of literature

01:27:51.510 --> 01:27:54.750
that has taken all the other
literature into consideration,

01:27:54.750 --> 01:27:56.340
and here's where we arrive.

01:27:56.340 --> 01:27:57.600
So there's a really nice study

01:27:57.600 --> 01:28:00.600
that explored creativity
in cannabis users,

01:28:00.600 --> 01:28:02.337
and we will provide a link to this study.

01:28:02.337 --> 01:28:04.560
First author is Emily LaFrance,

01:28:04.560 --> 01:28:06.930
and the title of the paper,

01:28:06.930 --> 01:28:08.760
somewhat amusing in its own right,

01:28:08.760 --> 01:28:10.800
which is, it starts with a question,

01:28:10.800 --> 01:28:12.600
inspired by Mary Jane, of course,

01:28:12.600 --> 01:28:14.976
Mary Jane being one of
the old school versions

01:28:14.976 --> 01:28:18.420
or ways of talking about
cannabis or marijuana.

01:28:18.420 --> 01:28:20.640
Nowadays, people refer
to it mainly as pot,

01:28:20.640 --> 01:28:21.780
as other names too, of course,

01:28:21.780 --> 01:28:23.557
and the title of the paper is,

01:28:23.557 --> 01:28:26.137
"Inspired by Mary Jane
Mechanisms Underlying

01:28:26.137 --> 01:28:28.200
"Enhanced Creativity in Cannabis Users."

01:28:28.200 --> 01:28:30.870
And I really like this study
for a couple of reasons.

01:28:30.870 --> 01:28:35.870
First of all, they looked at
people who did not use cannabis

01:28:36.270 --> 01:28:38.700
as well as people who used cannabis,

01:28:38.700 --> 01:28:39.930
so they had two different groups,

01:28:39.930 --> 01:28:44.790
but they did not evaluate
creativity of the cannabis users

01:28:44.790 --> 01:28:47.250
while they were under the
influence of cannabis.

01:28:47.250 --> 01:28:50.047
They looked at the level of creativity

01:28:50.047 --> 01:28:52.590
in these cannabis users

01:28:52.590 --> 01:28:55.410
when they were not under
the influence of cannabis

01:28:55.410 --> 01:28:58.320
and asked whether or not
their ability to be creative

01:28:58.320 --> 01:28:59.700
was enhanced by cannabis.

01:28:59.700 --> 01:29:01.860
Now we're going to compare this to studies

01:29:01.860 --> 01:29:03.930
in which people come into the laboratory

01:29:03.930 --> 01:29:04.830
and actually use cannabis,

01:29:04.830 --> 01:29:08.246
and then they evaluate
creativity under that context.

01:29:08.246 --> 01:29:10.680
But this study has some unique takeaways

01:29:10.680 --> 01:29:11.910
that I think are really interesting.

01:29:11.910 --> 01:29:14.430
First of all, they did, yes,

01:29:14.430 --> 01:29:16.920
see evidence for enhanced creativity,

01:29:16.920 --> 01:29:18.390
and when I say enhanced creativity,

01:29:18.390 --> 01:29:21.397
I mean within the context of
this divergent thinking thing

01:29:21.397 --> 01:29:23.253
that I talked about a moment ago.

01:29:24.330 --> 01:29:25.620
And when I say enhanced,

01:29:25.620 --> 01:29:28.050
I mean significantly
greater than in non-users,

01:29:28.050 --> 01:29:29.970
so people that don't use cannabis.

01:29:29.970 --> 01:29:32.062
So right now I can imagine
that all the cannabis users

01:29:32.062 --> 01:29:35.137
are cheering, "Yes, cannabis
increases creativity,

01:29:35.137 --> 01:29:37.800
"makes people more creative
than they would be otherwise."

01:29:37.800 --> 01:29:39.450
Well, this is interesting.

01:29:39.450 --> 01:29:42.960
We have to ask ourselves
how that was accomplished.

01:29:42.960 --> 01:29:44.670
And it turns out that
one of the major ways

01:29:44.670 --> 01:29:47.640
in which it was accomplished
is that cannabis users,

01:29:47.640 --> 01:29:51.060
even if they're not under
the influence of cannabis,

01:29:51.060 --> 01:29:54.150
are far more open to novel ideas,

01:29:54.150 --> 01:29:58.980
and they have a more
explorative and reduced anxiety,

01:29:58.980 --> 01:30:01.470
or I should say lower
anxiety mode of thinking

01:30:01.470 --> 01:30:02.880
when they explore novel ideas,

01:30:02.880 --> 01:30:04.860
which is essential for divergent thinking.

01:30:04.860 --> 01:30:07.440
So they observed both enhanced divergent

01:30:07.440 --> 01:30:12.440
and convergent creative type
thinking in cannabis users.

01:30:12.960 --> 01:30:16.830
And the source of that, they conclude is,

01:30:16.830 --> 01:30:19.957
and here, I'll just quote,
"Cannabis users' higher levels

01:30:19.957 --> 01:30:21.937
"of openness to experience are responsible

01:30:21.937 --> 01:30:23.677
"for their enhanced
self-reported creativity

01:30:23.677 --> 01:30:26.070
"and convergent thinking
test performance."

01:30:26.070 --> 01:30:29.130
So it's not necessarily that cannabis

01:30:29.130 --> 01:30:31.410
is increasing the capacity
of the brain areas

01:30:31.410 --> 01:30:33.960
that are associated with creativity,

01:30:33.960 --> 01:30:38.960
but rather, cannabis appears
to be increasing an openness

01:30:39.030 --> 01:30:42.510
and probably doing that in
part through lowering anxiety

01:30:42.510 --> 01:30:45.330
in particular people, and that openness

01:30:45.330 --> 01:30:48.630
is leading to inclusion of more ideas

01:30:48.630 --> 01:30:51.210
during the divergent thinking process.

01:30:51.210 --> 01:30:52.860
They're willing to consider throwing up

01:30:52.860 --> 01:30:55.950
more things on the wall to see
if they stick, so to speak.

01:30:55.950 --> 01:30:58.380
So in their conclusions, they
have a really nice statement.

01:30:58.380 --> 01:31:00.210
Again, I'll just read from the paper,

01:31:00.210 --> 01:31:02.587
because they said it better
than I ever could, quote,

01:31:02.587 --> 01:31:04.957
"While mainstream media
has propagated the idea

01:31:04.957 --> 01:31:08.377
"that cannabis expands the
mind and enhances creativity,

01:31:08.377 --> 01:31:10.927
"our results show the link
between cannabis and creativity

01:31:10.927 --> 01:31:13.680
"is largely a spurious correlation,"

01:31:13.680 --> 01:31:16.080
meaning that it's not the case

01:31:16.080 --> 01:31:20.997
that cannabis increases creativity, but,

01:31:20.997 --> 01:31:23.703
and I inserted the 'but' in this quote,

01:31:25.039 --> 01:31:28.507
but "driven by differences in personality

01:31:28.507 --> 01:31:30.547
"that are related to cannabis use.

01:31:30.547 --> 01:31:33.037
"For example, openness to
experience that are related

01:31:33.037 --> 01:31:36.265
"to both cannabis use and
augmented creativity."

01:31:36.265 --> 01:31:39.603
This is a real chicken/egg
argument. What do I mean by that?

01:31:39.603 --> 01:31:42.600
What I mean is, this paper finds

01:31:42.600 --> 01:31:46.080
that people who are
more open to experience

01:31:46.080 --> 01:31:48.330
are more likely to use cannabis,

01:31:48.330 --> 01:31:49.950
and people who use cannabis

01:31:49.950 --> 01:31:53.460
are going to be more
open to new experiences,

01:31:53.460 --> 01:31:56.880
and that combination of
features, openness to experience,

01:31:56.880 --> 01:32:00.480
and what that openness to
new experiences brings,

01:32:00.480 --> 01:32:03.720
enhances the convergent
and divergent thinking

01:32:03.720 --> 01:32:06.780
that is characteristic
of the creative process.

01:32:06.780 --> 01:32:10.080
So in short, cannabis
increases creativity,

01:32:10.080 --> 01:32:12.450
but through changes in personality

01:32:12.450 --> 01:32:14.760
that tap into the creative process,

01:32:14.760 --> 01:32:18.220
rather than directly
impacting the neural circuits

01:32:18.220 --> 01:32:21.120
that, for instance, turn on creativity.

01:32:21.120 --> 01:32:24.270
And I have to say, this
study is really important,

01:32:24.270 --> 01:32:28.410
because by exploring cannabis users,

01:32:28.410 --> 01:32:30.900
not while under the influence of cannabis,

01:32:30.900 --> 01:32:33.990
they were able to tap
into this very important,

01:32:33.990 --> 01:32:36.090
what I believe to be fact.

01:32:36.090 --> 01:32:37.560
Because if you think about a study

01:32:37.560 --> 01:32:40.470
in which you would have
one group using cannabis

01:32:40.470 --> 01:32:42.120
and another group not using cannabis,

01:32:42.120 --> 01:32:44.910
and then you give them some
task that taps into creativity,

01:32:44.910 --> 01:32:46.770
you will see effects and very likely,

01:32:46.770 --> 01:32:48.900
you'll see effects where
cannabis might even increase

01:32:48.900 --> 01:32:51.090
divergent and convergent
thinking and creativity.

01:32:51.090 --> 01:32:52.350
Those results have actually been published

01:32:52.350 --> 01:32:53.850
many times before.

01:32:53.850 --> 01:32:56.213
But given the varied
effects of cannabis and THC

01:32:56.213 --> 01:32:57.570
that we talked about earlier,

01:32:57.570 --> 01:32:59.640
through all of that
complex signaling stuff,

01:32:59.640 --> 01:33:01.950
you can imagine that there
will also be other studies,

01:33:01.950 --> 01:33:03.300
and in fact, there are,

01:33:03.300 --> 01:33:06.750
where divergent and convergent
thinking and creativity

01:33:06.750 --> 01:33:09.240
is not assisted by cannabis

01:33:09.240 --> 01:33:12.000
and might even be reduced
by ingesting cannabis.

01:33:12.000 --> 01:33:14.820
However, if one considers
that divergent thinking

01:33:14.820 --> 01:33:18.690
is absolutely crucial to
the creativity process

01:33:18.690 --> 01:33:21.690
and the range of things
that one will explore

01:33:21.690 --> 01:33:24.330
will be enhanced by openness

01:33:24.330 --> 01:33:26.850
and by reduced levels of anxiety,

01:33:26.850 --> 01:33:28.800
so a willingness to
explore different options,

01:33:28.800 --> 01:33:31.230
some of which might seem completely crazy,

01:33:31.230 --> 01:33:34.290
and cannabis increases
the personality types

01:33:34.290 --> 01:33:38.100
and reduces the anxiety that
create that sense of openness,

01:33:38.100 --> 01:33:39.048
well then it makes perfect sense

01:33:39.048 --> 01:33:42.111
why cannabis would increase creativity

01:33:42.111 --> 01:33:45.330
in certain individuals, but not directly.

01:33:45.330 --> 01:33:47.970
And this study, the
one I just referred to,

01:33:47.970 --> 01:33:49.230
which I should say was published

01:33:49.230 --> 01:33:50.940
in the journal,
"Consciousness and Cognition,"

01:33:50.940 --> 01:33:53.070
and again, we'll provide a link to it,

01:33:53.070 --> 01:33:55.950
did a wonderful job of teasing out

01:33:55.950 --> 01:33:58.920
this impact of cannabis on personality,

01:33:58.920 --> 01:34:01.320
which then impacts creativity.

01:34:01.320 --> 01:34:03.480
So if somebody asks you,
or if you're wondering,

01:34:03.480 --> 01:34:06.600
or if you feel like cannabis
increases creativity,

01:34:06.600 --> 01:34:08.130
in some sense the answer is 'yes,'

01:34:08.130 --> 01:34:10.290
but the answer is 'yes'
because of the ways

01:34:10.290 --> 01:34:13.140
that it shapes openness to new ideas

01:34:13.140 --> 01:34:16.020
and can, I should say 'can,'
because not in everybody,

01:34:16.020 --> 01:34:18.848
but can, in some
individuals, reduce anxiety.

01:34:18.848 --> 01:34:21.990
What this means is that
if you are somebody

01:34:21.990 --> 01:34:26.990
who experiences anxiety or
increased levels of focus

01:34:27.253 --> 01:34:30.998
from cannabis, regardless of the strain,

01:34:30.998 --> 01:34:32.010
and here I have to imagine people

01:34:32.010 --> 01:34:33.360
are exploring different strains,

01:34:33.360 --> 01:34:35.520
if they're exploring them at all,

01:34:35.520 --> 01:34:36.900
exploring different modes of delivery,

01:34:36.900 --> 01:34:38.730
smoking or ingestible, et cetera.

01:34:38.730 --> 01:34:41.670
If you're somebody who
experiences anxiety,

01:34:41.670 --> 01:34:46.180
it's very likely that you won't
have the increased openness

01:34:47.250 --> 01:34:49.470
to experience and divergent ideas

01:34:49.470 --> 01:34:50.880
that will facilitate creativity.

01:34:50.880 --> 01:34:53.430
However, if you are somebody
who achieves heightened levels

01:34:53.430 --> 01:34:57.630
of relaxation and reduced
levels of anxiety from cannabis,

01:34:57.630 --> 01:35:00.450
regardless of which strain we
happen to be talking about,

01:35:00.450 --> 01:35:03.090
well then, yes, it will position you

01:35:03.090 --> 01:35:05.790
to be in a heightened state of creativity,

01:35:05.790 --> 01:35:08.550
at least as defined by convergent
and divergent thinking.

01:35:08.550 --> 01:35:10.200
One of the more characteristic,

01:35:10.200 --> 01:35:13.920
or I should say stereotype
qualities of people

01:35:13.920 --> 01:35:16.650
that smoke a lot of
marijuana or ingest cannabis

01:35:16.650 --> 01:35:20.340
through other means is there
changed patterns of speech.

01:35:20.340 --> 01:35:25.340
In fact, there's a lore
in the clinical realm

01:35:25.530 --> 01:35:29.430
that you can predict or
get some strong indication

01:35:29.430 --> 01:35:31.740
as to whether or not
somebody is a cannabis user

01:35:31.740 --> 01:35:34.563
or pot smoker based on their voice

01:35:34.563 --> 01:35:37.230
and their particular tone of voice

01:35:37.230 --> 01:35:39.270
and their lack of inflection.

01:35:39.270 --> 01:35:40.710
There's a lot of speculation here,

01:35:40.710 --> 01:35:42.390
but fortunately, it's been studied.

01:35:42.390 --> 01:35:43.680
So I'd like to discuss now

01:35:43.680 --> 01:35:46.770
whether or not cannabis can
impact patterns of speech,

01:35:46.770 --> 01:35:50.100
both acutely, meaning while
under the influence of cannabis,

01:35:50.100 --> 01:35:53.670
and over time, in chronic cannabis users.

01:35:53.670 --> 01:35:55.200
And when I say 'chronic cannabis use,'

01:35:55.200 --> 01:35:56.820
I want to be very specific what I mean.

01:35:56.820 --> 01:35:59.040
Chronic cannabis use
does not necessarily mean

01:35:59.040 --> 01:36:00.270
that people are smoking cannabis

01:36:00.270 --> 01:36:02.100
or ingesting cannabis every day,

01:36:02.100 --> 01:36:03.060
although certainly if they are,

01:36:03.060 --> 01:36:05.370
that qualifies as chronic use.

01:36:05.370 --> 01:36:07.800
Chronic use is regular use over time

01:36:07.800 --> 01:36:10.230
of anywhere from twice a week or more.

01:36:10.230 --> 01:36:11.850
So using cannabis once a month

01:36:11.850 --> 01:36:13.350
would not be considered chronic use,

01:36:13.350 --> 01:36:15.570
even if it's for many, many years.

01:36:15.570 --> 01:36:17.190
Using cannabis or ingesting cannabis

01:36:17.190 --> 01:36:19.290
in some way or form twice a week

01:36:19.290 --> 01:36:21.450
would be considered chronic use.

01:36:21.450 --> 01:36:22.830
Then of course, some of you out there

01:36:22.830 --> 01:36:24.247
are going to ask me to
split hairs and say,

01:36:24.247 --> 01:36:26.610
"Well, what if somebody
uses it twice a month?"

01:36:26.610 --> 01:36:28.890
Well, listen, the clinical literature,

01:36:28.890 --> 01:36:31.500
and the scientific literature
don't get that specific.

01:36:31.500 --> 01:36:33.360
And of course, they're an
infinite number of ways

01:36:33.360 --> 01:36:35.610
to arrange one's cannabis use,

01:36:35.610 --> 01:36:37.470
everything from zero, none at all,

01:36:37.470 --> 01:36:41.220
to constantly, every day, all
day and everywhere in between.

01:36:41.220 --> 01:36:45.000
But think of chronic use
as twice a week or more.

01:36:45.000 --> 01:36:47.670
Think of occasional use as less than that

01:36:47.670 --> 01:36:51.390
and realize that within the
realm of chronic use, excuse me,

01:36:51.390 --> 01:36:54.390
that 'or more' can be
anywhere from twice a week,

01:36:54.390 --> 01:36:57.123
to every day, to just in
the evenings, et cetera.

01:36:58.920 --> 01:37:01.503
The effects of chronic use of cannabis,

01:37:02.640 --> 01:37:06.903
as I just defined it, on
speech have been studied.

01:37:07.950 --> 01:37:10.140
Because of this characteristic,

01:37:10.140 --> 01:37:14.700
drawing out of certain
syllables, a slowing of speech,

01:37:14.700 --> 01:37:19.170
and in many cases a total
change or alteration

01:37:19.170 --> 01:37:22.613
in the way that people
speak and use language,

01:37:22.613 --> 01:37:25.650
both when under the influence of cannabis

01:37:25.650 --> 01:37:28.080
and when not under the
influence of cannabis

01:37:28.080 --> 01:37:29.880
if they're chronic users.

01:37:29.880 --> 01:37:33.930
And here we really want to
distinguish between THC and CBD

01:37:33.930 --> 01:37:35.670
and just make it really simple and say

01:37:35.670 --> 01:37:39.180
that CBD is not responsible

01:37:39.180 --> 01:37:41.970
for most of the psychoactive
effects of cannabis,

01:37:41.970 --> 01:37:44.130
whereas THC is, and again,

01:37:44.130 --> 01:37:47.220
the ratio of CBD to THC is
going to be relevant there,

01:37:47.220 --> 01:37:50.010
but let's just think
about cannabis and THC

01:37:50.010 --> 01:37:52.890
as one in the same for this
portion of the discussion,

01:37:52.890 --> 01:37:53.723
realizing that, of course,

01:37:53.723 --> 01:37:55.680
they are not exactly the same thing.

01:37:55.680 --> 01:37:57.247
There's an excellent study entitled,

01:37:57.247 --> 01:37:59.647
"Adults with History of
Recreational Cannabis Use

01:37:59.647 --> 01:38:01.860
"Have Altered Speech Production,"

01:38:01.860 --> 01:38:02.880
and we will provide a link to that,

01:38:02.880 --> 01:38:06.060
first author, Adam Vogel,
really like this paper.

01:38:06.060 --> 01:38:07.627
It was published in the journal,

01:38:07.627 --> 01:38:09.205
"Drug and Alcohol Dependence."

01:38:09.205 --> 01:38:11.880
We will provide a link to it for you

01:38:11.880 --> 01:38:14.370
if you'd like to peruse it in more detail.

01:38:14.370 --> 01:38:17.197
The title itself, "Adults
with a History of Recreational

01:38:17.197 --> 01:38:20.070
"Cannabis Use Have Altered
Speech Production,"

01:38:20.070 --> 01:38:22.290
tells you pretty much
everything you need to know,

01:38:22.290 --> 01:38:24.750
except there's some
important nuance in here,

01:38:24.750 --> 01:38:26.850
because, as I mentioned earlier,

01:38:26.850 --> 01:38:30.180
people who smoke sativa
varieties of cannabis

01:38:30.180 --> 01:38:34.440
oftentimes will become more
talkative, much more talkative.

01:38:34.440 --> 01:38:37.350
However, whether or
not people tend to rely

01:38:37.350 --> 01:38:42.350
on sativa cannabis use
or indica cannabis use,

01:38:42.439 --> 01:38:44.970
there is a very consistent finding

01:38:44.970 --> 01:38:46.320
that people who are chronic users,

01:38:46.320 --> 01:38:48.060
again twice a week or more,

01:38:48.060 --> 01:38:50.760
recreational use or medicinal use,

01:38:50.760 --> 01:38:54.360
undergo pretty profound changes
in the way that they speak,

01:38:54.360 --> 01:38:57.930
but in a very specific set of ways.

01:38:57.930 --> 01:39:00.660
Now, first of all, the changes in speech

01:39:00.660 --> 01:39:02.640
shouldn't surprise us at all,

01:39:02.640 --> 01:39:05.760
because both sativa varieties of cannabis

01:39:05.760 --> 01:39:08.130
and indica varieties of cannabis

01:39:08.130 --> 01:39:10.860
impact those brain centers
involved in movement,

01:39:10.860 --> 01:39:13.680
the basal ganglia, remember
the go, no-go circuitry,

01:39:13.680 --> 01:39:15.510
the circuitry that makes
you want to do things,

01:39:15.510 --> 01:39:18.150
and the circuitry that makes
you want to withhold action?

01:39:18.150 --> 01:39:21.090
And it tends to shift the body and brain

01:39:21.090 --> 01:39:23.294
toward more inaction.

01:39:23.294 --> 01:39:27.210
And cannabis impacts CB1
receptors in the cerebellum,

01:39:27.210 --> 01:39:31.170
which is involved in motor
planning, execution and balance.

01:39:31.170 --> 01:39:32.970
So, regardless of whether or not people

01:39:32.970 --> 01:39:37.560
are using cannabis of the
sativa or the indica variety,

01:39:37.560 --> 01:39:39.510
there are disruptions in motor circuitry.

01:39:39.510 --> 01:39:40.920
And, as you may have heard,

01:39:40.920 --> 01:39:42.150
if you listened to our episode

01:39:42.150 --> 01:39:45.360
with Rockefeller professor,
Dr. Erich Jarvis,

01:39:45.360 --> 01:39:50.360
who works on speech and
movement, speech is movement.

01:39:50.670 --> 01:39:53.190
The movements of the mouth,
the movements of the hands,

01:39:53.190 --> 01:39:55.890
those are intimately related
in terms of our speech.

01:39:55.890 --> 01:39:58.950
In fact, the centers of the
brain involved in hand movements

01:39:58.950 --> 01:40:01.865
are part of the speech
areas and vice versa.

01:40:01.865 --> 01:40:03.210
Erich actually pointed out

01:40:03.210 --> 01:40:05.100
that if you put your
hands behind your back,

01:40:05.100 --> 01:40:07.290
provided you normally do
have use of your hands,

01:40:07.290 --> 01:40:10.047
it actually will reduce
your fluidity of speech,

01:40:10.047 --> 01:40:12.383
and so I'm going to put them
back in front of me now.

01:40:13.770 --> 01:40:18.300
The point is, smoking marijuana
or consuming marijuana

01:40:18.300 --> 01:40:21.540
by edible changes one's speech

01:40:21.540 --> 01:40:23.100
and does it in a very specific way.

01:40:23.100 --> 01:40:26.670
And in this study by Adam
Vogel and colleagues,

01:40:26.670 --> 01:40:30.510
they explored a huge different
variety of aspects of speech.

01:40:30.510 --> 01:40:32.700
And this can be done
using spectral processing,

01:40:32.700 --> 01:40:34.230
which is fancy nerd speak

01:40:34.230 --> 01:40:36.480
for looking at how much
inflection there is

01:40:36.480 --> 01:40:38.760
or looking at how long people hold vowels

01:40:38.760 --> 01:40:40.290
or consonants, et cetera, and again,

01:40:40.290 --> 01:40:42.840
these are people not under
the influence of cannabis,

01:40:42.840 --> 01:40:45.720
but rather people who tend
to be under the influence

01:40:45.720 --> 01:40:48.080
of cannabis when not
participating in the study,

01:40:48.080 --> 01:40:51.390
in other words, chronic
recreational cannabis users.

01:40:51.390 --> 01:40:54.236
So what are the two major
shifts that cannabis causes

01:40:54.236 --> 01:40:56.100
on our patterns of speech?

01:40:56.100 --> 01:40:57.960
Well, the first one is a change

01:40:57.960 --> 01:40:59.970
in what's called spectral tilt.

01:40:59.970 --> 01:41:01.170
Again, that's fancy nerd speak

01:41:01.170 --> 01:41:03.780
for vocal effort and intensity.

01:41:03.780 --> 01:41:06.753
So, I'm not a pot smoker, I confess,

01:41:08.167 --> 01:41:10.893
but if I were to say the sentence,

01:41:11.767 --> 01:41:13.357
"Vocal effort and intensity

01:41:13.357 --> 01:41:16.200
"are important components of speech,"

01:41:16.200 --> 01:41:18.000
that's the way I would say that sentence

01:41:18.000 --> 01:41:20.520
if I was striving to
enunciate very carefully

01:41:20.520 --> 01:41:24.210
and to accent certain words and syllables.

01:41:24.210 --> 01:41:28.560
A pot smoker or somebody who
uses recreational cannabis

01:41:28.560 --> 01:41:32.193
fairly often would have
reduced spectral tilt,

01:41:32.193 --> 01:41:36.416
AKA, vocal effort
intensity, and might say,

01:41:36.416 --> 01:41:39.187
[mumbling] "Spectral tilt is
vocal effort in intensity,

01:41:39.187 --> 01:41:41.287
"and it differed between
groups and appeared to change

01:41:41.287 --> 01:41:44.880
"in line with the duration of
abstinence from cannabis use."

01:41:44.880 --> 01:41:47.130
That I think is not a far cry

01:41:47.130 --> 01:41:49.530
from the change in spectral
tilt that they observed here.

01:41:49.530 --> 01:41:54.120
In addition, there are
changes in verbal timing,

01:41:54.120 --> 01:41:56.670
that is pronunciation of words

01:41:56.670 --> 01:42:00.240
and accenting particular
syllables of words

01:42:00.240 --> 01:42:04.740
in people that consume
cannabis or smoke cannabis.

01:42:04.740 --> 01:42:09.360
So rather than emphasize
particular words within a sentence,

01:42:09.360 --> 01:42:11.280
so again, I'll just use
a sentence from the paper

01:42:11.280 --> 01:42:13.447
so that you can gain more
knowledge from the paper,

01:42:13.447 --> 01:42:16.050
"Cannabis," and I'll say it
the way that I would say it,

01:42:16.050 --> 01:42:18.937
since I'm not a cannabis user.

01:42:18.937 --> 01:42:21.397
"Cannabis, marijuana, is
the most commonly used,

01:42:21.397 --> 01:42:24.667
"illicit drug in the world with
approximately 4% of adults,

01:42:24.667 --> 01:42:27.607
"aged 15 to 64 years reporting recent use,

01:42:27.607 --> 01:42:30.037
"and the citation is from
the United Nations Office

01:42:30.037 --> 01:42:33.030
"on Drugs and Crime, 2019."

01:42:33.030 --> 01:42:34.050
So that would be the way

01:42:34.050 --> 01:42:35.940
that I would typically read that sentence.

01:42:35.940 --> 01:42:38.460
And having gone into
the data on this paper,

01:42:38.460 --> 01:42:41.160
and of course evaluated references therein

01:42:41.160 --> 01:42:44.220
and listened to some of
the spectral analysis

01:42:44.220 --> 01:42:46.200
that they include as data,

01:42:46.200 --> 01:42:47.970
you can literally go
into these papers online

01:42:47.970 --> 01:42:51.270
and hear recordings of
people who are cannabis users

01:42:51.270 --> 01:42:52.800
or non-cannabis users,

01:42:52.800 --> 01:42:55.350
and I'll try and give you a a clear sense

01:42:55.350 --> 01:42:58.620
without picking an extreme
example of how somebody

01:42:58.620 --> 01:43:02.550
who's a fairly consistent or
even occasional cannabis user

01:43:02.550 --> 01:43:04.300
might read that very same sentence.

01:43:05.908 --> 01:43:08.917
"Cannabis, marijuana, is
the most commonly used

01:43:08.917 --> 01:43:12.547
"illicit drug in the world
with approximately 4% of adults

01:43:12.547 --> 01:43:15.757
"age 15 to 64 years reporting recent use,

01:43:15.757 --> 01:43:18.277
"and the citation comes from
the United Nation Office

01:43:18.277 --> 01:43:21.000
"on Drugs and Crime in 2019."

01:43:21.000 --> 01:43:23.490
Now you'll notice that
wasn't a dramatic difference.

01:43:23.490 --> 01:43:25.500
And, of course I could
have taken the liberty

01:43:25.500 --> 01:43:27.210
to pick an extreme example of the sort

01:43:27.210 --> 01:43:29.520
that they did occasionally observe

01:43:29.520 --> 01:43:31.770
in evaluating subjects for this paper.

01:43:31.770 --> 01:43:33.548
I could have said something like,

01:43:33.548 --> 01:43:34.627
[mumbling in low tone]
"Cannabis, marijuana,

01:43:34.627 --> 01:43:36.450
"is the most commonly used illicit drug,"

01:43:36.450 --> 01:43:39.360
but frankly that would've been
selecting an outlier example,

01:43:39.360 --> 01:43:40.230
and I don't want to do that.

01:43:40.230 --> 01:43:42.720
I don't want to skew the data as they say.

01:43:42.720 --> 01:43:47.070
Rather, if you heard the
first time I read the sentence

01:43:47.070 --> 01:43:48.300
and the second time I read the sentence,

01:43:48.300 --> 01:43:51.900
what's mainly different is the difference

01:43:51.900 --> 01:43:55.170
in the amount of enunciation and accenting

01:43:55.170 --> 01:43:57.510
of particular words and
symbols within a sentence.

01:43:57.510 --> 01:44:00.390
So the total content that's
delivered is exactly the same,

01:44:00.390 --> 01:44:02.580
and while I wasn't measuring my pace,

01:44:02.580 --> 01:44:07.380
the overall rate of communication
is essentially the same,

01:44:07.380 --> 01:44:10.410
but there's less lilting
and falling of the voice

01:44:10.410 --> 01:44:11.280
and less accenting.

01:44:11.280 --> 01:44:14.370
That's the major consistent
effect of cannabis use.

01:44:14.370 --> 01:44:16.350
Now of course, there
are examples of people

01:44:16.350 --> 01:44:18.660
who are using a lot of cannabis,

01:44:18.660 --> 01:44:20.250
and it impacts brain centers

01:44:20.250 --> 01:44:21.750
involved in movement and speech,

01:44:21.750 --> 01:44:23.880
so much so that they really do have

01:44:23.880 --> 01:44:26.610
the really drawn out kind of, nah,

01:44:26.610 --> 01:44:29.130
and oftentimes this will
be detected in the laugh.

01:44:29.130 --> 01:44:31.890
There's this stereotypical stoner laugh,

01:44:31.890 --> 01:44:33.060
as it's sometimes called.

01:44:33.060 --> 01:44:34.380
Rather than say "Ha, ha, ha"

01:44:34.380 --> 01:44:36.720
or that's a fake laugh, obviously.

01:44:36.720 --> 01:44:37.890
It's hard to make myself laugh

01:44:37.890 --> 01:44:38.970
if something's not actually funny.

01:44:38.970 --> 01:44:40.380
I'll have to think of something funny.

01:44:40.380 --> 01:44:41.370
When people say like, "Ha!"

01:44:41.370 --> 01:44:43.380
That's a bit more of
the way I might laugh,

01:44:43.380 --> 01:44:45.870
like, "Ha," sort of the inspiratory laugh

01:44:45.870 --> 01:44:48.510
or the, "Ha," which is expiratory laugh.

01:44:48.510 --> 01:44:50.250
People who use cannabis chronically

01:44:50.250 --> 01:44:53.988
will often do that, "Haa,"
which is the back of the throat,

01:44:53.988 --> 01:44:57.270
[laughs raspingly] it's
neither inspiratory

01:44:57.270 --> 01:44:59.040
nor expiratory laugh,
and believe it or not,

01:44:59.040 --> 01:45:00.060
there's an entire literature

01:45:00.060 --> 01:45:03.030
on inspiratory, inhaling,
versus expiratory laughs,

01:45:03.030 --> 01:45:06.177
and there's also a literature on cannabis

01:45:06.177 --> 01:45:09.450
altering the pattern of
inspiratory and expiratory laughs.

01:45:09.450 --> 01:45:11.820
It almost sounds like a sarcastic laugh

01:45:11.820 --> 01:45:14.370
when, in fact, they may not
be feeling sarcastic at all.

01:45:14.370 --> 01:45:16.920
And I have a very close
friend and colleague

01:45:16.920 --> 01:45:19.290
who's a phenomenal
neurosurgeon and neuroscientist

01:45:19.290 --> 01:45:20.520
by the name of Dr. Eddie Chang.

01:45:20.520 --> 01:45:22.290
He's the chair of neurosurgery at UCSF,

01:45:22.290 --> 01:45:25.890
and his lab and him
are expert in the study

01:45:25.890 --> 01:45:29.610
of neuroscience controlling
language and speech.

01:45:29.610 --> 01:45:34.140
And he often tells me that he can predict,

01:45:34.140 --> 01:45:35.280
with almost certainty,

01:45:35.280 --> 01:45:38.250
whether or not somebody
is a regular cannabis user

01:45:38.250 --> 01:45:42.330
based, not just on the patterns
of speech that they use,

01:45:42.330 --> 01:45:45.330
but even just by recording
specific neurons in their brain

01:45:45.330 --> 01:45:47.910
that underlie the laugh reflex

01:45:47.910 --> 01:45:49.470
and certain patterns of speech.

01:45:49.470 --> 01:45:52.560
So this idea that cannabis
use changes your ability

01:45:52.560 --> 01:45:55.860
to speak and enunciate clearly
does appear to be true.

01:45:55.860 --> 01:45:58.590
And the stereotype that cannabis use

01:45:58.590 --> 01:46:00.690
tends to create more of a drawl

01:46:00.690 --> 01:46:04.830
or, if you will, a laziness in the laugh,

01:46:04.830 --> 01:46:08.040
and some of the reflexive
enunciations that people use,

01:46:08.040 --> 01:46:09.300
does also appear to be true.

01:46:09.300 --> 01:46:12.000
And I say all that, of
course, with the caveat

01:46:12.000 --> 01:46:15.060
that many people out there
will know individuals

01:46:15.060 --> 01:46:18.000
or perhaps you,
yourselves, are individuals

01:46:18.000 --> 01:46:22.230
that may use cannabis but that
have incredible articulation,

01:46:22.230 --> 01:46:23.340
probably better than mine

01:46:23.340 --> 01:46:24.690
and better than other people out there

01:46:24.690 --> 01:46:26.850
who speak for a living, so I'm not saying

01:46:26.850 --> 01:46:29.640
that 100% of people that
use cannabis regularly

01:46:29.640 --> 01:46:31.350
can't speak well or clearly.

01:46:31.350 --> 01:46:33.030
That's simply not what I'm saying,

01:46:33.030 --> 01:46:35.550
and I don't want to
communicate that idea at all,

01:46:35.550 --> 01:46:36.660
but it is the case that people

01:46:36.660 --> 01:46:38.760
who are regular cannabis users

01:46:38.760 --> 01:46:41.370
are impacting the neural
circuits involved in movement.

01:46:41.370 --> 01:46:43.140
Movement also controls speech,

01:46:43.140 --> 01:46:46.440
and therefore, cannabis
is impacting speech.

01:46:46.440 --> 01:46:47.580
Now I'd like to turn our attention

01:46:47.580 --> 01:46:51.120
to whether or not cannabis
can increase sexual activity,

01:46:51.120 --> 01:46:54.480
sexual desire, and/or sexual function,

01:46:54.480 --> 01:46:57.600
so we're going to be talking
about libido, about sexual desire

01:46:57.600 --> 01:47:00.630
and about effects of cannabis on hormones.

01:47:00.630 --> 01:47:02.700
And while this might
sound like a discussion

01:47:02.700 --> 01:47:06.120
that's purely oriented towards
recreational use of cannabis,

01:47:06.120 --> 01:47:08.430
that is people using
cannabis to heightened

01:47:08.430 --> 01:47:12.060
or increase their sense of
arousal for sexual activity,

01:47:12.060 --> 01:47:14.610
it actually ventures into
the clinical realm too,

01:47:14.610 --> 01:47:17.640
meaning there have been
excellent peer-reviewed studies

01:47:17.640 --> 01:47:19.110
that I'll describe to you in a moment

01:47:19.110 --> 01:47:22.616
exploring the use of cannabis
or THC, more specifically,

01:47:22.616 --> 01:47:27.030
for something called hypoactive
sexual desire disorder,

01:47:27.030 --> 01:47:30.450
which refers to a persistent
or recurrent deficiency

01:47:30.450 --> 01:47:32.760
or absence of sexual
fantasies and thoughts

01:47:32.760 --> 01:47:37.320
and/or desire for, or
receptivity to, sexual activity.

01:47:37.320 --> 01:47:39.660
This is a disorder that is fairly common,

01:47:39.660 --> 01:47:43.620
anywhere from 6% to 9% of
people, both males and females.

01:47:43.620 --> 01:47:46.320
It used to be considered higher prevalence

01:47:46.320 --> 01:47:48.330
in females than in males,

01:47:48.330 --> 01:47:50.790
but now those numbers
seem to be evening out.

01:47:50.790 --> 01:47:54.480
So basically, there are
anywhere from 6% to 9%

01:47:54.480 --> 01:47:58.389
of people out there who have
very diminished sexual desire.

01:47:58.389 --> 01:48:00.450
And so, a number of those people

01:48:00.450 --> 01:48:02.160
are interested in figuring out ways

01:48:02.160 --> 01:48:04.290
to increase their amount of sexual desire,

01:48:04.290 --> 01:48:07.230
and of course, there are people
in the general population

01:48:07.230 --> 01:48:10.710
who may not have hyperactive
sexual desire disorder

01:48:10.710 --> 01:48:13.350
who are interested in using
cannabis recreationally

01:48:13.350 --> 01:48:15.630
in order to increase their desire for,

01:48:15.630 --> 01:48:18.570
or their experience of, sexual activity.

01:48:18.570 --> 01:48:19.890
Now first we have to acknowledge

01:48:19.890 --> 01:48:21.810
that sexual desire and activity

01:48:21.810 --> 01:48:24.120
is a complex set of processes,

01:48:24.120 --> 01:48:27.720
meaning it's not just
one event, sex, the verb.

01:48:27.720 --> 01:48:31.770
It involves arousal, it
involves sex, the verb,

01:48:31.770 --> 01:48:35.370
and it involves a whole set of
mindsets and emotional states

01:48:35.370 --> 01:48:37.830
that vary tremendously
between individuals.

01:48:37.830 --> 01:48:41.505
But once again, we can distill
out a few basic principles,

01:48:41.505 --> 01:48:43.560
and I should emphasize,

01:48:43.560 --> 01:48:44.827
these are by no means the only chemicals

01:48:44.827 --> 01:48:48.330
and neuro circuits
involved in the sexual arc,

01:48:48.330 --> 01:48:51.750
as we'll call it, desire,
the act of sex, et cetera,

01:48:51.750 --> 01:48:54.300
but they are central to
it, they are vital to it.

01:48:54.300 --> 01:48:55.560
They would be considered

01:48:55.560 --> 01:48:58.470
what I would call necessary
but not sufficient,

01:48:58.470 --> 01:49:00.300
so there'll be other
chemicals involved too,

01:49:00.300 --> 01:49:01.800
but the main chemicals in neuro circuits

01:49:01.800 --> 01:49:03.540
are those involved in dopamine,

01:49:03.540 --> 01:49:05.850
so the so-called
mesolimbic reward pathway,

01:49:05.850 --> 01:49:08.760
in particular, a brain structure
called nucleus accumbens,

01:49:08.760 --> 01:49:10.410
which we'll talk about more in a moment,

01:49:10.410 --> 01:49:14.460
is vital to all motivated
behaviors and to the seeking out

01:49:14.460 --> 01:49:17.610
of all particular types of
pleasurable experiences,

01:49:17.610 --> 01:49:18.990
and sex is no exception.

01:49:18.990 --> 01:49:21.990
So when we hear that the
nucleus accumbens is activated,

01:49:21.990 --> 01:49:24.120
that almost always means that dopamine

01:49:24.120 --> 01:49:25.830
has been released in that area

01:49:25.830 --> 01:49:27.570
and other areas of the brain body,

01:49:27.570 --> 01:49:29.550
and we can consider dopamine central

01:49:29.550 --> 01:49:34.539
to the desire for,
pursuit of, an act of sex.

01:49:34.539 --> 01:49:38.580
In addition to that, there
are molecules like oxytocin,

01:49:38.580 --> 01:49:40.050
which are involved in pair bonding,

01:49:40.050 --> 01:49:41.580
and they're going to be
neural circuit specific

01:49:41.580 --> 01:49:43.620
to the oxytocin circuitry,

01:49:43.620 --> 01:49:47.400
but in terms of sexual
arousal and sexual behavior,

01:49:47.400 --> 01:49:50.160
it's really the dopamine pathway
in this nucleus accumbens,

01:49:50.160 --> 01:49:52.297
which are especially vital.

01:49:52.297 --> 01:49:54.600
That allows us to address the question,

01:49:54.600 --> 01:49:58.890
does cannabis increase, decrease,

01:49:58.890 --> 01:50:01.680
or have no effect on sexual desire

01:50:01.680 --> 01:50:04.680
and/or the ability to have sex?

01:50:04.680 --> 01:50:08.700
And therein, we will find
some very interesting answers,

01:50:08.700 --> 01:50:10.590
and because, once again,

01:50:10.590 --> 01:50:14.760
it will point to the fact
that the effects of cannabis

01:50:14.760 --> 01:50:18.060
on different individuals
can be highly divergent,

01:50:18.060 --> 01:50:20.310
meaning in one set of individuals,

01:50:20.310 --> 01:50:22.770
cannabis will make them far less anxious.

01:50:22.770 --> 01:50:24.450
And in another set of individuals,

01:50:24.450 --> 01:50:26.790
the same strain of cannabis,
at the same dosage,

01:50:26.790 --> 01:50:28.350
will make them extremely anxious.

01:50:28.350 --> 01:50:31.980
The same can be said
also of sexual activity.

01:50:31.980 --> 01:50:33.990
And this was beautifully illustrated

01:50:33.990 --> 01:50:36.697
in the context of sexual
desire in the journal,

01:50:36.697 --> 01:50:39.450
"Psycho-pharmacology," in
a paper published in 2017.

01:50:39.450 --> 01:50:43.267
The title of this paper is,
"Individual Prolactin Reactivity

01:50:43.267 --> 01:50:46.897
"Modulates Response of Nucleus
Accumbens to Erotic Stimuli

01:50:46.897 --> 01:50:51.051
"During Acute Cannabis
Intoxication, an fMRI Pilot Study."

01:50:51.051 --> 01:50:53.610
So I'll give a little bit of definition

01:50:53.610 --> 01:50:54.750
to some of the terms in the title

01:50:54.750 --> 01:50:57.660
that will make it easier for
you to understand the paper,

01:50:57.660 --> 01:50:59.160
but then I'll just march
through the results,

01:50:59.160 --> 01:51:00.660
because they're very straightforward

01:51:00.660 --> 01:51:03.390
and easy to understand
and very interesting.

01:51:03.390 --> 01:51:06.570
fMRI is just functional
magnetic resonance imaging,

01:51:06.570 --> 01:51:09.300
so basically subjects in this experiment

01:51:09.300 --> 01:51:12.060
came into the laboratory.

01:51:12.060 --> 01:51:16.230
They were either people who
had not used cannabis before

01:51:16.230 --> 01:51:18.849
or who had used cannabis before.

01:51:18.849 --> 01:51:20.970
They were placed into a brain scanner,

01:51:20.970 --> 01:51:22.680
one of these fMRI devices.

01:51:22.680 --> 01:51:25.802
It looks like a tube that
people are backed up into

01:51:25.802 --> 01:51:27.267
and then they can view images in there,

01:51:27.267 --> 01:51:28.830
and their brain can be imaged

01:51:28.830 --> 01:51:30.360
without having to remove any skull

01:51:30.360 --> 01:51:32.410
or drill into the skull, no neurosurgery.

01:51:33.720 --> 01:51:35.850
The participants in
this study were grouped

01:51:35.850 --> 01:51:37.710
according to whether or
not they had experienced

01:51:37.710 --> 01:51:39.185
any aphrodisiac effects

01:51:39.185 --> 01:51:41.790
during the intoxication with cannabis,

01:51:41.790 --> 01:51:43.770
so that would be the first group, Group A,

01:51:43.770 --> 01:51:45.960
they literally called it
group A for aphrodisiac.

01:51:45.960 --> 01:51:47.070
And then the second group,

01:51:47.070 --> 01:51:48.840
and this is the only thing I
don't like about this study,

01:51:48.840 --> 01:51:50.340
is rather than call it group B,

01:51:50.340 --> 01:51:52.710
they called them 'group non A,'

01:51:52.710 --> 01:51:54.750
which just gets a little confusing.

01:51:54.750 --> 01:51:56.460
So I'll try and simplify all this.

01:51:56.460 --> 01:51:57.870
There are two groups,

01:51:57.870 --> 01:52:02.220
and one group experiences sexual arousal

01:52:02.220 --> 01:52:06.270
when under the influence of
cannabis, THC specifically.

01:52:06.270 --> 01:52:07.590
The other group does not.

01:52:07.590 --> 01:52:12.060
And it turns out this is a very
commonly observed divergence

01:52:12.060 --> 01:52:14.310
of effects of cannabis.

01:52:14.310 --> 01:52:16.860
Some people experience
a lot of sexual arousal

01:52:16.860 --> 01:52:20.610
from cannabis and THC in
particular, and some people do not,

01:52:20.610 --> 01:52:24.120
in fact, they experience
suppression of sexual desire,

01:52:24.120 --> 01:52:25.950
and it's always been a
little bit mysterious

01:52:25.950 --> 01:52:27.990
as to why that is.

01:52:27.990 --> 01:52:31.770
Well, in this study, they showed people

01:52:31.770 --> 01:52:33.750
in both groups erotic images,

01:52:33.750 --> 01:52:35.940
and they measured sexual arousal

01:52:35.940 --> 01:52:36.990
through a number of different measures.

01:52:36.990 --> 01:52:37.890
We won't go into all that.

01:52:37.890 --> 01:52:39.360
It was largely subjective.

01:52:39.360 --> 01:52:40.290
There have been other studies

01:52:40.290 --> 01:52:42.090
where they've actually measured things

01:52:42.090 --> 01:52:45.822
like erections in males and
vaginal lubrication in females,

01:52:45.822 --> 01:52:49.533
so-called autonomic responses
that people can't lie about,

01:52:50.400 --> 01:52:54.360
so to speak, and that
tap into other aspects

01:52:54.360 --> 01:52:57.690
of the so-called sexual arousal process.

01:52:57.690 --> 01:52:59.790
In this study, they
also took blood samples

01:52:59.790 --> 01:53:03.210
to look at the concentration
of things like cannabinoids,

01:53:03.210 --> 01:53:04.470
so this is a really nice study

01:53:04.470 --> 01:53:06.964
in that they actually
measured how much THC

01:53:06.964 --> 01:53:09.720
was in the bloodstream
in different individuals

01:53:09.720 --> 01:53:12.450
who reacted to these erotic
stimuli in different ways.

01:53:12.450 --> 01:53:15.240
And they measured
hormones, namely cortisol,

01:53:15.240 --> 01:53:16.320
which is a stress hormone,

01:53:16.320 --> 01:53:17.850
which tends to negatively correlate

01:53:17.850 --> 01:53:20.580
with sexual arousal and prolactin.

01:53:20.580 --> 01:53:23.610
And the interesting
takeaway from the study was

01:53:23.610 --> 01:53:25.830
that for people, and it didn't matter

01:53:25.830 --> 01:53:28.580
if it was males or females,
'cause they looked at both,

01:53:30.300 --> 01:53:34.964
for people that experienced
elevated prolactin levels

01:53:34.964 --> 01:53:39.300
under cannabis intoxication,
that's how they refer to it.

01:53:39.300 --> 01:53:41.010
People take cannabis,
they measured prolactin.

01:53:41.010 --> 01:53:43.960
Some people had elevated
prolactin and some people did not.

01:53:44.966 --> 01:53:48.033
For the people that had
elevated levels of prolactin,

01:53:49.230 --> 01:53:51.570
they did not observe activation

01:53:51.570 --> 01:53:54.390
of brain areas associated
with sexual arousal,

01:53:54.390 --> 01:53:56.460
in this case, the right nucleus accumbens,

01:53:56.460 --> 01:53:58.860
so you have two nucleus accumbi,

01:53:58.860 --> 01:54:00.780
I guess they would be called,
one on each side of the brain,

01:54:00.780 --> 01:54:02.510
and the activation of that brain area

01:54:02.510 --> 01:54:05.550
is strongly associated with
dopamine and with arousal

01:54:05.550 --> 01:54:07.920
and sexual arousal in
particular in this study,

01:54:07.920 --> 01:54:10.020
and if people had elevated prolactin,

01:54:10.020 --> 01:54:12.930
they did not experience
activation of nucleus accumbens,

01:54:12.930 --> 01:54:15.450
and they did not report
feeling sexually aroused

01:54:15.450 --> 01:54:17.370
to those pictures, at least
not to the same degree

01:54:17.370 --> 01:54:18.540
as the other group.

01:54:18.540 --> 01:54:20.670
So some people's prolactin levels go up

01:54:20.670 --> 01:54:22.710
when they ingest cannabis,

01:54:22.710 --> 01:54:25.920
and those people do not
achieve elevated levels

01:54:25.920 --> 01:54:29.400
of sexual arousal when under
the influence of cannabis,

01:54:29.400 --> 01:54:31.738
even if they're looking at erotic stimuli.

01:54:31.738 --> 01:54:35.790
That makes sense, because prolactin

01:54:35.790 --> 01:54:39.150
is mutually inhibitory, as we would say.

01:54:39.150 --> 01:54:41.070
It's kind of in a push-pull with dopamine.

01:54:41.070 --> 01:54:42.330
When dopamine levels are high,

01:54:42.330 --> 01:54:44.610
prolactin levels tend to be low,

01:54:44.610 --> 01:54:46.200
and when prolactin levels are high,

01:54:46.200 --> 01:54:48.030
dopamine levels tend to be low.

01:54:48.030 --> 01:54:50.580
The other group, so-called Group A,

01:54:50.580 --> 01:54:53.520
that experienced elevated
levels of sexual arousal

01:54:53.520 --> 01:54:55.260
when under the influence of cannabis

01:54:55.260 --> 01:54:56.913
and viewing erotic stimuli,

01:54:57.780 --> 01:55:01.620
that group did not show
elevated levels of prolactin

01:55:01.620 --> 01:55:03.840
in response to cannabis.

01:55:03.840 --> 01:55:04.860
So this, I believe,

01:55:04.860 --> 01:55:08.070
resolves a longstanding
controversy in the field,

01:55:08.070 --> 01:55:11.340
which is, does cannabis
increase sexual arousal?

01:55:11.340 --> 01:55:12.330
Well, it depends.

01:55:12.330 --> 01:55:14.250
If you fall into the category of person

01:55:14.250 --> 01:55:15.990
who has elevated levels of prolactin

01:55:15.990 --> 01:55:20.160
in response to cannabis,
then no, actually cannabis

01:55:20.160 --> 01:55:22.921
will suppress your sexual
response and desire.

01:55:22.921 --> 01:55:26.048
If, however, you are in
the category of person

01:55:26.048 --> 01:55:29.850
that does not have elevated
levels of prolactin

01:55:29.850 --> 01:55:31.740
in response to cannabis,

01:55:31.740 --> 01:55:35.130
well then, erotic stimuli can potentially

01:55:35.130 --> 01:55:37.800
and in fact, do increase sexual arousal

01:55:37.800 --> 01:55:39.360
in the majority of individuals.

01:55:39.360 --> 01:55:40.860
Now, many of you are probably hearing this

01:55:40.860 --> 01:55:42.600
and wondering whether or not you fall

01:55:42.600 --> 01:55:45.900
into one category of
individual or another.

01:55:45.900 --> 01:55:48.990
And the key thing here to understand

01:55:48.990 --> 01:55:53.130
is that levels of prolactin
heading into the study

01:55:53.130 --> 01:55:57.950
did not predict whether or
not people would respond

01:55:57.950 --> 01:56:02.340
to cannabis with elevated or non elevated

01:56:02.340 --> 01:56:04.980
or even reduced levels of sexual arousal.

01:56:04.980 --> 01:56:08.730
It was whether or not people's
prolactin levels went up

01:56:08.730 --> 01:56:11.310
or did not go up, that predicted

01:56:11.310 --> 01:56:14.730
whether or not their levels
of arousal would go up or not.

01:56:14.730 --> 01:56:17.130
So if you are somebody who, yes,

01:56:17.130 --> 01:56:20.460
does experience elevated
levels of sexual arousal

01:56:20.460 --> 01:56:23.610
and function went under
the influence of cannabis,

01:56:23.610 --> 01:56:25.200
well that's very likely that cannabis

01:56:25.200 --> 01:56:26.910
does not increase your prolactin levels,

01:56:26.910 --> 01:56:30.510
at least not to a significant
degree while you're taking it.

01:56:30.510 --> 01:56:33.180
And if you're somebody who
does not experience increases

01:56:33.180 --> 01:56:35.070
in sexual arousal or function

01:56:35.070 --> 01:56:36.783
or even diminished sexual
arousal and function

01:56:36.783 --> 01:56:38.400
when under the influence of cannabis,

01:56:38.400 --> 01:56:39.780
it's very likely that cannabis

01:56:39.780 --> 01:56:41.760
is increasing your levels of prolactin.

01:56:41.760 --> 01:56:44.370
Unfortunately, there's
no way to know or predict

01:56:44.370 --> 01:56:46.410
based on some other measure.

01:56:46.410 --> 01:56:47.880
I think the outcome measure,

01:56:47.880 --> 01:56:50.160
that is increased or not increased

01:56:50.160 --> 01:56:53.040
or even reduced sexual arousal
is really the litmus test

01:56:53.040 --> 01:56:54.990
by which one can figure that out.

01:56:54.990 --> 01:56:57.240
While we are on the topic
of the effects of cannabis

01:56:57.240 --> 01:57:00.360
on sexual function and
hormones like prolactin,

01:57:00.360 --> 01:57:01.680
it's probably worth mentioning

01:57:01.680 --> 01:57:04.110
that cannabis has been studied extensively

01:57:04.110 --> 01:57:06.300
for its impact on other hormones,

01:57:06.300 --> 01:57:09.990
and we can summarize those
literature in the following way.

01:57:09.990 --> 01:57:12.090
And here I'm referring to
studies only on adults.

01:57:12.090 --> 01:57:13.860
We'll talk about the
developing brain and body

01:57:13.860 --> 01:57:17.430
in a little bit, but it is very clear

01:57:17.430 --> 01:57:22.430
that smoking cannabis
increases prolactin levels.

01:57:22.978 --> 01:57:24.480
Very, very clear.

01:57:24.480 --> 01:57:27.277
Now, you might say, "Didn't,
you just describe a study

01:57:27.277 --> 01:57:31.477
"about a set of individuals
whose prolactin didn't increase

01:57:31.477 --> 01:57:33.067
"and as a consequence,

01:57:33.067 --> 01:57:35.790
"their level of sexual desire went up?"

01:57:35.790 --> 01:57:37.560
Yes, there are a subset of individuals

01:57:37.560 --> 01:57:42.370
for which that's true, but
people who smoke cannabis

01:57:42.370 --> 01:57:45.420
do experience increases in prolactin,

01:57:45.420 --> 01:57:47.190
and that's especially pronounced in people

01:57:47.190 --> 01:57:49.800
that smoke cannabis
more than twice a week.

01:57:49.800 --> 01:57:54.450
So this is important, prolactin,
as I referred to earlier,

01:57:54.450 --> 01:57:59.450
is reciprocal or mutually
inhibitory with dopamine.

01:57:59.790 --> 01:58:01.530
One way to think about
this is in the context

01:58:01.530 --> 01:58:03.630
of the normal sexual arousal arc

01:58:03.630 --> 01:58:05.430
whereby dopamine is increased

01:58:05.430 --> 01:58:09.600
when people are sexually
aroused, but then after orgasm,

01:58:09.600 --> 01:58:12.840
both in males and females,
prolactin levels skyrocket.

01:58:12.840 --> 01:58:14.400
This is actually what creates

01:58:14.400 --> 01:58:16.410
the so-called refractory period for males

01:58:16.410 --> 01:58:18.420
during which they cannot
achieve erection again

01:58:18.420 --> 01:58:19.620
for some period of time.

01:58:19.620 --> 01:58:22.380
That relates directly to how long

01:58:22.380 --> 01:58:24.603
the prolactin increase lasts.

01:58:26.460 --> 01:58:30.270
Prolactin is also increased
in new parents of all species,

01:58:30.270 --> 01:58:35.100
including humans, which
at least partially,

01:58:35.100 --> 01:58:38.520
explains some of the reported
or typical reductions

01:58:38.520 --> 01:58:41.070
in sexual desire and
activity in new parents.

01:58:41.070 --> 01:58:42.240
Now, there are other reasons for that too,

01:58:42.240 --> 01:58:45.090
sleep deprivation, but nature is smart

01:58:45.090 --> 01:58:47.400
and has arranged a set
of hormones and circuits

01:58:47.400 --> 01:58:49.650
in the brain and body,
such that when tending

01:58:49.650 --> 01:58:53.160
to a newborn is the most
important thing, it relegates,

01:58:53.160 --> 01:58:57.840
it reduces the importance
of producing more children

01:58:57.840 --> 01:59:00.090
and sexual activity in those moments

01:59:00.090 --> 01:59:01.950
and days and weeks, sometimes longer.

01:59:01.950 --> 01:59:04.560
So when prolactin levels are
up, dopamine levels are down.

01:59:04.560 --> 01:59:06.570
Smoking marijuana more than twice a week

01:59:06.570 --> 01:59:09.060
significantly increases prolactin.

01:59:09.060 --> 01:59:11.400
There are fewer studies exploring

01:59:11.400 --> 01:59:14.280
whether or not edible
marijuana has the same effect,

01:59:14.280 --> 01:59:18.210
although the preliminary evidence
suggests that it does not.

01:59:18.210 --> 01:59:21.210
I get into this in a lot more
detail in a future episode,

01:59:21.210 --> 01:59:24.120
all about hormones with Dr. Kyle Gillett,

01:59:24.120 --> 01:59:25.950
who's been on this podcast before.

01:59:25.950 --> 01:59:28.410
But he verified that, and
my read of the literature

01:59:28.410 --> 01:59:33.410
is that the edible forms
of marijuana, cannabis,

01:59:33.476 --> 01:59:37.080
probably again, let's put
an asterisks next to this,

01:59:37.080 --> 01:59:39.480
but it appears do not have as as much

01:59:39.480 --> 01:59:41.100
of a prolactin elevating effect

01:59:41.100 --> 01:59:45.030
and therefore, not as much of
a dopamine suppressive effect

01:59:45.030 --> 01:59:46.200
and therefore, not as much

01:59:46.200 --> 01:59:48.390
of a testosterone suppressing effect,

01:59:48.390 --> 01:59:50.040
and that gets to the
issue of testosterone.

01:59:50.040 --> 01:59:52.770
Does cannabis, marijuana
suppress testosterone?

01:59:52.770 --> 01:59:55.720
And this is a very controversial
literature and here's why.

01:59:56.700 --> 01:59:59.980
Some studies say, "Yes,
it suppresses testosterone

01:59:59.980 --> 02:00:02.670
"in males and females," and keep in mind

02:00:02.670 --> 02:00:04.770
that testosterone in
females is vital for libido

02:00:04.770 --> 02:00:08.430
and cognitive function,
cellular repair, et cetera,

02:00:08.430 --> 02:00:11.400
so it's not just important
in males, of course.

02:00:11.400 --> 02:00:14.370
However, other studies say that cannabis

02:00:14.370 --> 02:00:16.530
does not decrease testosterone,

02:00:16.530 --> 02:00:20.490
and it seems to depend on
whether or not the cannabis

02:00:20.490 --> 02:00:23.670
is brought into the system
by way of smoking or edible.

02:00:23.670 --> 02:00:27.210
And it seems to depend on
whether or not the cannabis

02:00:27.210 --> 02:00:30.030
is used chronically by
an individual or acutely.

02:00:30.030 --> 02:00:31.980
And here I just want to zoom out and say

02:00:31.980 --> 02:00:35.190
that studies on cannabis or
drugs of any kind in humans

02:00:35.190 --> 02:00:36.240
are really complicated.

02:00:36.240 --> 02:00:37.080
If you think about it,

02:00:37.080 --> 02:00:38.850
someone has to come into the laboratory,

02:00:38.850 --> 02:00:41.542
and let's say you want to
study chronic cannabis use.

02:00:41.542 --> 02:00:43.980
Well, you can't keep them in
the laboratory all the time,

02:00:43.980 --> 02:00:45.720
so you have to rely on their self-report

02:00:45.720 --> 02:00:47.940
of how often they use
cannabis and in what form,

02:00:47.940 --> 02:00:50.430
and you can't really control
from one individual to the next

02:00:50.430 --> 02:00:51.990
of how much cannabis and THC

02:00:51.990 --> 02:00:52.950
they're bringing into their system.

02:00:52.950 --> 02:00:55.650
One person might smoke
cannabis out of a bong

02:00:55.650 --> 02:01:00.590
and take big, deep, lungs-full
bong inhalations or such.

02:01:02.310 --> 02:01:04.590
Other people might smoke joints.

02:01:04.590 --> 02:01:06.150
Other people might use edibles.

02:01:06.150 --> 02:01:08.460
It becomes very complicated
to know what people have done

02:01:08.460 --> 02:01:10.350
and that they're reporting it accurately,

02:01:10.350 --> 02:01:12.990
and no joke here, especially if marijuana

02:01:12.990 --> 02:01:15.000
is impacting the
short-term memory systems.

02:01:15.000 --> 02:01:16.170
They might not actually remember.

02:01:16.170 --> 02:01:18.660
They might not be tracking it that well.

02:01:18.660 --> 02:01:20.970
Contrast that with
studies of the acute use

02:01:20.970 --> 02:01:24.090
of cannabis and THC, where
people who are not regular users

02:01:24.090 --> 02:01:26.550
come into the laboratory and now suddenly,

02:01:26.550 --> 02:01:29.580
with institutional guidelines
and safety protocols,

02:01:29.580 --> 02:01:31.770
are under the influence
of THC and cannabis.

02:01:31.770 --> 02:01:32.970
Well, now you're dealing with a person

02:01:32.970 --> 02:01:37.170
who may not have experience
with the elevated heart rate

02:01:37.170 --> 02:01:38.687
and blood pressure that's
characteristic of cannabis,

02:01:38.687 --> 02:01:40.140
'cause it is a stimulant,

02:01:40.140 --> 02:01:42.090
at least when initially
brought into the system,

02:01:42.090 --> 02:01:44.430
even if it might eventually
lead to relaxation.

02:01:44.430 --> 02:01:45.840
So now you've got someone who's anxious

02:01:45.840 --> 02:01:48.780
or somebody who's not
anxious, who's deeply relaxed,

02:01:48.780 --> 02:01:50.040
and you're trying to study these effects,

02:01:50.040 --> 02:01:52.410
so it's a moving target of sorts,

02:01:52.410 --> 02:01:55.940
and it's very complicated to
study marijuana and cannabis

02:01:55.940 --> 02:01:57.810
in its various derivatives in this way.

02:01:57.810 --> 02:01:59.910
Now you can probably appreciate better

02:01:59.910 --> 02:02:02.580
as to why there's so little nuanced data

02:02:02.580 --> 02:02:04.830
about sativa versus indica,

02:02:04.830 --> 02:02:08.010
versus different ratios of CBD and THC.

02:02:08.010 --> 02:02:09.810
It's really difficult to do these studies

02:02:09.810 --> 02:02:10.650
in the first place.

02:02:10.650 --> 02:02:12.270
That said, the general rules

02:02:12.270 --> 02:02:14.940
are smoking marijuana increases
prolactin in men and women,

02:02:14.940 --> 02:02:17.310
which will reduce
dopamine and testosterone.

02:02:17.310 --> 02:02:19.643
Smoking marijuana chronically,

02:02:19.643 --> 02:02:22.232
meaning more than twice a week,

02:02:22.232 --> 02:02:25.320
does appear to reduce
testosterone significantly

02:02:25.320 --> 02:02:27.886
and elevate so-called aromatase enzymes,

02:02:27.886 --> 02:02:29.310
which are the enzymes

02:02:29.310 --> 02:02:31.560
that convert testosterone into estrogen.

02:02:31.560 --> 02:02:33.401
This might partially explain the effect

02:02:33.401 --> 02:02:38.400
that occurs in about 35% of
males, which is gynecomastia,

02:02:38.400 --> 02:02:41.100
which is a development of
breast tissue in males,

02:02:41.100 --> 02:02:42.690
in particular young males,

02:02:42.690 --> 02:02:44.550
who have elevated levels of testosterone

02:02:44.550 --> 02:02:47.370
or who are taking exogenous testosterone

02:02:47.370 --> 02:02:49.350
for testosterone replacement therapy

02:02:49.350 --> 02:02:51.780
or if they're taking high
doses, anabolic steroids,

02:02:51.780 --> 02:02:55.380
or in females, the
increase in breast size,

02:02:55.380 --> 02:02:58.230
which is due to additional estrogen

02:02:58.230 --> 02:02:59.940
from testosterone converted to estrogen.

02:02:59.940 --> 02:03:01.800
So it does appear that
marijuana and cannabis

02:03:01.800 --> 02:03:04.890
increase estrogen, reduce
testosterone, increase prolactin,

02:03:04.890 --> 02:03:07.470
especially in chronic users.

02:03:07.470 --> 02:03:09.030
Now, I'm sure that some
people out there will say,

02:03:09.030 --> 02:03:12.330
well, their testosterone
levels are exceedingly high

02:03:12.330 --> 02:03:15.450
or they are fine, meaning
the constellation of symptoms

02:03:15.450 --> 02:03:18.230
associated with low testosterone
and elevate estrogen

02:03:18.230 --> 02:03:20.160
are not present in them.

02:03:20.160 --> 02:03:24.810
That probably means one
of two things, or both.

02:03:24.810 --> 02:03:27.090
They either had elevated
levels of testosterone

02:03:27.090 --> 02:03:28.740
to begin with, so their
ceiling was higher,

02:03:28.740 --> 02:03:30.660
so bringing it down didn't
have that much of effect,

02:03:30.660 --> 02:03:33.510
or that they have very low levels

02:03:33.510 --> 02:03:35.640
of aromatase in their system.

02:03:35.640 --> 02:03:38.220
There are some anecdotal evidence

02:03:38.220 --> 02:03:41.220
that smoking particular
parts of the marijuana plant,

02:03:41.220 --> 02:03:45.420
in particular, the seeds,
can increase aromatase

02:03:45.420 --> 02:03:47.430
in the conversion of
testosterone to estrogen.

02:03:47.430 --> 02:03:48.540
I think in the old days,

02:03:48.540 --> 02:03:50.580
the lore was the seeds make you sterile,

02:03:50.580 --> 02:03:52.710
and I think that was related
to what I just told you,

02:03:52.710 --> 02:03:55.830
this increase in conversion
of testosterone to estrogen.

02:03:55.830 --> 02:03:57.570
There's a vast literature

02:03:57.570 --> 02:04:00.833
on the effects of cannabis on fertility.

02:04:00.833 --> 02:04:05.220
Yes, it does seem to alter sperm motility

02:04:05.220 --> 02:04:06.660
and sperm health and function

02:04:06.660 --> 02:04:08.850
when taken chronically,
more than twice per week,

02:04:08.850 --> 02:04:12.270
in particular high doses
of high potency THC.

02:04:12.270 --> 02:04:14.310
This is something we will
cover in far more detail

02:04:14.310 --> 02:04:16.440
on a future episode all about fertility.

02:04:16.440 --> 02:04:19.560
And in females, there's
an increase in estrogen

02:04:19.560 --> 02:04:22.470
as a consequence of smoking marijuana

02:04:22.470 --> 02:04:26.430
and increasing prolactin
and estrogen in parallel.

02:04:26.430 --> 02:04:29.070
Whether or not that's
detrimental isn't clear,

02:04:29.070 --> 02:04:33.152
although I point out that
elevated estrogen and prolactin

02:04:33.152 --> 02:04:36.210
can be associated,
again, can be associated,

02:04:36.210 --> 02:04:38.670
not necessarily, and
certainly, not causative,

02:04:38.670 --> 02:04:41.003
but can be associated with elevated levels

02:04:41.003 --> 02:04:43.620
or frequency of breast cancer detection.

02:04:43.620 --> 02:04:46.080
So cannabis and its effects on hormones

02:04:46.080 --> 02:04:47.643
are not without consequence.

02:04:48.870 --> 02:04:50.370
There are effects of cannabis

02:04:50.370 --> 02:04:53.070
on cortisol in some individuals.

02:04:53.070 --> 02:04:55.650
It greatly increases
cortisol due the anxiety

02:04:55.650 --> 02:04:58.020
and paranoia it can create,

02:04:58.020 --> 02:05:00.330
and in other individuals
it reduces cortisol.

02:05:00.330 --> 02:05:02.250
Again, we have these divergent effects.

02:05:02.250 --> 02:05:04.890
But I want to be very clear,
the effects on prolactin,

02:05:04.890 --> 02:05:07.080
meaning elevated prolactin,
the effects on testosterone,

02:05:07.080 --> 02:05:09.120
meaning, at least most studies point to,

02:05:09.120 --> 02:05:12.510
reduced levels of testosterone
and increased estrogen.

02:05:12.510 --> 02:05:15.420
That seems to be true
for most all individuals

02:05:15.420 --> 02:05:17.760
that chronically use cannabis,

02:05:17.760 --> 02:05:21.420
whereas the effects on
cortisol tend to be divergent.

02:05:21.420 --> 02:05:23.670
Cannabis increases cortisol
in some individuals

02:05:23.670 --> 02:05:25.290
and decreases cortisol in others.

02:05:25.290 --> 02:05:28.680
In general, increases in
cortisol that are ongoing

02:05:28.680 --> 02:05:31.606
are not healthy for us and so on.

02:05:31.606 --> 02:05:34.530
And then of course, there are
other effects on hormones,

02:05:34.530 --> 02:05:35.940
and I'll just briefly summarize those,

02:05:35.940 --> 02:05:39.870
that THC in particular, not
CBD, but THC in particular,

02:05:39.870 --> 02:05:41.670
is known to be strongly inhibitory

02:05:41.670 --> 02:05:43.980
for something called
gonadotropin-releasing hormone.

02:05:43.980 --> 02:05:46.020
This is a hormone that's
released from the brain,

02:05:46.020 --> 02:05:49.500
from the hypothalamus
that then feeds onto,

02:05:49.500 --> 02:05:51.690
or I should say signals
to the pituitary gland,

02:05:51.690 --> 02:05:53.010
which is also near the roof of your mouth,

02:05:53.010 --> 02:05:54.510
a lot of stuff happening
near the roof of your mouth

02:05:54.510 --> 02:05:56.100
it turns out, biologically,

02:05:56.100 --> 02:05:59.250
and reduced levels of
gonadotropin-releasing hormone

02:05:59.250 --> 02:06:00.510
caused by cannabis use,

02:06:00.510 --> 02:06:02.850
reduced levels of LH luteinizing hormone

02:06:02.850 --> 02:06:05.910
and FSH, which reduce
levels of testosterone

02:06:05.910 --> 02:06:10.910
and sperm production in males
and egg health and ovulation

02:06:12.300 --> 02:06:14.970
and menstrual function in females.

02:06:14.970 --> 02:06:17.880
Now, I'm sure there are a
number of women out there

02:06:17.880 --> 02:06:19.920
who will say they have perfectly
normal menstrual cycles

02:06:19.920 --> 02:06:22.140
despite using cannabis.

02:06:22.140 --> 02:06:24.540
I'm certainly not going to dispute that,

02:06:24.540 --> 02:06:28.170
but if you are somebody who's
trying to maximize fertility

02:06:28.170 --> 02:06:30.870
or regulate or balance hormones,

02:06:30.870 --> 02:06:34.369
marijuana and cannabis reduces GnRH.

02:06:34.369 --> 02:06:38.070
That is the gonadotropin-release
from the hypothalamus

02:06:38.070 --> 02:06:40.740
and thereby reduces luteinizing hormone

02:06:40.740 --> 02:06:42.240
and follicle stimulating hormone,

02:06:42.240 --> 02:06:43.830
which are released from the pituitary

02:06:43.830 --> 02:06:45.510
and travel in the bloodstream

02:06:45.510 --> 02:06:48.600
to support normal ovarian
function and health

02:06:48.600 --> 02:06:51.120
and normal testicular function and health

02:06:51.120 --> 02:06:53.250
in females and males, respectively.

02:06:53.250 --> 02:06:55.350
Up until now I've been
discussing the biological

02:06:55.350 --> 02:06:57.960
and psychological effects of cannabis.

02:06:57.960 --> 02:06:59.460
Now I'd like to shift our attention

02:06:59.460 --> 02:07:02.550
to some of the negative
health effects of cannabis

02:07:02.550 --> 02:07:05.490
and shine light on some of the individuals

02:07:05.490 --> 02:07:08.910
or groups out there that
need to be especially wary of

02:07:08.910 --> 02:07:11.730
and probably avoid cannabis use entirely,

02:07:11.730 --> 02:07:16.230
including ingestion of
cannabis by way of edible.

02:07:16.230 --> 02:07:17.310
And I frame things that way,

02:07:17.310 --> 02:07:20.070
because I think there is an
increasingly large number

02:07:20.070 --> 02:07:24.720
of people out there that
appreciate that smoking tobacco

02:07:24.720 --> 02:07:29.040
or smoking cannabis, vaping tobacco,

02:07:29.040 --> 02:07:31.833
yes, vaping tobacco, or vaping cannabis,

02:07:32.880 --> 02:07:36.720
each and all have negative
health consequences on the lungs

02:07:36.720 --> 02:07:39.390
and on the so-called
endothelial cells of the body,

02:07:39.390 --> 02:07:41.250
the cells that make up the vasculature,

02:07:41.250 --> 02:07:43.380
the capillaries and blood vessels.

02:07:43.380 --> 02:07:46.920
If you don't already know
this, I'll make it very clear,

02:07:46.920 --> 02:07:48.630
and I'll make it very brief.

02:07:48.630 --> 02:07:53.460
Whether or not you smoke or
vape tobacco or cannabis,

02:07:53.460 --> 02:07:57.510
you are severely impairing the
function of endothelial cells

02:07:57.510 --> 02:07:59.850
that make up the capillaries
and blood vessels

02:07:59.850 --> 02:08:01.230
of your brain and body,

02:08:01.230 --> 02:08:05.850
and that is known to decrease
cognitive capacity over time,

02:08:05.850 --> 02:08:07.650
increase probability of strokes,

02:08:07.650 --> 02:08:11.040
severely impact lung function,

02:08:11.040 --> 02:08:14.400
and also lead to things like
peripheral neuropathies.

02:08:14.400 --> 02:08:16.320
It leads to sexual dysfunction,

02:08:16.320 --> 02:08:19.710
because of lack of blood
flow to the genitals,

02:08:19.710 --> 02:08:22.530
can lead to other aspects
of reproductive damage,

02:08:22.530 --> 02:08:24.780
including to the ovaries and testes.

02:08:24.780 --> 02:08:27.270
Essentially, there is no
other way to state it,

02:08:27.270 --> 02:08:30.060
except that smoking and vaping

02:08:30.060 --> 02:08:32.520
have negative health consequences

02:08:32.520 --> 02:08:35.753
that are independent of the substances

02:08:35.753 --> 02:08:39.030
that people are trying to
get into their bloodstream

02:08:39.030 --> 02:08:40.320
by smoking or vaping.

02:08:40.320 --> 02:08:42.000
So people smoke and vape tobacco,

02:08:42.000 --> 02:08:45.660
and people smoke and vape
cannabis, and in both cases,

02:08:45.660 --> 02:08:49.050
if we just set aside the
direct effects of tobacco

02:08:49.050 --> 02:08:52.440
and the direct effects of cannabis,

02:08:52.440 --> 02:08:56.100
we can confidently say that
the process of smoking,

02:08:56.100 --> 02:08:57.810
of inhaling smoke into the lungs,

02:08:57.810 --> 02:09:02.220
and yes, also vaping,
bringing the chemicals

02:09:02.220 --> 02:09:05.730
that transport nicotine,
or in this case, cannabis,

02:09:05.730 --> 02:09:07.280
into the lungs by way of vaping

02:09:08.160 --> 02:09:11.550
are both severely detrimental
to endothelial cells.

02:09:11.550 --> 02:09:14.400
I think a few years ago, when
vaping wasn't as prominent,

02:09:14.400 --> 02:09:16.380
there was this question and this idea

02:09:16.380 --> 02:09:18.870
that maybe vaping was
going to be far healthier

02:09:18.870 --> 02:09:22.650
or at least not as bad as smoking,

02:09:22.650 --> 02:09:24.900
but now we can see a huge number

02:09:24.900 --> 02:09:26.490
of negative health effects of vaping,

02:09:26.490 --> 02:09:29.250
some of which are distinct
from the effects of smoking.

02:09:29.250 --> 02:09:32.340
So if you'd like more information
on smoking versus vaping,

02:09:32.340 --> 02:09:34.290
please see the episode
that I did on nicotine.

02:09:34.290 --> 02:09:36.120
We will also do an entire episode

02:09:36.120 --> 02:09:38.640
all about vaping in the future,

02:09:38.640 --> 02:09:41.400
but there's really no way
to slice it and dice it

02:09:41.400 --> 02:09:42.630
or candy coat it.

02:09:42.630 --> 02:09:45.600
The fact of the matter
is that smoking has clear

02:09:45.600 --> 02:09:48.630
and severe negative health
consequences, regardless of

02:09:48.630 --> 02:09:52.863
whether or not you're smoking
tobacco or cannabis, THC,

02:09:53.700 --> 02:09:57.810
and vaping has negative
health consequences,

02:09:57.810 --> 02:10:00.750
whether or not you're using
the vape to bring in nicotine

02:10:00.750 --> 02:10:04.290
or THC or some combination of THC and CBD.

02:10:04.290 --> 02:10:05.580
That's simply the way it is.

02:10:05.580 --> 02:10:07.740
With that said, now I'd
like to focus our attention

02:10:07.740 --> 02:10:10.500
on the direct effects that cannabis has,

02:10:10.500 --> 02:10:15.180
either by way of THC action
or by way of CBD action

02:10:15.180 --> 02:10:18.270
in terms of positive or
negative health effects

02:10:18.270 --> 02:10:20.850
on the brain and body, and
we're going to explore that

02:10:20.850 --> 02:10:22.920
first as a function of age.

02:10:22.920 --> 02:10:24.600
And the reason we're going to do that

02:10:24.600 --> 02:10:26.550
is related to a fact that I mentioned

02:10:26.550 --> 02:10:27.990
at the beginning of the episode,

02:10:27.990 --> 02:10:31.560
which is that the CB1
and the CB2 receptors,

02:10:31.560 --> 02:10:36.560
the two receptors for cannabis
to which THC and CBD and CBN

02:10:36.660 --> 02:10:40.800
and all other psychoactive
compounds of cannabis bind to

02:10:40.800 --> 02:10:45.360
to have their actions are
present throughout development.

02:10:45.360 --> 02:10:46.200
Believe it or not,

02:10:46.200 --> 02:10:49.380
they are present very
soon after conception,

02:10:49.380 --> 02:10:53.610
and the CB1 and CB2 receptors
actually play a critical role

02:10:53.610 --> 02:10:55.800
in the development of the fetus.

02:10:55.800 --> 02:10:58.440
Now, you might wonder why that is,

02:10:58.440 --> 02:11:00.450
because of course, the developing fetus

02:11:00.450 --> 02:11:02.610
doesn't necessarily expect to see cannabis

02:11:02.610 --> 02:11:05.880
or to be exposed to
cannabis and THC and CBD.

02:11:05.880 --> 02:11:10.650
But, as you recall, endogenous
cannabinoids are present

02:11:10.650 --> 02:11:14.370
in the adult brain and body
and endogenous cannabinoids,

02:11:14.370 --> 02:11:16.980
it turns out, are also present
in the developing fetus.

02:11:16.980 --> 02:11:19.590
In fact, endogenous
cannabinoids are present

02:11:19.590 --> 02:11:22.200
at much greater levels
in the developing fetus

02:11:22.200 --> 02:11:25.890
than they are after a child is born,

02:11:25.890 --> 02:11:27.840
and levels of endogenous cannabinoids

02:11:27.840 --> 02:11:30.270
actually go down across development.

02:11:30.270 --> 02:11:31.560
I find this really interesting.

02:11:31.560 --> 02:11:34.110
What this means is that
endogenous cannabinoids

02:11:34.110 --> 02:11:37.050
and activation of the
CB1 and CB2 receptors

02:11:37.050 --> 02:11:40.350
are an integral part
of neural development,

02:11:40.350 --> 02:11:42.240
and this is going to
become especially relevant

02:11:42.240 --> 02:11:44.160
in considering whether
or not pregnant mothers

02:11:44.160 --> 02:11:47.939
should or should not use cannabis or CBD,

02:11:47.939 --> 02:11:51.280
and it also points to some
very interesting biology

02:11:52.258 --> 02:11:54.330
in terms of how the brain develops

02:11:54.330 --> 02:11:55.680
and how the body develops.

02:11:55.680 --> 02:11:58.950
Now the development of the brain
and nervous system and body

02:11:58.950 --> 02:12:01.170
is a fascinating and vast literature,

02:12:01.170 --> 02:12:04.170
certainly far too vast to
cover in today's episode,

02:12:04.170 --> 02:12:06.900
especially at this late hour as it were,

02:12:06.900 --> 02:12:08.820
but we will have a future episode

02:12:08.820 --> 02:12:10.860
all about brain development.

02:12:10.860 --> 02:12:13.740
In terms of the effects of cannabis,

02:12:13.740 --> 02:12:17.370
it's sufficient to say
that cannabinoid receptors

02:12:17.370 --> 02:12:21.180
are present and active
in the developing fetus.

02:12:21.180 --> 02:12:23.520
They're present and active in the newborn.

02:12:23.520 --> 02:12:26.040
They're present and active in adolescence.

02:12:26.040 --> 02:12:30.120
And across that time, from
conception until adolescence,

02:12:30.120 --> 02:12:32.850
endogenous cannabinoids
are mainly responsible

02:12:32.850 --> 02:12:35.970
for the actions of those
cannabinoid receptors.

02:12:35.970 --> 02:12:37.950
During that time, the
cannabinoid receptors

02:12:37.950 --> 02:12:39.810
are having very specific effects

02:12:39.810 --> 02:12:42.330
that are distinct from
their effects later in life,

02:12:42.330 --> 02:12:44.220
and those effects can largely be explained

02:12:44.220 --> 02:12:45.540
in terms of neural development.

02:12:45.540 --> 02:12:49.200
Again, we don't have time for
an entire lecture on this now,

02:12:49.200 --> 02:12:51.630
but during development,

02:12:51.630 --> 02:12:54.330
your body was a collection
of a bunch of cells.

02:12:54.330 --> 02:12:55.980
It's actually called a blastula,

02:12:55.980 --> 02:12:58.110
which means a ball of
cells, and then those cells

02:12:58.110 --> 02:12:59.760
actually have to grow out connections

02:12:59.760 --> 02:13:01.410
and duplicate themselves,

02:13:01.410 --> 02:13:03.270
and this is a very interesting process

02:13:03.270 --> 02:13:06.150
by which neurons initially
are situated far apart,

02:13:06.150 --> 02:13:07.710
and then they grow out connections

02:13:07.710 --> 02:13:09.030
and make contacts with one another.

02:13:09.030 --> 02:13:10.320
They remove certain connections,

02:13:10.320 --> 02:13:13.290
depending on what kind of
life events you're exposed to.

02:13:13.290 --> 02:13:15.570
If you have a wonderful
event early in life

02:13:15.570 --> 02:13:17.490
or a traumatic early life,

02:13:17.490 --> 02:13:19.383
those connections change, et cetera.

02:13:20.340 --> 02:13:21.870
The important point for today's discussion

02:13:21.870 --> 02:13:25.110
is that the CB1 receptor, in particular,

02:13:25.110 --> 02:13:28.710
is expressed on every neuron
in the developing brain

02:13:28.710 --> 02:13:30.060
and has been shown to be important

02:13:30.060 --> 02:13:33.090
for every aspect of neural development,

02:13:33.090 --> 02:13:35.190
from the proliferation of cells,

02:13:35.190 --> 02:13:37.530
meaning getting enough
cells to create a brain,

02:13:37.530 --> 02:13:40.050
to the outgrowth of the so-called axons,

02:13:40.050 --> 02:13:43.260
the little wires that connect
up neurons with one another,

02:13:43.260 --> 02:13:45.780
to the steering, the direction

02:13:45.780 --> 02:13:50.640
which those axons go in
development, which is essential,

02:13:50.640 --> 02:13:54.240
and even so far as to
explain the connections

02:13:54.240 --> 02:13:56.730
that form between neurons,
the so-called synapses,

02:13:56.730 --> 02:13:58.710
and then how those synapses work.

02:13:58.710 --> 02:14:02.970
So the basic statement here is
that endogenous cannabinoids

02:14:02.970 --> 02:14:05.130
and CB1 receptor activation are critical

02:14:05.130 --> 02:14:08.588
for every aspect of brain
wiring and development.

02:14:08.588 --> 02:14:11.430
With that in mind, the
statement I'm about to make

02:14:11.430 --> 02:14:14.190
is absolutely terrifying, at least to me,

02:14:14.190 --> 02:14:17.010
and frankly, it should be
terrifying to you as well.

02:14:17.010 --> 02:14:21.750
And the statement is,
the current statistics

02:14:21.750 --> 02:14:26.750
on cannabis use in pregnant
mothers is absolutely shocking.

02:14:27.600 --> 02:14:29.870
The most recent survey of pregnant mothers

02:14:29.870 --> 02:14:33.848
in the United States
show that 15%, one-five,

02:14:33.848 --> 02:14:37.650
15% of pregnant mothers
report using cannabis

02:14:37.650 --> 02:14:40.500
in some form or another, either smoking it

02:14:40.500 --> 02:14:43.890
or more likely ingestion of in edible,

02:14:43.890 --> 02:14:45.690
because they are aware
of the negative effects

02:14:45.690 --> 02:14:49.080
of smoking on the developing
fetus, ingestion of an edible

02:14:49.080 --> 02:14:53.580
to increase THC and/or
CBD during pregnancy,

02:14:53.580 --> 02:14:55.380
which to me, I have to say,

02:14:55.380 --> 02:15:00.380
as a developmental neurobiologist
is, frankly, it's scary.

02:15:00.540 --> 02:15:03.600
It's absolutely scary,
because that CB1 receptor

02:15:03.600 --> 02:15:06.360
is not just a minor player
in neural development.

02:15:06.360 --> 02:15:09.480
It is absolutely central
to every critical aspect

02:15:09.480 --> 02:15:11.550
of brain wiring and development.

02:15:11.550 --> 02:15:12.990
Now, the long-term implications

02:15:12.990 --> 02:15:14.910
or even the short-term
implications of this,

02:15:14.910 --> 02:15:18.930
15% of mothers self-reporting
the use of cannabis

02:15:18.930 --> 02:15:22.200
at some point during
pregnancy are not yet known.

02:15:22.200 --> 02:15:25.740
This is, as we would say, an
experiment that's ongoing.

02:15:25.740 --> 02:15:28.590
But I'd be remiss if I
didn't point out these data

02:15:28.590 --> 02:15:31.440
and just implore you,
please, please, please,

02:15:31.440 --> 02:15:33.330
if you are pregnant or
considering getting pregnant,

02:15:33.330 --> 02:15:35.490
you're a cannabis user, whether
or not you're using edibles,

02:15:35.490 --> 02:15:40.490
if you're a CBD user, please
do whatever is necessary

02:15:41.310 --> 02:15:45.150
to not ingest cannabis or smoke cannabis

02:15:45.150 --> 02:15:47.790
or ingest CBD during pregnancy.

02:15:47.790 --> 02:15:50.130
Now, there may be certain
clinical indications

02:15:50.130 --> 02:15:53.340
by which your physician and your OB/GYN,

02:15:53.340 --> 02:15:55.560
and the pediatrician that will eventually

02:15:55.560 --> 02:16:00.560
be the pediatrician for your
child will prescribe CBD,

02:16:00.750 --> 02:16:02.460
although it's hard to
imagine what those are.

02:16:02.460 --> 02:16:07.167
I contacted a number of different
pediatricians and OB/GYNs

02:16:07.167 --> 02:16:10.500
and not a single one said
they would ever suggest,

02:16:10.500 --> 02:16:12.990
and in fact would strongly
discourage their patients

02:16:12.990 --> 02:16:16.020
from using cannabis during pregnancy.

02:16:16.020 --> 02:16:20.940
But I think that the advent
of edible forms of cannabis,

02:16:20.940 --> 02:16:25.830
and the combination of THC
and CBD in certain products,

02:16:25.830 --> 02:16:28.800
and the fact that most
people view CBD as safer,

02:16:28.800 --> 02:16:32.850
because it does not include,
or does not have, I should say,

02:16:32.850 --> 02:16:35.250
the psychoactive effects that THC does,

02:16:35.250 --> 02:16:39.600
has led to a situation where you have 15%

02:16:39.600 --> 02:16:41.370
of pregnant mothers using cannabis

02:16:41.370 --> 02:16:43.140
at some point during pregnancy

02:16:43.140 --> 02:16:45.420
and maybe even frequently
throughout pregnancy,

02:16:45.420 --> 02:16:47.310
and the effects on the developing fetus

02:16:47.310 --> 02:16:49.080
are completely unknown.

02:16:49.080 --> 02:16:54.080
But recall that cannabis
and THC and CBD outcompete,

02:16:54.840 --> 02:16:56.910
meaning they park in the receptor

02:16:56.910 --> 02:16:58.650
for endogenous cannabinoids

02:16:58.650 --> 02:17:01.200
and prevent endogenous cannabinoids

02:17:01.200 --> 02:17:04.530
from having their normal
level and pattern of action,

02:17:04.530 --> 02:17:06.120
so this is absolutely critical.

02:17:06.120 --> 02:17:08.610
I cannot encourage you enough,
or rather, I should say,

02:17:08.610 --> 02:17:12.780
I cannot discourage
enough the use of cannabis

02:17:12.780 --> 02:17:16.034
and any related compounds in cannabis,

02:17:16.034 --> 02:17:18.360
edible or smoked, certainly not smoked,

02:17:18.360 --> 02:17:21.180
but even edible during pregnancy,

02:17:21.180 --> 02:17:25.470
and certainly in breastfeeding,
lactating mothers,

02:17:25.470 --> 02:17:26.430
the same is also true.

02:17:26.430 --> 02:17:30.030
Recall that cannabis and THC and CBD

02:17:30.030 --> 02:17:31.948
are incredibly lipophilic.

02:17:31.948 --> 02:17:33.810
They're fat soluble,

02:17:33.810 --> 02:17:35.850
and they get into cells very readily,

02:17:35.850 --> 02:17:37.290
and they cross the blood brain barrier.

02:17:37.290 --> 02:17:39.750
They cross the blood placental barrier.

02:17:39.750 --> 02:17:41.610
So when I encountered this statistic,

02:17:41.610 --> 02:17:43.530
I had to kind of wipe my eyes a few times.

02:17:43.530 --> 02:17:46.860
I could not believe it, and
yet I crosschecked that number

02:17:46.860 --> 02:17:47.970
with a few other studies.

02:17:47.970 --> 02:17:49.770
A few others have come
in a little bit lower

02:17:49.770 --> 02:17:51.747
at somewhere like 13% to 14%,

02:17:51.747 --> 02:17:53.340
and a few have come in
a little bit higher,

02:17:53.340 --> 02:17:57.420
but the average of 15% is
both striking and shocking,

02:17:57.420 --> 02:17:59.400
so I don't know how to make
the message more clear.

02:17:59.400 --> 02:18:00.600
I hope that is clear.

02:18:00.600 --> 02:18:04.778
Please do not use any cannabis, THC

02:18:04.778 --> 02:18:09.360
or related things including
CBD, smoked or edible

02:18:09.360 --> 02:18:12.900
if you're pregnant, lactating, et cetera.

02:18:12.900 --> 02:18:15.270
Now we are at the point
where we need to consider

02:18:15.270 --> 02:18:17.160
some of the negative
health effects of cannabis

02:18:17.160 --> 02:18:19.590
that have been well documented
in peer-reviewed studies.

02:18:19.590 --> 02:18:20.940
And before I do this,

02:18:20.940 --> 02:18:22.680
I want to return to a
point that I made earlier,

02:18:22.680 --> 02:18:25.410
which is that nothing I am about to say

02:18:25.410 --> 02:18:28.140
relates directly to issues of legality.

02:18:28.140 --> 02:18:30.480
If we consider alcohol, for instance,

02:18:30.480 --> 02:18:32.940
alcohol is legal in
most areas of the world.

02:18:32.940 --> 02:18:34.830
It's certainly legal in the US,

02:18:34.830 --> 02:18:37.980
and yet there's an age limit for its use.

02:18:37.980 --> 02:18:39.960
Typically, it's not available to people

02:18:39.960 --> 02:18:41.700
until they're 21 or older.

02:18:41.700 --> 02:18:43.260
It's not to say that
certain people don't use it

02:18:43.260 --> 02:18:46.980
before age 21, but it's not legal.

02:18:46.980 --> 02:18:50.130
It is illegal to buy or possess alcohol,

02:18:50.130 --> 02:18:53.460
consume alcohol before age 21,
and I think with good reason,

02:18:53.460 --> 02:18:56.010
because the brain is still developing.

02:18:56.010 --> 02:19:01.010
Likewise, we can have a informed
discussion about cannabis

02:19:01.265 --> 02:19:03.179
and its various components

02:19:03.179 --> 02:19:06.750
that can fully acknowledge the reality,

02:19:06.750 --> 02:19:11.700
which is that one of the major
harms of cannabis in the past

02:19:11.700 --> 02:19:15.498
has been the legal ramifications
of cannabis being illegal.

02:19:15.498 --> 02:19:19.020
That's a statement that is
no longer controversial,

02:19:19.020 --> 02:19:20.310
and this is not a discussion

02:19:20.310 --> 02:19:22.320
about legalization or non legalization.

02:19:22.320 --> 02:19:24.270
If you look to the scientific literature,

02:19:24.270 --> 02:19:26.730
the epidemiological literature,

02:19:26.730 --> 02:19:29.340
there are wonderful data
out of Carleton University

02:19:29.340 --> 02:19:32.022
and elsewhere in Canada showing that

02:19:32.022 --> 02:19:37.022
many of the negative
effects of marijuana and THC

02:19:37.800 --> 02:19:41.130
are due to the criminal
justice system, itself,

02:19:41.130 --> 02:19:45.630
that is the creation of
illicit drug businesses,

02:19:45.630 --> 02:19:47.820
the creation of organized crime,

02:19:47.820 --> 02:19:50.250
the creation of a number
of different features

02:19:50.250 --> 02:19:54.240
related to the illegality of cannabis.

02:19:54.240 --> 02:19:57.330
And again, this isn't the
topic of today's episode,

02:19:57.330 --> 02:19:59.250
but that should be acknowledged.

02:19:59.250 --> 02:20:01.350
And at the same time,
we need to acknowledge

02:20:01.350 --> 02:20:05.700
that when a compound, a drug
or whatever you want to call it

02:20:05.700 --> 02:20:09.000
becomes legal, there's
a tendency to assume

02:20:09.000 --> 02:20:11.250
that it's safe and safe for everybody.

02:20:11.250 --> 02:20:15.870
And with respect to cannabis
and THC and perhaps even CBD,

02:20:15.870 --> 02:20:20.820
but certainly for THC and
cannabis that's smoked or vaped

02:20:20.820 --> 02:20:24.780
or consumed in edible form,
that is simply not the case.

02:20:24.780 --> 02:20:28.470
There are clear data pointing
to negative health effects

02:20:28.470 --> 02:20:31.830
of cannabis use and THC use,
which again, is not to say

02:20:31.830 --> 02:20:33.870
that there are not positive effects

02:20:33.870 --> 02:20:36.060
on mood, anxiety, pain relief, et cetera.

02:20:36.060 --> 02:20:37.530
Those are out there and they exist,

02:20:37.530 --> 02:20:39.240
and we will mention
some of those of course,

02:20:39.240 --> 02:20:40.830
and we've talked about some of those,

02:20:40.830 --> 02:20:42.930
creativity for instance, et cetera.

02:20:42.930 --> 02:20:46.320
But if we do not acknowledge
the negative health effects

02:20:46.320 --> 02:20:48.330
that are documented in the literature,

02:20:48.330 --> 02:20:51.570
then we are overlooking
some very important data,

02:20:51.570 --> 02:20:54.990
especially as it relates to
the development of psychosis

02:20:54.990 --> 02:20:57.000
in certain individuals.

02:20:57.000 --> 02:21:01.230
So, with that said, there
are very strong data,

02:21:01.230 --> 02:21:03.300
and I will provide links
to these resources,

02:21:03.300 --> 02:21:04.860
pointing to the fact that for people

02:21:04.860 --> 02:21:07.230
who are chronic users of cannabis,

02:21:07.230 --> 02:21:09.750
that is using it twice a week or more,

02:21:09.750 --> 02:21:13.530
that over time, their levels
of anxiety actually increase,

02:21:13.530 --> 02:21:15.600
and this is true even for individuals

02:21:15.600 --> 02:21:17.220
that are using strains of cannabis,

02:21:17.220 --> 02:21:21.510
that while under the influence
of cannabis, reduce anxiety.

02:21:21.510 --> 02:21:26.106
Over time, meaning over the
course of 12 or more months,

02:21:26.106 --> 02:21:31.106
there is a well-documented
effect of the anxiety relief

02:21:31.830 --> 02:21:35.250
that cannabis and THC initially brought,

02:21:35.250 --> 02:21:36.870
being less and less potent.

02:21:36.870 --> 02:21:41.340
That is people need to smoke
more of it or ingest more THC

02:21:41.340 --> 02:21:44.460
in order to achieve the same
level of anxiety relief,

02:21:44.460 --> 02:21:48.030
and in some cases, a
switch from anxiety relief

02:21:48.030 --> 02:21:50.130
to increase in anxiety,

02:21:50.130 --> 02:21:52.440
and again, that's increase in anxiety,

02:21:52.440 --> 02:21:55.920
not just when the drug
is not being consumed,

02:21:55.920 --> 02:21:58.650
but also while under the
influence of the drug.

02:21:58.650 --> 02:21:59.940
Why would that be?

02:21:59.940 --> 02:22:03.960
We have to go back to our
understanding of the CB1 receptor

02:22:03.960 --> 02:22:08.253
and the potency with which THC
binds to that CB1 receptor.

02:22:09.300 --> 02:22:12.693
When THC is brought into the
system over and over again,

02:22:13.680 --> 02:22:16.020
meaning twice a week or more,

02:22:16.020 --> 02:22:19.350
the binding of THC, that CB1 receptor,

02:22:19.350 --> 02:22:23.520
eventually causes a
habituation or attenuation

02:22:23.520 --> 02:22:26.790
of the entire process
of binding the receptor

02:22:26.790 --> 02:22:28.710
and creating the psychoactive effects.

02:22:28.710 --> 02:22:30.780
So initially it creates anxiety relief,

02:22:30.780 --> 02:22:35.130
but over time, the affinity for
the receptor doesn't change,

02:22:35.130 --> 02:22:36.630
meaning it can still park in that slot

02:22:36.630 --> 02:22:39.390
with a lot of affinity, a lot of strength,

02:22:39.390 --> 02:22:43.140
but there are fewer receptors available,

02:22:43.140 --> 02:22:46.560
and then the signaling that's
downstream of those receptors

02:22:46.560 --> 02:22:48.750
becomes less and less robust.

02:22:48.750 --> 02:22:50.850
Now this is a topic we didn't get into

02:22:50.850 --> 02:22:53.580
in too much detail today,
because I didn't want to include

02:22:53.580 --> 02:22:56.910
even more biological
detail, but the CB1 receptor

02:22:56.910 --> 02:22:59.842
is a so-called G protein-coupled receptor.

02:22:59.842 --> 02:23:02.700
That's a mouthful, but a
G protein-coupled receptor

02:23:02.700 --> 02:23:04.830
basically is like a bucket brigade.

02:23:04.830 --> 02:23:06.660
So while some receptors
in the brain and body

02:23:06.660 --> 02:23:09.990
are such that when something,
a chemical binds to them,

02:23:09.990 --> 02:23:11.700
that receptor has a direct action,

02:23:11.700 --> 02:23:14.100
like it opens and allows
stuff to rush into the cell,

02:23:14.100 --> 02:23:16.320
increases the excitability of the cell,

02:23:16.320 --> 02:23:18.060
so called fast effects,

02:23:18.060 --> 02:23:20.400
these G protein-coupled receptors,

02:23:20.400 --> 02:23:23.610
and CB1 is a G protein-coupled receptor,

02:23:23.610 --> 02:23:25.050
they are more like a bucket brigade

02:23:25.050 --> 02:23:28.350
where they kick off a
process through one molecule

02:23:28.350 --> 02:23:30.780
that then is handed
off to another molecule

02:23:30.780 --> 02:23:32.880
that then is handed off
to another molecule.

02:23:32.880 --> 02:23:36.210
It's a long chain or cascade of events.

02:23:36.210 --> 02:23:38.700
Those long chains or cascades of events

02:23:38.700 --> 02:23:42.450
have a lot of opportunity for
regulation, for adjustment.

02:23:42.450 --> 02:23:44.130
Receptor systems in the brain and body,

02:23:44.130 --> 02:23:46.440
especially receptor systems
like the cannabinoid system

02:23:46.440 --> 02:23:49.620
that are used to being
tickled, not punched,

02:23:49.620 --> 02:23:51.390
tickled by endogenous cannabinoids,

02:23:51.390 --> 02:23:53.389
every once in a while
some binds, has an effect,

02:23:53.389 --> 02:23:58.140
but certainly not bound
with incredible potency

02:23:58.140 --> 02:23:59.610
and over and over again as they are

02:23:59.610 --> 02:24:01.560
when THC is coming into the system.

02:24:01.560 --> 02:24:03.180
Well those systems eventually, over time,

02:24:03.180 --> 02:24:06.237
they adjust themselves so that the body

02:24:06.237 --> 02:24:09.390
and those cells can achieve
so-called homeostasis.

02:24:09.390 --> 02:24:13.770
So when people are using
THC more than twice a week,

02:24:13.770 --> 02:24:16.290
what ends up happening is those
G protein-coupled receptors

02:24:16.290 --> 02:24:18.030
in the downstream signaling mechanisms

02:24:18.030 --> 02:24:19.500
start to adjust themselves,

02:24:19.500 --> 02:24:23.340
and it requires more and more drug,

02:24:23.340 --> 02:24:26.043
so either higher dosages
or more frequent use,

02:24:27.000 --> 02:24:29.430
and a lot of the positive effects,

02:24:29.430 --> 02:24:30.930
the so-called decrease in anxiety,

02:24:30.930 --> 02:24:32.700
increased focus, increased creativity,

02:24:32.700 --> 02:24:35.220
some of that starts to wane,
it starts to dissipate,

02:24:35.220 --> 02:24:37.860
and people wonder why they
have to use so much cannabis

02:24:37.860 --> 02:24:40.470
just to achieve a fraction of the effect

02:24:40.470 --> 02:24:44.580
that they used to be able to
achieve with even a lower dose.

02:24:44.580 --> 02:24:46.710
So anxiety is getting worse over time,

02:24:46.710 --> 02:24:49.260
and that's anxiety during the drug use

02:24:49.260 --> 02:24:51.180
and outside of the drug use.

02:24:51.180 --> 02:24:54.990
Some people work around that
or try to work around that

02:24:54.990 --> 02:24:57.960
by using varying strains of cannabis

02:24:57.960 --> 02:25:01.650
or changing the pattern of
delivery from smoking to vaping

02:25:01.650 --> 02:25:04.770
or from vaping to edible and
from edible to transdermal.

02:25:04.770 --> 02:25:06.750
Anyway, they go through
a lot of gymnastics

02:25:06.750 --> 02:25:09.900
and writhing and seeking,

02:25:09.900 --> 02:25:12.870
but nonetheless, anxiety
increases over time.

02:25:12.870 --> 02:25:17.460
Also, it's very clear that
depression increases over time

02:25:17.460 --> 02:25:19.650
and especially, this is surprising to me,

02:25:19.650 --> 02:25:22.770
but especially for individuals
that were not depressed

02:25:22.770 --> 02:25:24.300
at the the outset of their use.

02:25:24.300 --> 02:25:25.890
In other words, they
didn't start using cannabis

02:25:25.890 --> 02:25:27.510
because they were depressed,

02:25:27.510 --> 02:25:30.690
but rather the depression starts to emerge

02:25:30.690 --> 02:25:34.350
as a consequence of the
cannabis and THC use.

02:25:34.350 --> 02:25:37.800
So that's serious, in fact, we now know,

02:25:37.800 --> 02:25:40.260
based on really solid
epidemiological evidence,

02:25:40.260 --> 02:25:43.050
that depression is not a strong predictor

02:25:43.050 --> 02:25:45.480
of seeking out cannabis.

02:25:45.480 --> 02:25:48.120
It doesn't drive terribly many people

02:25:48.120 --> 02:25:50.040
to seek out cannabis use,

02:25:50.040 --> 02:25:54.480
but cannabis use, itself, makes
people four times likelier

02:25:54.480 --> 02:25:57.630
to develop a chronic major depression.

02:25:57.630 --> 02:26:01.650
So anxiety is increasing,
depression is increasing,

02:26:01.650 --> 02:26:05.010
and this turns out to
be especially relevant

02:26:05.010 --> 02:26:06.840
and important to young people.

02:26:06.840 --> 02:26:07.770
Why do I say that?

02:26:07.770 --> 02:26:09.360
Well, if you look at the data,

02:26:09.360 --> 02:26:11.520
and again, I think some
of the strongest data

02:26:11.520 --> 02:26:13.920
are data to come out
of the Canadian system.

02:26:13.920 --> 02:26:15.960
They've done some really
beautiful controlled studies.

02:26:15.960 --> 02:26:18.420
I really hope to invite some of the people

02:26:18.420 --> 02:26:20.700
who arranged and ran those studies

02:26:20.700 --> 02:26:22.860
as guests onto the Huberman Lab Podcast.

02:26:22.860 --> 02:26:25.590
But if you look at the data out of Canada,

02:26:25.590 --> 02:26:26.610
or you look at some of the data

02:26:26.610 --> 02:26:28.410
out of Northern Europe and the US,

02:26:28.410 --> 02:26:31.170
what you find is that the probability

02:26:31.170 --> 02:26:33.327
that somebody will use cannabis

02:26:33.327 --> 02:26:36.000
and then go on to use it chronically,

02:26:36.000 --> 02:26:37.770
correlates very strongly with age.

02:26:37.770 --> 02:26:40.860
So for instance, some of the
highest degree of cannabis use

02:26:40.860 --> 02:26:44.296
is among individuals 16 to 24 years old.

02:26:44.296 --> 02:26:48.420
In fact, in individuals
who are 16 to 24 years old,

02:26:48.420 --> 02:26:52.680
and in particular, in students
and people who are working,

02:26:52.680 --> 02:26:56.190
surprising, more than in
unemployed populations,

02:26:56.190 --> 02:26:59.250
being young, 16 to 24, at
least to me that's young,

02:26:59.250 --> 02:27:03.720
and being a student or
working doubles the likelihood

02:27:03.720 --> 02:27:06.450
that somebody is going to use
cannabis on a regular basis,

02:27:06.450 --> 02:27:08.640
twice or more per week.

02:27:08.640 --> 02:27:11.160
The typical age of initiating
cannabis use nowadays

02:27:11.160 --> 02:27:14.790
is about 19 years old, so 18.7,

02:27:14.790 --> 02:27:18.420
and about 20% of people
in that age bracket,

02:27:18.420 --> 02:27:22.020
of 16 to 24 years old,
are using cannabis daily,

02:27:22.020 --> 02:27:24.900
either by vaping, by smoking or by edible.

02:27:24.900 --> 02:27:27.180
That's an enormous number,
at least by my read.

02:27:27.180 --> 02:27:28.380
It's an enormous number,

02:27:28.380 --> 02:27:30.693
and here's why it's of
really serious concern.

02:27:32.100 --> 02:27:35.380
During the ages of 16 to 24,

02:27:35.380 --> 02:27:38.820
the cannabinoid receptors
are still available.

02:27:38.820 --> 02:27:40.800
They are not being as strongly driven

02:27:40.800 --> 02:27:42.660
by endogenous cannabinoids,

02:27:42.660 --> 02:27:47.660
but by ingestion of THC and/or CBD,

02:27:47.940 --> 02:27:49.380
there are downstream effects

02:27:49.380 --> 02:27:51.360
on the signaling within those cells

02:27:51.360 --> 02:27:56.360
that all the data point
to creating a much,

02:27:56.550 --> 02:28:00.720
much higher likelihood of
developing major depression,

02:28:00.720 --> 02:28:04.680
severe anxiety or psychosis at later ages.

02:28:04.680 --> 02:28:06.750
So to be very clear, cannabis use,

02:28:06.750 --> 02:28:11.370
between the ages of 16 to
24, in both males and females

02:28:11.370 --> 02:28:14.520
is increasing anxiety,
increasing depression

02:28:14.520 --> 02:28:17.490
in the immediate years and
within the one year's time or so,

02:28:17.490 --> 02:28:21.180
so much so that people are
using the cannabis ongoing

02:28:21.180 --> 02:28:24.120
in an attempt to reduce that anxiety

02:28:24.120 --> 02:28:25.620
and reduce that depression,

02:28:25.620 --> 02:28:28.170
but in addition to that, the cannabis use,

02:28:28.170 --> 02:28:31.380
and because of the signaling
mechanisms involved

02:28:31.380 --> 02:28:33.870
are predisposing those individuals

02:28:33.870 --> 02:28:35.643
to psychosis later in life.

02:28:36.630 --> 02:28:38.970
If you look at individuals
who start using cannabis,

02:28:38.970 --> 02:28:43.710
even younger, age 14,
or even as young as 12,

02:28:43.710 --> 02:28:46.080
the probability of
psychosis later in life,

02:28:46.080 --> 02:28:49.710
in particular schizophrenic
or schizophrenic-like episodes

02:28:49.710 --> 02:28:54.270
more than doubles, so this
is of really serious concern,

02:28:54.270 --> 02:28:56.520
and this is completely aside

02:28:56.520 --> 02:28:58.740
from any so-called positive effects

02:28:58.740 --> 02:29:01.350
or beneficial effects of cannabis

02:29:01.350 --> 02:29:04.020
that people might derive from
occasional use as adults,

02:29:04.020 --> 02:29:06.030
meaning people older than 25.

02:29:06.030 --> 02:29:07.770
So for the person who's older than 25,

02:29:07.770 --> 02:29:10.020
who eats an edible every once in a while,

02:29:10.020 --> 02:29:12.210
or who smokes cannabis
every once in a while,

02:29:12.210 --> 02:29:14.100
and people love to make the argument,

02:29:14.100 --> 02:29:15.810
it's not as bad as alcohol,

02:29:15.810 --> 02:29:17.550
which frankly, is a terrible argument,

02:29:17.550 --> 02:29:19.104
because if you saw our episode on alcohol,

02:29:19.104 --> 02:29:20.512
[laughs] alcohol is pretty bad,

02:29:20.512 --> 02:29:23.730
but even so, it's just
not a good argument.

02:29:23.730 --> 02:29:25.170
It's saying that something is good,

02:29:25.170 --> 02:29:27.090
because it's not as bad as something else

02:29:27.090 --> 02:29:29.310
is simply just not a
good or valid argument,

02:29:29.310 --> 02:29:31.060
at least not biologically speaking.

02:29:32.194 --> 02:29:35.250
The use of cannabis in young populations

02:29:35.250 --> 02:29:38.700
is very strongly predisposing
people to psychotic episodes,

02:29:38.700 --> 02:29:42.720
and we know the mechanism
by which this occurs.

02:29:42.720 --> 02:29:46.890
This occurs by a thinning of
the so-called gray matter,

02:29:46.890 --> 02:29:50.310
and it's called gray matter
because with neurons,

02:29:50.310 --> 02:29:52.740
nerve cells, they have
a so-called cell body,

02:29:52.740 --> 02:29:54.690
that's the part that contains the DNA

02:29:54.690 --> 02:29:57.150
and manufacturers all the
neurotransmitters, et cetera.

02:29:57.150 --> 02:30:00.120
And those are shipped out to
the other parts of the neuron

02:30:00.120 --> 02:30:02.460
that include the axon,
the wires between axons,

02:30:02.460 --> 02:30:05.130
and those axons under the microscope,

02:30:05.130 --> 02:30:07.020
because they have a lot of
fatty tissue around them,

02:30:07.020 --> 02:30:08.310
and this is healthy fatty tissue

02:30:08.310 --> 02:30:10.350
that allows electrical
transmission to be fast,

02:30:10.350 --> 02:30:12.840
that fatty tissue, those
portions of the cells,

02:30:12.840 --> 02:30:13.950
are called white matter,

02:30:13.950 --> 02:30:15.600
so you have gray matter and white matter.

02:30:15.600 --> 02:30:17.400
Gray matter are the so-called cell bodies

02:30:17.400 --> 02:30:19.710
where the DNA and all the
stuff is manufactured.

02:30:19.710 --> 02:30:23.334
White matter or the axons or the wires

02:30:23.334 --> 02:30:26.340
through which all the key
components are shipped out

02:30:26.340 --> 02:30:27.783
to the synapse, et cetera.

02:30:29.190 --> 02:30:31.410
Wonderful data and I do say wonderful,

02:30:31.410 --> 02:30:33.780
because it is part of a
large scale consortium,

02:30:33.780 --> 02:30:35.100
and we will provide a link to the paper.

02:30:35.100 --> 02:30:37.740
This was published in
"Translational Psychiatry"

02:30:37.740 --> 02:30:40.950
just this year, point to the fact

02:30:40.950 --> 02:30:45.240
that adolescent cannabis
use accelerates the thinning

02:30:45.240 --> 02:30:47.940
of the prefrontal cortex and
the gray matter in particular.

02:30:47.940 --> 02:30:51.600
So what this means is, while
during normal development,

02:30:51.600 --> 02:30:53.880
the gray matter, the prefrontal cortex

02:30:53.880 --> 02:30:56.280
and all the cells there
are indeed intended,

02:30:56.280 --> 02:30:58.470
it's a normal process for it to thicken

02:30:58.470 --> 02:31:02.580
and then thin a little bit
as connections are adjusted

02:31:02.580 --> 02:31:04.650
and people learn and mature and grow up,

02:31:04.650 --> 02:31:07.230
this is part of the normal
healthy maturation process,

02:31:07.230 --> 02:31:09.840
independent of cannabis use.

02:31:09.840 --> 02:31:13.860
When kids, 'cause these
really are kids, use cannabis,

02:31:13.860 --> 02:31:15.900
and it doesn't matter the
mode of cannabis delivery,

02:31:15.900 --> 02:31:18.390
whether or not it's vaping
or smoking or edible,

02:31:18.390 --> 02:31:21.570
that gray matter thins at
a much, much greater rate.

02:31:21.570 --> 02:31:23.250
And the reason I like this paper published

02:31:23.250 --> 02:31:26.430
in "Translational
Psychiatry" this year so much

02:31:26.430 --> 02:31:29.550
is that they link the
amount of cannabis use,

02:31:29.550 --> 02:31:32.790
heavy, moderate, light or no cannabis use,

02:31:32.790 --> 02:31:35.520
to the rate of prefrontal
cortical thinning,

02:31:35.520 --> 02:31:38.520
and it's absolutely clear from these data

02:31:38.520 --> 02:31:41.550
that the more often young people,

02:31:41.550 --> 02:31:44.760
meaning individuals between
the age of 14 and 25,

02:31:44.760 --> 02:31:48.840
the more often they consume
or smoke or vape cannabis,

02:31:48.840 --> 02:31:52.290
the faster and the more extreme
that cortical thinning is.

02:31:52.290 --> 02:31:53.670
And the cortical thinning is occurring

02:31:53.670 --> 02:31:55.110
in exactly the area of the brain

02:31:55.110 --> 02:31:58.950
that's involved in planning,
in control over one's emotions,

02:31:58.950 --> 02:32:03.180
in reflexes, in organizing one's life

02:32:03.180 --> 02:32:04.020
in a number of different ways,

02:32:04.020 --> 02:32:06.690
anywhere from cleaning
one's room, literally,

02:32:06.690 --> 02:32:08.130
knowing what goes where,

02:32:08.130 --> 02:32:10.740
to making plans that
extend out through the day,

02:32:10.740 --> 02:32:12.180
through the week, through a year,

02:32:12.180 --> 02:32:14.070
essentially becoming a
functional human being

02:32:14.070 --> 02:32:16.020
involves using your prefrontal cortex

02:32:16.020 --> 02:32:17.550
in a variety of different contexts

02:32:17.550 --> 02:32:20.520
and different time domains,
the time domain of an hour,

02:32:20.520 --> 02:32:21.870
the time domain of a day.

02:32:21.870 --> 02:32:25.050
Making plans and being
able to execute plans

02:32:25.050 --> 02:32:27.000
is fundamental to being
a healthy human being,

02:32:27.000 --> 02:32:29.100
and it's absolutely clear from these data

02:32:29.100 --> 02:32:32.820
that the more cannabis one uses,

02:32:32.820 --> 02:32:35.430
the more impaired those
neural circuits are.

02:32:35.430 --> 02:32:38.850
There's simply no other
way to to view these data.

02:32:38.850 --> 02:32:42.480
In fact, so much so, that even
small amounts of cannabis use

02:32:42.480 --> 02:32:44.610
are associated with rates
of cortical thinning

02:32:44.610 --> 02:32:47.670
and degrees of cortical thinning
that are really detrimental

02:32:47.670 --> 02:32:51.240
and concerning for normal
cognitive processes.

02:32:51.240 --> 02:32:54.210
If you were somebody who smoked marijuana

02:32:54.210 --> 02:32:57.180
or consumed cannabis
in any form or another

02:32:57.180 --> 02:32:59.820
during adolescence, does that mean

02:32:59.820 --> 02:33:02.040
that your prefrontal cortex
can never be rescued,

02:33:02.040 --> 02:33:03.810
that it can't come back?

02:33:03.810 --> 02:33:05.310
Well, the short answer is,

02:33:05.310 --> 02:33:07.410
it probably can be rescued to some degree.

02:33:07.410 --> 02:33:10.980
It will depend on how much
cannabis you were using

02:33:10.980 --> 02:33:14.940
and how often and what strains
of cannabis, et cetera.

02:33:14.940 --> 02:33:16.680
There's really no traveling back in time,

02:33:16.680 --> 02:33:18.480
as my graduate advisor used to say.

02:33:18.480 --> 02:33:20.070
Time machines are broken.

02:33:20.070 --> 02:33:21.510
At least for now, we
don't have time machines.

02:33:21.510 --> 02:33:24.750
So all you can really
do is try and emphasize,

02:33:24.750 --> 02:33:28.049
first of all, quitting
cannabis in any form

02:33:28.049 --> 02:33:33.049
and focusing on behaviors that
emphasize endothelial cell

02:33:33.630 --> 02:33:35.730
blood flow health to the brain,

02:33:35.730 --> 02:33:38.760
so that would be cardiovascular exercise,

02:33:38.760 --> 02:33:41.520
adequate nutrition, not smoking nicotine,

02:33:41.520 --> 02:33:43.890
and there are a number of
other things that one can do.

02:33:43.890 --> 02:33:45.060
We will do an entire episode

02:33:45.060 --> 02:33:48.990
all about trying to reverse
the effects of cannabis

02:33:48.990 --> 02:33:50.850
and other drug use during adolescence.

02:33:50.850 --> 02:33:52.980
We don't have time to do a
deep dive on that right now,

02:33:52.980 --> 02:33:56.039
but all the things that
standardize and promote health,

02:33:56.039 --> 02:33:59.313
adequate sleep, good social connection,

02:33:59.313 --> 02:34:03.510
regular cardiovascular and
weight training exercise,

02:34:03.510 --> 02:34:05.850
healthy nutrition, and what
that represents to you,

02:34:05.850 --> 02:34:08.100
healthy metabolic function
and weight, et cetera,

02:34:08.100 --> 02:34:11.190
those are all going to
facilitate some recovery

02:34:11.190 --> 02:34:13.050
of brain function, in particular,

02:34:13.050 --> 02:34:14.700
prefrontal cortical function,

02:34:14.700 --> 02:34:16.140
by way of all the positive effects

02:34:16.140 --> 02:34:19.200
that those behaviors and choices have.

02:34:19.200 --> 02:34:22.290
But with that said, if
you are in the age bracket

02:34:22.290 --> 02:34:23.310
that I've been referring to,

02:34:23.310 --> 02:34:25.740
this 14 to 25 year old age bracket,

02:34:25.740 --> 02:34:30.630
and you are a occasional even
or chronic cannabis user,

02:34:30.630 --> 02:34:34.080
you should be very, very
careful and concerned

02:34:34.080 --> 02:34:35.460
about the long-term effects

02:34:35.460 --> 02:34:36.990
that that could potentially have.

02:34:36.990 --> 02:34:39.420
That statement is bolstered
by another statistic,

02:34:39.420 --> 02:34:42.843
which is that unlike a lot of other drugs,

02:34:43.920 --> 02:34:47.640
the rate of cannabis
use is strongly related

02:34:47.640 --> 02:34:52.242
to how dangerous people
perceive cannabis to be.

02:34:52.242 --> 02:34:54.780
Now, that might seem
obvious, on the one hand,

02:34:54.780 --> 02:34:57.300
if you think something
is very, very dangerous,

02:34:57.300 --> 02:34:58.890
you would expect that the probability

02:34:58.890 --> 02:35:01.050
that somebody would use it
would be very, very low,

02:35:01.050 --> 02:35:02.130
and if they think something is safe,

02:35:02.130 --> 02:35:04.560
the probability would be high.

02:35:04.560 --> 02:35:06.660
But that isn't necessarily the case.

02:35:06.660 --> 02:35:10.530
If you think about it,
cannabis is a unique instance

02:35:10.530 --> 02:35:12.960
in which nowadays we are hearing,

02:35:12.960 --> 02:35:15.390
yes, it's becoming legal
in a number of areas,

02:35:15.390 --> 02:35:17.640
and we talked earlier about why
that's probably a good thing

02:35:17.640 --> 02:35:19.320
in most circumstances,

02:35:19.320 --> 02:35:22.380
but that we aren't just
hearing that cannabis is safe,

02:35:22.380 --> 02:35:26.010
or it's not just being implied
that cannabis is safer,

02:35:26.010 --> 02:35:28.110
but many more people are talking

02:35:28.110 --> 02:35:30.491
about the positive effects of cannabis

02:35:30.491 --> 02:35:32.400
without a lot of discussion

02:35:32.400 --> 02:35:34.380
about the negative effects of cannabis.

02:35:34.380 --> 02:35:35.880
And I realize that saying this

02:35:35.880 --> 02:35:37.380
is going to upset some people out there,

02:35:37.380 --> 02:35:38.790
because I know that there
are a number of people

02:35:38.790 --> 02:35:41.130
who fought very hard for
the legalization process,

02:35:41.130 --> 02:35:42.930
and I want to acknowledge that.

02:35:42.930 --> 02:35:47.730
I also want to acknowledge the
many known positive effects

02:35:47.730 --> 02:35:52.560
of cannabis in adults
with very occasional use,

02:35:52.560 --> 02:35:57.120
provided it is delivered
safely and in the safe context

02:35:57.120 --> 02:35:59.580
and setting and with legality.

02:35:59.580 --> 02:36:02.040
That is entirely distinct from the issue

02:36:02.040 --> 02:36:03.510
of whether or not cannabis is safe

02:36:03.510 --> 02:36:05.760
for the developing brain and body.

02:36:05.760 --> 02:36:09.211
Again, I'm not demonizing
anybody for using cannabis,

02:36:09.211 --> 02:36:12.780
but I want to make the point
very simply and very directly.

02:36:12.780 --> 02:36:17.230
It is far and away a different
circumstance for the brain

02:36:18.270 --> 02:36:21.960
for an individual to be 25 years or older

02:36:21.960 --> 02:36:25.260
and using cannabis in whatever form,

02:36:25.260 --> 02:36:27.780
occasionally or maybe even frequently,

02:36:27.780 --> 02:36:32.780
than it is for a young
person, aged 14 to 25,

02:36:33.090 --> 02:36:36.231
to be using cannabis,
either by smoking or vaping

02:36:36.231 --> 02:36:41.231
or by edible or any other
form, on the brain and body.

02:36:41.280 --> 02:36:44.280
It's absolutely clear that
the brain continues to develop

02:36:44.280 --> 02:36:48.000
at least until age 25, and
that a huge number of systems

02:36:48.000 --> 02:36:51.330
related to mood regulation,
so-called executive function,

02:36:51.330 --> 02:36:53.070
the ability to organize one's thoughts,

02:36:53.070 --> 02:36:55.530
plan and execute plans,

02:36:55.530 --> 02:36:58.110
essentially to become a
functional human being,

02:36:58.110 --> 02:37:00.540
that's one portion of becoming
a functional human being,

02:37:00.540 --> 02:37:02.670
but certainly an essential one.

02:37:02.670 --> 02:37:05.040
All of that relies on the fine tuning

02:37:05.040 --> 02:37:06.416
of this neural circuitry

02:37:06.416 --> 02:37:09.720
that we've been talking
about up until now,

02:37:09.720 --> 02:37:13.590
and it's abundantly clear that cannabis

02:37:13.590 --> 02:37:18.590
and THC in particular, dramatically
disrupt those processes.

02:37:20.220 --> 02:37:23.970
So if this isn't clear enough,
just from my statements,

02:37:23.970 --> 02:37:26.040
I'd like to point to a particular paper.

02:37:26.040 --> 02:37:27.660
This is one of the more impactful papers

02:37:27.660 --> 02:37:29.670
in this area in recent years.

02:37:29.670 --> 02:37:33.720
This is a paper published in
"Lancet Psychiatry" in 2022.

02:37:33.720 --> 02:37:36.667
Title is "Association of Cannabis Potency

02:37:36.667 --> 02:37:40.390
"with Mental Ill Health and
Addiction, A Systematic Review."

02:37:40.390 --> 02:37:41.970
There are a number of
very important points

02:37:41.970 --> 02:37:43.717
in this very fine paper.

02:37:43.717 --> 02:37:46.260
"Lancet Psychiatry" is one of the premier

02:37:46.260 --> 02:37:47.673
medical journals out there,

02:37:48.540 --> 02:37:51.450
and they evaluated a
huge number of studies.

02:37:51.450 --> 02:37:54.450
They actually looked at
more than 4,000 studies.

02:37:54.450 --> 02:37:57.600
They selected the ones that
were only the most rigorous

02:37:57.600 --> 02:38:00.480
in terms of study design and analysis

02:38:00.480 --> 02:38:05.430
and rigor of conclusions, and
they looked at how early use

02:38:05.430 --> 02:38:09.960
of cannabis impacted later
probability of development

02:38:09.960 --> 02:38:12.960
of psychosis and other
psychiatric conditions,

02:38:12.960 --> 02:38:16.650
and the takeaways from
this study are very clear.

02:38:16.650 --> 02:38:19.860
First of all, chronic cannabis use,

02:38:19.860 --> 02:38:22.410
so more than twice per
week, has consistently

02:38:22.410 --> 02:38:24.390
been associated with
mental health disorders.

02:38:24.390 --> 02:38:26.970
I'm pulling some phrases
directly from the paper.

02:38:26.970 --> 02:38:30.420
Heavy cannabis use, meaning cannabis use

02:38:30.420 --> 02:38:32.836
more frequent than twice per week,

02:38:32.836 --> 02:38:35.760
has been associated
with four times the risk

02:38:35.760 --> 02:38:37.890
of psychosis later in life, in particular,

02:38:37.890 --> 02:38:40.740
schizophrenia and bipolar-like episodes.

02:38:40.740 --> 02:38:43.119
Now, we've done an episode
on bipolar disorder.

02:38:43.119 --> 02:38:44.700
It's also called bipolar depression.

02:38:44.700 --> 02:38:46.260
We have not yet done one on schizophrenia,

02:38:46.260 --> 02:38:48.840
but both bipolar disorder
and schizophrenia

02:38:48.840 --> 02:38:51.900
have a very, very strong
genetic component.

02:38:51.900 --> 02:38:55.380
There's a 30, three-zero,
30 times greater likelihood

02:38:55.380 --> 02:38:56.610
that you'll have bipolar disorder

02:38:56.610 --> 02:38:59.460
if you have a first relative
who has bipolar disorder.

02:38:59.460 --> 02:39:03.030
And then, it's also the
case that using cannabis,

02:39:03.030 --> 02:39:05.790
especially during adolescents
and the teen years,

02:39:05.790 --> 02:39:07.470
and up until age 25,

02:39:07.470 --> 02:39:10.469
create a four times
greater risk of psychosis

02:39:10.469 --> 02:39:12.600
for those that have a predisposition

02:39:12.600 --> 02:39:15.900
to bipolar disorder and/or schizophrenia.

02:39:15.900 --> 02:39:18.720
Now, I don't hear very much
about this in the media.

02:39:18.720 --> 02:39:22.830
This paper got some attention
and then it got swept away.

02:39:22.830 --> 02:39:24.480
I don't think that was an
intentional sweeping way.

02:39:24.480 --> 02:39:27.810
There's just a lot of events
in the world as you well know.

02:39:27.810 --> 02:39:31.200
But I think it's a particularly
important set of findings,

02:39:31.200 --> 02:39:34.980
because obviously, in
looking at so many studies,

02:39:34.980 --> 02:39:37.320
it distills out the strongest
findings that are out there

02:39:37.320 --> 02:39:40.620
and really pulls the consistent messages

02:39:40.620 --> 02:39:42.720
that are arriving from all
these different studies.

02:39:42.720 --> 02:39:45.671
And as they point out, and
again, I'm paraphrasing here,

02:39:45.671 --> 02:39:47.610
this is the first systematic review

02:39:47.610 --> 02:39:50.850
of the association of cannabis potency,

02:39:50.850 --> 02:39:53.340
and all of the data point
to a very clear conclusion,

02:39:53.340 --> 02:39:58.140
which is the more potent
the THC concentration,

02:39:58.140 --> 02:40:00.690
the higher probability
of developing psychosis

02:40:00.690 --> 02:40:02.220
or a major depressive episode

02:40:02.220 --> 02:40:04.650
or a major anxiety disorder later in life.

02:40:04.650 --> 02:40:08.850
That should be of particular
concern, because we know,

02:40:08.850 --> 02:40:11.310
we are absolutely clear about the fact

02:40:11.310 --> 02:40:14.760
that with the advent of all
these new strains of cannabis,

02:40:14.760 --> 02:40:19.560
and with the engineering
and availability of cannabis

02:40:19.560 --> 02:40:24.090
at much higher potency,
meaning THC potency,

02:40:24.090 --> 02:40:27.000
the risk of psychosis is going up and up

02:40:27.000 --> 02:40:29.610
and is likely to continue going
up unless something is done

02:40:29.610 --> 02:40:34.610
to reduce the frequency of
cannabis use to zero, ideally,

02:40:36.120 --> 02:40:39.210
or to very low frequency, very low potency

02:40:39.210 --> 02:40:43.650
in adolescence and teens and
people age 25 or younger.

02:40:43.650 --> 02:40:45.900
I know a lot of people don't
want to hear this message,

02:40:45.900 --> 02:40:47.220
because first of all, it's alarming,

02:40:47.220 --> 02:40:49.830
and second of all, as I mentioned earlier,

02:40:49.830 --> 02:40:53.190
the statistics tell us that
the greatest number of people

02:40:53.190 --> 02:40:54.540
that are starting to use cannabis

02:40:54.540 --> 02:40:57.273
are in the age bracket of 16 to 24.

02:40:58.230 --> 02:41:00.390
Many of them are functional
in other areas of life.

02:41:00.390 --> 02:41:03.780
They're students, they
are employed, et cetera.

02:41:03.780 --> 02:41:05.880
But when you couple that with the fact

02:41:05.880 --> 02:41:08.580
that the most frequent
adopters of cannabis use

02:41:08.580 --> 02:41:11.310
are in this age bracket of 16 to 24,

02:41:11.310 --> 02:41:14.670
they're twice as likely to
use as other individuals

02:41:14.670 --> 02:41:17.520
or to start using cannabis
as are other individuals.

02:41:17.520 --> 02:41:19.530
Plus, the general perception out there,

02:41:19.530 --> 02:41:21.900
because of the way that
cannabis is discussed

02:41:21.900 --> 02:41:24.810
in the media and by sports
figures and by celebrities

02:41:24.810 --> 02:41:27.270
and by politicians, et cetera,

02:41:27.270 --> 02:41:30.960
that it's not as bad as
alcohol and maybe not that bad

02:41:30.960 --> 02:41:33.630
and maybe even has health benefits,

02:41:33.630 --> 02:41:35.040
then you're essentially
setting up a system

02:41:35.040 --> 02:41:37.080
where young people are far more likely

02:41:37.080 --> 02:41:39.178
to adopt and continue cannabis use

02:41:39.178 --> 02:41:42.690
without realizing these
serious health consequences

02:41:42.690 --> 02:41:43.803
that await them later.

02:41:45.300 --> 02:41:47.790
With all of that said, I, of course,

02:41:47.790 --> 02:41:51.510
again want to acknowledge
that there have been

02:41:51.510 --> 02:41:56.510
well-demonstrated effects of
cannabis for reducing pain,

02:41:56.940 --> 02:42:00.180
in particular in chemotherapy
and in the context

02:42:00.180 --> 02:42:03.000
of reducing nausea in people suffering

02:42:03.000 --> 02:42:04.830
from cancer or chemotherapy.

02:42:04.830 --> 02:42:07.133
There is a well-known effect

02:42:07.133 --> 02:42:10.020
that one can generally
point to as positive,

02:42:10.020 --> 02:42:13.440
using cannabis for things like glaucoma,

02:42:13.440 --> 02:42:15.720
for lowering intraocular pressure

02:42:15.720 --> 02:42:17.220
and offsetting the loss of neurons

02:42:17.220 --> 02:42:18.270
that would lead to blindness,

02:42:18.270 --> 02:42:19.680
although there are other tools, of course,

02:42:19.680 --> 02:42:21.270
that don't involve cannabis use

02:42:21.270 --> 02:42:23.610
that can accomplish that as well,

02:42:23.610 --> 02:42:28.470
so called intraocular pressure
lowering drugs or drops.

02:42:28.470 --> 02:42:33.470
There is a list of probably
a dozen or more psychological

02:42:34.020 --> 02:42:37.640
and bodily ailments that can
be aided by cannabis use,

02:42:37.640 --> 02:42:41.253
in particular edible cannabis
use of particular strains.

02:42:42.150 --> 02:42:44.250
I, of course, am going to address each

02:42:44.250 --> 02:42:47.550
and every one of those in
episodes where I'm talking,

02:42:47.550 --> 02:42:51.090
for instance, about eye
disease or about chronic pain.

02:42:51.090 --> 02:42:52.860
I am in no way, shape or form

02:42:52.860 --> 02:42:56.580
trying to rob the incredible
efforts of the laboratories

02:42:56.580 --> 02:42:58.256
and people that have worked very hard

02:42:58.256 --> 02:43:02.730
to study and establish
the valid uses of cannabis

02:43:02.730 --> 02:43:04.350
for treating various ailments

02:43:04.350 --> 02:43:07.200
and that continue to study
cannabis in order to try

02:43:07.200 --> 02:43:10.493
and ameliorate the symptoms
of different ailments.

02:43:10.493 --> 02:43:11.910
But today, I really wanted

02:43:11.910 --> 02:43:15.000
to emphasize the biology of cannabis,

02:43:15.000 --> 02:43:18.240
some of the often discussed effects,

02:43:18.240 --> 02:43:19.710
I guess one could call
them positive effects,

02:43:19.710 --> 02:43:20.970
things like enhanced creativity

02:43:20.970 --> 02:43:23.677
and really point to the nuance
and actually the divergence

02:43:23.677 --> 02:43:27.480
of people who take cannabis
and some experience

02:43:27.480 --> 02:43:29.550
heightened levels of
creativity and some do not.

02:43:29.550 --> 02:43:33.540
Some people experience heightened
levels of sexual arousal

02:43:33.540 --> 02:43:36.420
and some people experience
the exact opposite,

02:43:36.420 --> 02:43:37.890
and so on and so forth,

02:43:37.890 --> 02:43:40.680
rather than focus on all
the potential positive

02:43:40.680 --> 02:43:44.100
and emerging positive data about cannabis

02:43:44.100 --> 02:43:45.780
in different medical contexts.

02:43:45.780 --> 02:43:48.510
And at the same time, I strongly
feel that it's important

02:43:48.510 --> 02:43:51.436
to acknowledge the shocking,

02:43:51.436 --> 02:43:54.330
because there's really no
other way to describe it,

02:43:54.330 --> 02:43:58.860
the shocking effects of cannabis
use on the developing fetus

02:43:58.860 --> 02:44:01.080
and the fact that so many pregnant

02:44:01.080 --> 02:44:03.240
and lactating mothers are using cannabis.

02:44:03.240 --> 02:44:07.530
I mean, that number 15% still
has me dizzy with disbelief,

02:44:07.530 --> 02:44:08.850
and yet we need to acknowledge this

02:44:08.850 --> 02:44:11.370
and address this immediately.

02:44:11.370 --> 02:44:15.330
And I think it's vital to
understand that cannabis use

02:44:15.330 --> 02:44:19.020
through any delivery mechanism,

02:44:19.020 --> 02:44:22.140
smoking or vaping or edible or otherwise,

02:44:22.140 --> 02:44:24.720
is very, very concerning,

02:44:24.720 --> 02:44:26.880
in fact, dangerous to
the developing brain,

02:44:26.880 --> 02:44:30.196
certainly for the fetal
brain and for the baby brain,

02:44:30.196 --> 02:44:32.640
but also for the adolescent brain

02:44:32.640 --> 02:44:35.610
and for the teen and young adult brain,

02:44:35.610 --> 02:44:36.840
not just because of the effects

02:44:36.840 --> 02:44:38.310
that it can have in the immediate term,

02:44:38.310 --> 02:44:41.460
those slow creeping increases
in anxiety and depression,

02:44:41.460 --> 02:44:43.770
brought on by cannabis use,

02:44:43.770 --> 02:44:47.586
but also the time release, if you will,

02:44:47.586 --> 02:44:51.120
on the development of
psychosis and other types

02:44:51.120 --> 02:44:54.270
of major psychiatric
disorders later in life.

02:44:54.270 --> 02:44:56.460
I acknowledge we've covered
